        <p>more severe than is the law at afarielbad. By ths time theae fashionable people have made themselves a, long and skimpy as possible, the Direatoire idea wit' The Ex-Empress Eugene.—Lord Rosebery's descrip- probably have passed by. It will then be interesting 1 to see how the elongated ones will manage to more tion of Mrs. Gladstone as" that solitary and pathetic I 'comfortable looking dimensions. figure "is equally applicable to the ex-Empress Eugenie 1 New Petticoats.—The best, petticoats prepared for who was visited by the King and Queen of Spain at her outdoor autumn wear, are of soft non-rustling satin, home at Farnborough, where she has resided since 1881. , With the exception of the Emperor Joseph, there is no fitting likes glove about the waist and hips, and only 1 , living Royal personage who has been so sorely wounded moderately full at the hem. But one really excellent I poiut about these new petticoats is, their delightful in his or her affectionibas the Empress Eugenie, none who hes been overtake?' by so many heavy troubles. shortness. They are cut quite two inches shorter than " War robbed her of her Empire, of her only child, and the skirt that just covers the ankles, so that we shall indirectly, of her husband. mail wonder that she have no snore dragging, and perchance damp and muddy :e shonld say " I hate the horrors of war, and I abhor its frills about our feet, for at least this season. awful memories." It was thirty-eight years ago last Mole Colour.—The fashioriable woman will pass over Sunday that she arrived at Deauville in the cart of a white this coming winter, and instead pin her faith to Normandy peasant, and took refuge on the yacht of Sias mole colour, which by the way is certainly much more been John Fox Burgoyne, and from that time she has serviceable, and incidentally more generally becoming the guest of Britain. The a isit of the King and Queen than white or even cream. Further it is a delightful of Spain to her at Farnborou