 in London, the bride Marshall Field and Mr. highways' This allays the dust, but not entirely so and Drummond in a re;istrars () the tarred dust that arises irritates the throat and is la being the widow of the only son of the late Mr. destructive of plants and foliage around. If that is Marshall Field, the Chicago nuilti-millionaire. She is the case it is a serious drawback to the only means so not much past thirty and her youth and vast wealth far devised, for keeping down the motor dust nuisance. made her the object of much speculation in society as i A Novel not to he Missed.—Mrs. Robert Hichens, to whether she would re-marry. Her two boys of author of " The Garden of Allah," whose new novel " A Spirit in Prison " (Hutchinson) is just about to fourteen and twelve will inherit most of the fabulous wealth of their grandfather, and she has a little girl of appear, has lately returned from Italy where for several years past he has spent much of his time. This famous of Drummond, so that altogether Mr. an sic Mr. Drummond belongs to the rich bankingd Mrs. family , novelist is very familiar too with Egypt, where he , wrote his celebrated satire "The Green Carnation," Drummond are nut likely to want for much that money I a can buy. The great society wedding of the season, is and with Sicily which inspired him with" The Call of i of course, that of Mr. \Winston Churchill and Miss the Blood." Herder, on Saturday, at St. Margaret's Church, West- minster. In addition to the members! and friends of the two families, this event has drawn to London nearly all the members of the Government., and many prominent people in Parliamentary circles. The Poulett Family.—Earl Poulett will be remem- bered in connection with the barricading of Hinton St. George, the family seat at Crowkerne, on the death of the late Earl in 1999. These steps were taken to exclude "Viscount Itinton," who for many years went about the country with a street organ, and claimed to be Earl l'oulett's heir. The House of Lords eventually decided in favour of the present Earl, who is a son of the late Earl's third marriage, and was horn in 1983. He is not the first of his family to contract marriage with an actress. The third Duke of Bolton—who came of the same stock—made a duchess iu 1751 of Lavinia Fenton, a noted actress and singer who many years before bad taken his fancy. An earlier dachess however stood in the way of their marriage, until the Duke became a widower in 1751. Sir Amios Poolett was the cnstodian of Mary Queen of Scots at Fotheringay. Mary was urged by her friends to bestow her blandish- ments upon Sir Amias, in the hope of his conniving to her escape, brit he was not susceptible to her charms, as others had been, and did nothing to save the unfor- tunate Queen from her fate. Cook, Sweep, Wash, Sew.—Mr. Lloyd George's eulogy of the German housewife as one who stays at home, and does his duty is no doubt well-deserved, but one is always a little suspicious of the man who enthrones woman on a wash-tub with a darning needle in her hand. Gerinan wives may be better than British or American wives, but husbands are much the same all the world over, and there are too many who sum up woman's whole duty in life in four words, cook, sweep, wash and sew. The German " bans frau" accepts this r3le, but Western women are not so content with the mediaeval notion that home-making is the same thing as houaekeeping. Nor is the home played out because women no longer do their own pickling and jam making. It saves time and money to buy such things of the store- keepers, and although men are apt to accuse women who try to minimise their housework, of being slack and careless, it is those homes in which the wife finds time to take an interest in her husbands ambitions and pursuits, and to share in the family pleasures, that are the most successful. Elongated Beauties.—The quest for the Directoire figure continues, and the first essential being height, the chief of the exercises undertaken by up-to-date women, consist in hanging on to a pole, lying on the ground deliberately stretching all the muscles, arm- pulling, and wearing a contrivance that is supposed to elongate the neck. With regard to the diet this is ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>DRESS-FASHION-HOME.</head>
        <p>DRESS-FASHION-HOME.</p>
        <p>THE DISPLAY OF FASHIONS.</p>
        <p>The woman of to-day has every' opportunity d seeing the clothes she intend S to wear, and the effect they are likely to produce, for in Paris, in London, and in New York, at the leading dress- makers', women with pretty figures are arrayed in evening and day dresses with all the necessary jewels and accompaniments, and parade up and down, sometimes in the open air, more often in beautiful rooms. In old days, says the Queen, the dressmakers were content to slip on the dresses civet a flesh-tinted bodice. Now, the ex- hibition to which the leaders of the fashionable world are invited are of quite a different order, and constitute almost a/fete; indeed, they are bidden to an At Home, where dress, instead of music, is the attraction. A PRETTY AND SIMPLE NIGHTGOWN.</p>
        <p>The very pretty and simple little nightgown pictured in our sketch is an excellent model for realisation in some woollen material for late autumn and winter wear. This design would look very well carried out in flannel, flannel- ette, viyella, cashmere. Scotch wincey, delaine, or nun's-veiling, the simplicity of cut making it an ideal design for woollen fabrics. The night- gown is cut square at the neck, and is edg,pd by</p>
        <p>a wide band of insertion mitred, at the corners. Strong Cluny on Torchon lace is the best for the purpose, being firm and solid and not shrinking. The nightgown faktene down the front, and on either side are three tucks, which are set into the lace bana at the neck. Over each shoulder come four tucks, which give ample fulness to the sidea of the gown. The sleeves are of three. quarter length, and are cut in one with the nightgown. A band of insertion finishes them at the bottom.</p>
        <p>HA, HA!</p>
        <p>Englishwomen have recently been reminded that they do not understand the art of laughter, and certainly the majority of us have little ambi- tion above the involuntary cachininatory cackle of which we are held guilty. The "musical laugh," the "silvery peal," and all other fa.Fci- nating varieties of the feminine laugh, which must once have charmed the ear. are rarely recogni&amp;ed now. If the proposed "school of laughter" did not succeed in reviving what was formerly an effortless grace, we should at least learn, declares a ladies' paper, how to laugh at the critics of the "solemn English" and • Lon- don without a smile."</p>
        <p>*e.i.... •-•, , \ ‘ • \ /I .\\' '\''. :\''' /• I 0 - • '.:1,...1,f1iiii :: ) ' ////i ff • ; ~ I, / / ,L 171 ' i 'II r</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>HOPEFUL SIGN.</head>
        <p>A HOPEFUL SIGN.</p>
        <p>- It is early to speak of coming crazes, says the Lady's Pictorial, but, coming events, as we are proverbially reminded, cast their shadows before, and one such shadow seems to indicate that as soon as the dancing season sets in there will be &amp; revival of the old masked ball. It will be well if it is so. for this way ries much merry-making and something of the old spirit of romance and adventure. The tendency of the day is to be desperately matter-of-fact, and anything which counteracts this is to be warmly welcomed. There are all sorts of possibilities about the masked ball; for one thing, it breaks down a soupcon of the reserve to which Britons always will be slaves; then it tinges life with some little romance which is eminently desirable; and, furthermore, it possesses the charm of unconven- tionality which, alas! characterises so. few of our means of amusement. We English find it ex- tremely difficult to let ourselves go. The masked ball is just the kind of thing to give us confi- dence in the art of "letting ourseNes go "—an art possessed by our remote forebears if not by our snore immediate predecessors.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>USEITL LITTLE FROCK.</head>
        <p>A USEITL LITTLE FROCK.</p>
        <p>Pictured in our illustration is neat and use- ful little-frock, just the thing for everyday wear during the coming autumn and winter. The original was carried out in golden-brown serge of rather rough weave, but the same idea would look equally well realised in cloth, ca!hmere, or velveteen. Down the front of the dress come .s sort of wide plastron, which is machine-stitched</p>
        <p>on either edge, and further decorated by a rcw of buttons. This pleat is flanked on either side by two wide. machine-stitched tucks, the outer of which projects slightly over the top of the sleeve. The skirt has the wide piece with two tucks in front to 'match the bodice, and the rest of the skirt is gathered round the waist. The sleeves are plain bishop affairs, adorned with buttons at the cuffs to match the front. A dainty little turn down collar of white hand. embroidered linen, completed by a brown silk bow in front, puts the finishing - touch to this chit...mine _dad ap, 11AT MATERIALS AND COLOURS.</p>
        <p>The large hats for autumn and winter svear will be mainly in felt and beaver, says Clare North in M. A. P., but velvet will be more em- ployed than has been the case for a decade, and there is a decided feeling for soft and rather lustreless satin. Black, grey in half a hundred subtle shades, and various tones of green will doubtless rank foremost in general esteem, but many smart women will bestow their patronage on the khaki tint that has been so much seen at Continental "beds" during the past month or so. Many. of these khaki-coloured hats are trimmed with white wings or feathers, but they Jook even better in allisnce with black or brown. Wings will almost inevitably supplant to a large extent feathers, and hardly any hat recently noted has been adorned with ospreys. The humanitarian heart may therefore rejoice. TO-DAY'S RECIPE.</p>
        <p>PARTRIDGES A LA WESTFALIE.—Prepare a brace of partridges for roasting and lard the breasts. Sprinkle liberally with salt, lay a slice of fat bacon over the top, and then cover with a couple of vine-leaves, binding the whole with string. Put a little butter in a stew- pan, and when hot add the partridges, place the lid on the pan, and fry—not too quickly—until well browned. Then add by degrees a little water, and after half an hour a few spoonfuls of sour cream—one spoonful at a time—stirring it well into the stock, and basting the birds with the mixture. Then add a few spoonfuls of lean; finely-chopped ham, and at the la.st a little more butter -previously melted. When cooked ur.tie the string and serve with the bacon and yine ;eaves, the sauce, and ;tied potato</p>
