      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>BACK TO WORK AT 63.</head>
        <p>BACK TO WORK AT 63.</p>
        <p>Strength Renewed by Chas. Forde's Bile Beans.</p>
        <p>Bilious Headache and Sickness Cured.</p>
        <p>" When I was 60 years old," says Mr. Joseph llatton, a lamplighter, of 20, Greenfield-street, Liscard, Cliesbbe, " I began to suffer from pains in the head, sickness, and loss of appetite. Often I suffered so acutely I was almost blinded. The veins stood out like cords on my forehead. I pit up with these attacks patiently for some time, in the hope that they would pass away • but as they got worse, I placed myself in the bands of a doctor. The little improve- ment he brought about soon passed, leaving me worse than ever.</p>
        <p>"For more than two years I suffered frarn the lytd bilious bouts with their accompanying headaches, and all the medicines I took failed to give me relief. I tried Chas. Forde's Bile Beans.</p>
        <p>"The first few doses brought me an improvement, and encouraged me to continue with the medicine. Chas. Forde's Bile Beans eased the terrible pains in my head and stopped the sickness. Perseverance with Chas. Forde's Bile Beans finally resulted in a complete cure of the billiousness and headache. Now I am enjoying the full vigour of health, and do my work in a way many men 25 years my junior would envy." _ _</p>
        <p>Cha, Forde's Bile Beans are the most valuable medicine the worker, old and young, can keep at hand. Of all chemists, is. Id. and 2s. Od in sealed boxes marked " Charles Folda's."</p>
        <p>EXPRESS ENGINE'S BREAKDOWN. On Tuesday the 1.35 p.m. express from Wigan to Liverpool was proceeding down the bank from Prescot to Huyton Junction, when something went wrong with the mechanism of the engine,some of the parts breaking. The driver brought the train to a stand as speedily as possible. Another engine from St. Helens was procured and after an hour's delay the train proceeded to Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <p>thought you'd see I was only saying it to tease you. Only I wondered how much longer you were going to keep this up with your old friend.' "How could I see you were only teasing me when I didn't know you knew I was myself?" she asked, with a rueful smile. "You must give see time to think that out," he said, smiling. "I'll tell you later." "But how did you know me, Jack?" she asked. "You must tell me that, because I think it was very clever of you." "The ring on your little finger happened to ?etch my eye," he said; "the rest I managed to make out for myself." "How -could I be so stupid!" she said, hastily pulling off a little blue enamel snake ring from her finger. "And, oh dear! I do declare there's no pocket to this dress of Aunt Martha's for me to put it in!" , "May I take care of it for you? " he asked. "Will you be very careful of it?" "I'll give it back to you, if that's what you moan." She laughed and slipped it into his hand, and he put it in his pocket. Her eyes were sparkling, and the white hair became her wonderfully well. "I think I got one in about the minuet," he said, smiling at her. "You were simply horrid all along," she ansivered; and then, remembering what ho had said about her being the prettiest girl in the room, she blushed a little. , "So you object on principle to Lancers and waltzes?" he asked. • "I was a very nice old lady," she said, "end you ought to have been only too pleased to listen to my words of wisdom." • "It is splendid, the make-up," he said; 'I can't think how you did it. Nobody else Incei you the least. It was a good thing I was here to look after you." "Why" she asked, with innocent question- ing eyes. "Because I wouldn't have missed it for any- thing." "I shall take you in to supper," ho said, abruptly, after a. short pause. "You are so sure?" she questioned softly. "And look here, there's a room for sitting out downstairs which has every bit as good a floor as this, and when the next,alance strikes up you and I will go down and it all to ourselves." "What resourcefulness you have!" she mur. inured, admiringly. "That is unless the old lady will give the young ones a lesson," he continued. "The old lady, as you irreverently call her, is more mindful of her dignity than you think, sir," she answered, "and is also not a little anxious about her Aunt Martha's gown." "There's the music beginning," ho said; "come on, Nell, you will come, won't you?" "Say, 'I shall be much honoured, Mrs. Browne, if you will give me the pleaiure of you! company downstairs.'" "The honour and pleasure will be mine, Mrs. Browne, if you will take me downstairs, Mrs. Browne," he said, bowing. I "Oh, you are incorrigible!" sho said, smiling, I as she rose and went down 'the room with him. I The floor was as good as Jack had described I it to be, and soon the apparently ill-assortee couple were spinning down the room to the in- • spiration of the Blue Danube. I"Oh, I'm glad I came," said Nell, breath- lessly. "I didn't know I was going to have any, of the fun myself. But only fancy my going to I a grown-up subscription dance before I'm 'out!' • Isn't it shocking?" I "It shews you to be a most forward young • lady, I consider," said Jack. "How will you • like coming out and seeing a bit of. tho world, Nell? Will it be 'off with tho old friends and on with the nee, '? " "Are you one of the old friends?" she asked. "Ono of the very oldest," he said. "Then I think you must be allowed a readmis- sion ticket," she said, softly. "Aren't you going to thank me?" she went on, presently, as he did not answer. "'the music has stopped," he said, as if re- lieved. "Come and sit down over here; I want to speak to you." They 'crossed the room together and sat down on a sofa. "You must have noticed, well," he began, in a vibrating voice. "It cannot be anything new to you when I tell you--" If you're going to be serious, Jack, I shall go straight upstairs again," Nell interrupted him. And then, before they either of them had time to say any more, a sudden 'sound from the other side of the room, where it was almost dark, made them • both start and look round. A heavy curtain shut off a space at the extreme end of the room, and as they looked it was put aside and soiteone in a light dress emerged and began walking to- wards them. As tho figure came nearer Nell re- cognised with a pang at her heart that it was a 'very sad, downcast-looking Margaret, moving with slow steps and utter obliviousness of their presence towards the door. "Will you go back to the ballroom, Jack?" she said, in a low voice. "I want to speak to her alone." "I shall see you later," he said, rising un- willingly, and doing as she requested. "What has happened, Margaret?" said Nell, who was at her side in a moment. "Do tell me if anything has gone wrong to make you look so sad." "Why, you little monkey, where on earth did you spring from?" said Margaret, trying to be cheerful. "And, pray, with whom have you been sitting out down here?" "OnTY with Jack," said Nell. carelessly. "But do tell me what's wrong, darling; you know I should understand." . _ _ _</p>
        <p>"I should be too proud to tell anyone else, Nell," said Margaret, looking away. 'I think I'm too proud to tell you what a fool I've been. Oh, fool! fool! to think it was I he cared for! Listen, Nell. Ralph has been telling me about tho girl he is in lovo with; telling me how per. fectly beautiful and noble and pure and above all other women she is, while I—l am only his confidante!"</p>
        <p>"But, Margaret, he meant you. Ile meant</p>
        <p>you," oiled her sister. I "How could he?" said Margaret. "Why, he said"—and she choked down a. sob—" he said she had blue eyes and red-gold hair done up high on the top of her had, and you know my eyes are grey, and my hair is done low, and hasn't an atom of red in it." % ' Poor Margaret. Her situation might be pain- ful, but it had its humorous side! But Nell was I much too loyal to think of /anything but her ' sister's grief. The description did not seem to do i for Margaret, turn it which way she would, al- though she could ot understand any man failing . to be in love with frr sister when he had seen so ' much of her as R ph had. I "I couldn't go straight on to the next dance," I Margaret went on. "I wanted to be alone and I tealise once for all that I was nothing to him. Oh! I do hope I didn't give myself away to him. I know I was rather funny and off-band after I.that." "I'm sure you didn't," said Nell. soothingly. ; "I:terles, I'm perfectly certain he's in love with you all the time. Now you must listen to my story. Jack has found out who I am!" 1 "He has?" said Margaret, rather aghast. "Well, I couldn't think why he wanted to be in- trodueed to you unless he had." "Really, Margaret, your rudeness!" said Nell. I "But .it's perfectly safe with him, and—l don • think I'm very sorry now that he does knew,'. _ .. ... . . . .. .</p>
        <p>she said, smilinginio her sister's eyes. I "Oh! Nell, Nell. I see I shall have trouble with you." said Margaret, smilinT in spite of her- self. "And now we must go back to the ball- room at once, and I must try to pacify the part- I ner whose dance I have been cutting.' ; They entered the room together, and Nell noticed with amazement that Jack had been caught and introdueed to a bevy of girls and I was inscribing his name on their programmes with quick ierks of temper. He ,turned round 1 as if by instinct when the two girls came in, and catching Nell's smile of comprehension as she took in the situation, returned with even • fiercer energy to his obnoxious task. Margaret I Was soon claimed for the next dance, and Nell went off to the end of the room from which dig- . . . . .</p>
        <p>nified age looked on indulgently at the present generation taking its turn of dancing, flirting, and love-making. She sat turning over in her Mind what Mar- garet had told her until suddenly an old lady next to her looked un from her knitting and said, "How do you do, Miss Fairfax?" Nell literally jumped. ,!' I think there must be some .mistak(2," she</p>
        <p>said, recovering herself, and speaking in her as- sumed voice. "Oh, I beg your pardon," said the old lady, apologetically, "I saw you talking to Miss Mar. garet Fairfax. and. seeing you were in a black velvet dress very similar to one I've seen her I Aunt Martha in, if you'll excuse my saying so, I took you to be her." • "It was very natural that you should," said Nell, greatly relieved. "Miss Martha could not be hero to-night, as she is staying away with some friends.`' . . -</p>
        <p>"Well, I'm glad o hear it," said the old lady, emphatically. "I'm sure if anyone deserves a holiday she does." And then. confidentially, "Between ourselves I never think that poor thing's position- is a very enviablo one. What comfort or company can that brother of hers be to anyone, with Ins 'unsociable, fossilising ways! And then she ha l all the looking after of those two hits of mischief, his daughters." "What a life she must have of it," said Nell, with feeling, and sunoressing •rnuch inward taugnter. Anti are the daughters, indeed, sun a trial to her?</p>
        <p>"You may take my gorcl for it that they're the thorn in her 'side," said the old lady, nod- ding her head impressively, "although shot does say nothing about it.'= _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <p>Jack came and sat down by Nell just tnea, and she turned to him with dancing eyes. "You must give us your opinion of the Misses Fairfax now, Mr. Bradfield." she said. "W° have been designating them bits of mischief and ill aorta of things." "I always hope their foolishness is only on account of their extreme youth," said Jack, with calm superiority. Very well, sir, you can go," said Nell, in low aside. "The singing and practising that goes on in. that house must be enough to deafen anyone," said the waspish old lady, smoothing out her -knitting. "I couldn't stand their screeching and thumping for a day/if I were she." "It must be frightful," Jack agreed, while' Mrs. Browne turned off something very like o giggle into a cough. They're much admired, I hear," pursued the) old lady, "and I don't say they've not got good looks. But what's the good of that if it only makes them conceited? For my part I'd rather see a plain girl and unassuming than pretty and puffed up with pride." 'There, „Nell, you see," said Jack, in au - undertone; and then aloud, "Won't you come and have some refreshments, Mrs. Browne?" "Thank you, I should like some," said Nell, rising and bowing her adieu to the severe old lady., "Oh, Jack," she said as they went down the- room. "wasn't it a miracle 'I didn't explode with laughter? Isn't she just a terror?" "You did seem to be having rather a rough time of it when I arrived on the scene," he said. laughing, " I almost expected to see you go for the old girl before everyone. But; my word, what a lot of good it ought to do you." "What pretty girls those were you were talk- ing to when I came in!" she said a little later, as she sipped, her iced coffee in the refreshment- room. Jack knit his brows. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>"Some interfering fool went and introduced me to thorn," he said. "I suppose you were glad to see me sticking my name down for a lot of dances so that I shouldn't be able to come near you all the evening." "How annoyed you did look!" she said. laughing at the remembrance. "Suppose you were a man, and the one girl you cared for--L" "Do you mean the one in pink, whose fan you were holding?" Nell interrupted, eagerly. "Do tell me about her!" _</p>
        <p>"And the one girl you cared for was in the same room with you all the evening without your being. able to speak to her," Jack went on, without deigning to notice her interruption. "I should say to myself: How this does shove the contrariness of my nature Just because there are fifty girls with whom I can dance if I• choose and one with whom I can't, I go and make up my mind forthwith that she is the only one I care to dance with and proceed to get huffy with her as if it were all her own doing.' ". You know it would be just the same if the fifty girls were, here or at Jericho," he said, in- dignantly. "In the lattet case we might hear that you. bad gone On a pilgrimage to the Holy Land," ane said, • demurely. "How can you be so silly," he said, laughing in spite of himself, and then, as the music had begun again, he returned without anything being said.</p>
        <p>Nell was rather surprised when, a few minutes later, Ralph Wheatley cathe over to her and be- gan talking to her as she sat watching the dan- cers. She answered him in her most old lady- like manner, and • felt distinctly on tenterhooks. Do you dee my friend Miss Fairfax any- where?" she asked, primly, after a few ordinary, roma rks. had passed between them. . . .</p>
        <p>Don't yoKii see her standing under the chandelier over there by. the piano?" said Ralph, readily. "How the light brings, out the red gleams in her golden hair." A thrill of joy ran through Nell's whole being, but she answered, in her thin voice, Yes, sha has "pretty hair, but I always think her grey eyes are her best. point." "I should never have called them anything, but blue," ssid Ralph, smiling, "but I quite agree with you." This was the right topic as far as he was con- cerned, for ho had come to talk to Mrs. Browne with the vague hope that she might say some- thing which would throw light on Margaret's strange behaviour that evening. He was feeling sore and perplexed at the cold distance of heg manner when he had begun to make his con- fession of love to her, for until then he had read such encouragement in her eyes as he would never 'have dared to hope far otherwise. How- ever, his hopes of Mrs. Browne were doomed to disappointment, her answers were not enlighten- ing, and she appeared to be paying very little attention to tho conversation.</p>
        <p>So, to her great delight, it was not long befors he got up and left her, and she was able to ge and catch Margaret, in a moment of temporary partnerlessness, by the door. She drew her into a rams without a word of explanation, and be- gan, in a voice of triumph: **Margaret, I was perfectly right and you were wrong. Ralph's Leah talking to me about i your blue eyes and the red gleams n your golden hair, till I almost had to embrace him on the apot. You will forgive him for thinking there 'S red in your hair, won't you, dear: because he's an artist, and can't help it, and he admires it awfully." "Oh! Nell, I can't believe it," said Margaret. dropping into a chair, and holding her sister's hand.</p>
        <p>"And, of course, he's only a man, and he calls it done on top when you puff your hair like a Gibson Girl," said Nell, sententiously. "And so he meant me after said Mar- garet, softly, the forced cheerfulness of the even- ing giving way to real smiles and dimples. "Why, what a little witch you are, Nell, the way you got me out of all my troubles. I shalt have to keep you somewhere very handy 12 future in case of need." "I've not done a bad evening's work," said Nell, smiling.- "Who's your nett partner? Oh! Ralph himself, I see. Well, get along 'Carrots.' and don't waste your time here talking to me!" Margaret walked slowly back into the room, and at once encountered Ralph, who was looking for her.</p>
        <p>"Shall we it this out?" he asked, delighted to see that sho was her usual gracious self again. "It's very hot, and the barn dance is rather vigorous."</p>
        <p>"It would be cooler," assented Margaret. and Nell smiled to herself in the privacy of the recess.</p>
        <p>"Oh! if you knew how happy litin. Nell," said Margaret, as they brushed their hair to- gether that night. "There never could be any- body like Ralph in all the world. I do hope it won't be long beforo such happiness comes. to you, too. Do you know, I think Jack would just do for youl" "Do you?" said Nell. smiling, her brush poised in mid-air. "I think he is rather nice."</p>
        <p>When Jack arrived to call on the Fairfaxel next day, he was informed that Nell was the onll person at home, and was straightway ushered into her presence. "I have brought back your ring," he said, sit. ting down in a chair beside her. She held out her hand for it, but he slipped the ring on her little finger himself instead. Even when that was done he did not relinquish the hand. "I'm going away to-morrow for six months." he said. She turned her head away from him, and clid not speak. When at last she looked round again her eyes were full of tears. "May I be serious now, Nell?" he asked. "Yes," she whispered softly. And as the six months' travel had been inciitu• kd purely on her account it never took planet an Exp.]</p>
      </div>
