        <p>WARRINGTON MUSICAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>was going to lose these:vices of ND.. Newcombe as hon. registrar and auditor. He did not think any of them had worked so hard for the Society as :%fr. Newcombe, and thay deeply sympathised with him in the calso of his retirement.</p>
        <p>Mr. GOUGH. Ind resigned the secretaryship at the last meeting of tin Commitee, and on his proposition Mr. Atkin, who had previously filled the office, was elected. SUGGESTED *FREE 11ENIBERS.</p>
        <p>The following spesial resolution appeorel on the agenda • "That the committee be empowered to admit a limited number to membership without poyment of fees (the number to bo filed by the committee) aryl that the mode of selection be by exomination befc,re sub-committea who shall draw up rules for the competition." _</p>
        <p>Vie SF:CHET-MY formally moved the resolution, which had been sent in by Mr. Newcombe. Mr. Li R opposed the revolution as being unfair to the members of the Society in general. He did not think it would be right to allow anyone to enter the Society free of charge. If they loved music they would sacrifice something to join. Mr. ATKIN said he did not think the matter had bean anything like fully thought out an -I Mr. Newoomhe would probably see crest ressou for not curving such a resolution. Ile ('lr. Atkin) had no 'hesitation in saying that it would be a great mistake even to put it to that meeting. There was no seconder to the resolution which con- sequently fell to the ground. The CHAIRMAN then moved that the committee be empowered to select honorary memher3 of the society. His object was that the honour should be bestowed upon those who had served the Society faithfully and who had done well in ths musical world. He slytested the names of Mrs. Booth and Miss Anderton, and said he thought the Society wonld be •hononring itself as well as the two ladies by electing them hon. members. Arr, Gouort seconded.</p>
        <p>Mr'. W. H. PAYTON, in supporting, said he thought the honour might exteni to those who had been hard workers for the Society and who were now unable to attend, owing to having left the town or some other cause. He dia not think each election should he con- lined to those who had aohieved =slat honours after leaving the Society. The resolution was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>Annual Meeting.</head>
        <p>Annual Meeting.</p>
        <p>4010 annual general meeting of the Warrington nsical Society was held in the Cairo-street schools on f ondav evening. Mr. F. H. Crossley presided over a • - !sir attendance which included Mr. C. P. Gough (hon. ecretary) and Mr. W. E. Atkin (hon. treasurer). ;setters of apology for non-attendance were received trona the President (Mr. J. Charlton Parr), Mr. R. T. Arairclough, and Mr. R. H. Si!cock. THE ACCOUNTS. is,l:,4r• W. E. AVM; presented the accounts which a 3 that members' subscriptions tOtalled £55 ss. concert subscriptions £75 es. The sale of tickets t 1,14 Programmes realized £162 us. 21. The expe 4 included £212 Sc. for professional aid at concerts; £7l Od. for hire of hall and practice rooms,musio BG3-; printing, postages and advertising. The loss gen• 'eure concerts for the season was £l4 2!. 7d. On the • 8 fti account there was a balance in the bank of 3• Appended to the balance sheet was an analysis • IL VINCI° Profit and loss on each concert which showed vra, " the first concert ("Dream of Gerontius , ') there 11,,,a loss of £36 11s. 6d., on the second ("The ("1,424h") a profit of £8 Is. H., and on the third att "ti3t ") a loss of £l6 12s. 2d. Mr. Atkin milted ti on to the large decrease in the concert snbscrip, ,4118,!i?t during the past four seasons. In 1904-5 .the £lo,'„riPtions amounted to £122 2s. 63.; 1905.6, tho: ; 1900-7, £BO 10s. 64. ; 1907-8, £75 9s. Prior to lilt' 'Sr seasons he remembered that the subscription alton3 slightly over £l5O, but since that time they had for "'Kt the rate of subscription, and that might account Wll :°nleth in g. A subscription list for that Society for Very important thing indeed. 7t was a guarantee aso .143 work of the whole season and it was a pity to 18,0 It fall. On the other hand it was most satis- siker9 to see that the receipts from the sale of ti▪ ckets, lalßeratames at the concerts had gradually£s gone up. 7.. ISoc „I-5 they amounted to 68125. Id.; 10 a-6, 7 I Is.po •‘..69S Os. 24. ; 1907-8, £162 lls. J. He particu- theotrished to.thank Mr. Crossley and the members of tlek,',9eietv for the excellent way in which the sale of sotila had been taken up during the paTt, season. The conBil,t3i would have been in a very big bole indeed, i for .19ering the concerts they gave, if t had not been qurAo amount realized by the sale of tickets. Mr. reported with great regret the loss of a valuable covParter of the Society by the death of Miss Pierpoint. sona,iling he said that this time he had not received ths ,alance from the opera committee for inclusi▪ on in b,Theounts. That was not an oversight. There was cora "co but it was thought advisable by the opera "petnti,lettee to keep it in hand towards the preliminary the opera they contemplated giving this leas ia“' Ile thought they had acted rightly in doing so 11) e hoped the advantage would accrue to the Society War Boil- Mr. Atkin alluded to the departure from to trblagton of Mr. F. J. Lord, a handsome contrilmtor snbscrirition list, and Mrs. Lord, an enthusiastic °et' of the chorus. Mr. Monks, who had taken he h,elt interest in the Society had also left town but tc%i?generously continued his subscription up to now. CRARtmaa.7, in moving the adoption of the ,their'nts, said they were still on the losing side with C3nCertS and the season's results appeared to qO4 the fact that with the present prices of aft's- rot Reale"thov was impossible to give performances on the it „ were doing and make them pay. He oomph- Tr. Atkin on the exceptionally lucid statement Atr. nrosented. W., • IL PAYTON seconded, ow„' u• LEE asked if there was no way of cutting large- eaPenses. He considered that £7l was is very Aft 11'11 to nag for printing, postages and advertising. relic) • ziTisrx said that he had never SKR, such a large fOr printing, postages and advertising before. coopiGaght the matter up in committee and was clearly resait".,eti that the expenditure under those heads had a largo increase in the sale of tickets. tO Si \-41 tliftAt iN said the Committee thought it better 4.the e„"n,,tl, Mere rather than less in this department and justifiel the means. They were indebted I'oll4 r„ress for the preliminary notices of the concerts ,Iloy had kindly given. e accounts were then passel. IowinELEC LION OF OFFICERS. J. tVitPlarlton ofil-'ers were elected :—President, Mr. Farr ; Vice-Presidents. the Mayor of 41134 ten, Messrs. Z. Armitage, A. H. Crosfleld, ALP., 'laß. T. Fairclough, F. Monks, R. Pierpoint, ptialrke.r, John White, Captnin Crosfield.lT. Roberts, t. I°llgb F. A. Frost, J. Monro, W. N. Hutchings, on kin,'and James Dodds • committee Messrs. H. d' 'Knowles, Bibby, G. Johnson, J. T. g: re, P. C. Price, W. Scotson, R. H. &amp;iconic, W. 10Idh 'flgtOn, tH. Whitfield, J. P. Hnxtable, and F. riessl434loe. : hon. secretary, Mr. W. Atkin ; hon. Witati ' 'Tr. J. P. Reynolds: hon. registrar, Mr. J. P. hen Fieldhouse. r, A, • auditor, Mr. F. 'KIN expressed great regret that the Society</p>
