      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <p>A.</p>
        <p>VOTES OF THANKS.</p>
        <p>The Calutmot moved a vote of thanks to Mr. Gough and to Nit. Atkin for their services as secretary and treasurer respectively. Everyone knew how as yesrs went on the work of the Society became more strennous and_the duties devolving upon the officials increased.</p>
        <p>Mr. &amp;AMT.:ENG seconded and the vote was accorded.</p>
        <p>Mr. GOUGH Slid he would be sorry to give up the secretaryship, but he found he hal not time for it. With such an experieoced secretary as Mr. Atkin, the Society would be the gailner. On the proposition of the CHATRNIIN, seconded hy Mr. AmN, a similsr compliment WAS accorded to Mr. Newcombe for his many years' devoted work as registrar and auditor : to Mr. R. EL Pearson. leader of the orchestra Mr. Johnson, librarian; Mr. W. H. Payton, organist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>TRE SEkSON'S PEOGRMIE.</head>
        <p>TRE SEkSON'S PEOGRMIE.</p>
        <p>The Cr t unn ts said the arrangements for the corning season were well in hand and they were looking forward to a very successful series of concerts. At the first concert on November 12th, they intended to give " The Ancient Mariner " and short miscellaneous second part. Oa December 23th (Boxing Day) they were to have a change, for instead of repeating "The Messiah" they were arranging a miscellaneous programme which would provide surficient work to keep the chorus an -I orchestra interested, ts well as including solos. The last concert would be held on March 18th, and as it would be in Lent they had chosen Gounod's " Redemetion " a very beautiful work which they all enjoyed last time they gave it. Mr. Crossley called attention to the fe ;t that two or three concerts would be held on Thursday evenings. That never happened before owing to the fact that Halle's concerts were held on that evening and they had therefore dillculty in getting the orchestra complete. The number of lorchestras had been so much increased of late years— the multiplication of eaMs in Liverpool and Manchester being one of the causes—that there should be no difficulty in that respect now and as Thursday was the early closing day at Warrington it should be a suitable one for the audiences. It had &amp;ways bees found in the opera week that Thursday was the most crowded night. Referring to the Society's resources, Mr. Crossley said the tenors and basses required strengthen- ing. Tee sopranos were excellent and the contraltos had improved 50 per cent. in tone and quality during the last two seasons. The tenors and basses had good voices but were deficient in numbers. I A vote of thanks was passed to the Chairman on the pranosition of Mr. W. H. PkYTON. The CHAIRMAN said that was his eighteenth or nineteenth season with the s..ociety and he was not a bit tired of it yet.' He hoped they would all continue to work together as well as they had done in the past.</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, September 12, 1908.</p>
