      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>9d.</head>
        <p>9d.</p>
        <p>OUR -LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Tll3 New Wesleyan Ministers.</p>
        <p>Alluding to the ministerial changes the Warrington Circuit Wesleyan Church Record s Lys :—" By the time the magazine is in our readers' hands a new Methodist year will have commenced. It is always an interesting time. There is present a note of sadness and hopeful- ness. This year marks a great change in local Methodism. As a circuit we lose three and receive three ministers. The Revs. Sidney Pitt and E. J. Gawne enter upon their new circuits with the prayers and best wishes of hosts of friends made during their stay in %Warrington. Or friend, the Rev. William Mellor, lays down the responsibilities of the active ministry. lie has won all hearts. In his retirement he has the prayers of the Church that he may be spared to enjoy the freedom for many years. The brethren who come amongst us will receive a hearty welcome to their new sphere of labour. We pray that their ministry may be very successful."</p>
        <p>REV. E. TITAN-ST-TARO NEELING AT BOLD-STREET WESLEYAN CHURCEL</p>
        <p>The Rev. E. Blaushard Keeling, the newly-appainted superintendent minister for this circuit, preached his first sermon in Bold-street Wesleyan Church last Sunday , morning before a fairly good congregation. He took for his text "The voice of him that crieth in the wilderness, Prepare ye the way of the Lord, make straight in the desert a highway for our God " (Isaiah ' xl. 3). The rev. gentleman said that when John the Baptist was asked to give an account of himself by the , messengers of the Pharisees he said he was the Voice and that it was his business to call upon the people to prepare the way of the Lord, and as he (the preacher) understood preparation, it meant repentance. He did not ask the people to fast, lament and pray, but he told them to mend their ways, and to do what they were able. This whole chapter was rather a fine commentary, if one may call it so, on the message which John him- self uttered. Of course, as nearly always is the case in the prophetical writings, a great deal in the chapter was figurative and metaphor, and one needed to know a little bit of the things in the Bible to lend weight to the illusion. An almost universal forgotten fact is that this was a country without roads. Very great many illusions unveiled in the Old and New Testaments had an altogether different meaning, when they remembered there were hardly any roads at all. The whole principle of living was different. It they built a city the first consideration was that it should be difficult to reach. Why ? Because their first considera- tion was safety. Thera was a time, if they had a large family of sons it was considered a blessing, because there were so many more defenders against their foes. Now we have hardly a single fortified town in the , country. The world is getting a little better and one hopes that before very many centuries are past the I warships and the fo:tresses between country and country will be as obsolete as city walls are now. Referring to the Israelites the Rev, gentleman said that it was a wonderful story. They had been punished as very few nations had. In fact as you read this story you felt that there was something almost savage and ferocious in the punishment of this fallen people. They were not likely to turn to the end that was awaiting them, any more than the captured city is likely to welcome with sincere trust the congaeror to come throngh it. They had been tried ; they had been condemned, and the punishment had fallen upon them. The preacher implored the congregation to make a more in the direction of their God. He never forced his way ; the utmost approach was that He knocked at the door and called to the inhabitant inside. The message from God to men is not so much yield to God when lie forces himself upon you ; the message is to repent, change your mind towards God ; and take another attitude. In the evening the Rev. Mr. Keeling preached again to a large congregation and was listened to with close attention.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>REV. F. NV. KERNICR',</head>
        <p>REV. F. NV. KERNICR',</p>
        <p>- - The advent to the \Carrington Circuit of the Rev. F. Walwyn Remick, took place last Sunday morn- ing, when he preached to a good congregation in Peuketh Wesleyan Church. The congregation of Bewsey-roaci Wesleyan Church was favoured in the evening by a very effective sermon from the same gentleman, who took for his text " Who is my neigh- bour?' (St. Luke x. 29) and " Which of these three thinkest thou proved neighbour to him that fell among thieves "(St. Luke x., 31.) He said that the two verses gave altogether a different standpoint ; representing altogether different experiences of life. The question " Who is my neighbour ?" really was behind the social problems of modern days. The preaching of Jesus Christ held men and women from the beginning to tend as perhaps no other bad before. Two men passed the man who had been robbed. One was a priest. He may have been a minister in his day and genera- tion. He might have been thinking of some discourse in the Synagogue. There was his neighbour, but how he looked at him and passed by on the other side. The man saw the pitiable end of the poor fellow who was lying by the roadside. He went away still respectable, still a priest, still religious, but having lost his chance of becoming a man. He had lost the opportunity of his life, he passed out of this Book and he passed out of his moral record and it may be of God. Almost the whole of the teachings of Jesus Christ is made up of lost opportunities. Dives was a rich man but surely he was not a bad man. He was generous with his money. On his threshold thore was lying a man with sores. Dives passed by on the other side and time came when he lost his opportunity; when the opportunity was removed, and the beggar died. Nobody thought about him. The angels carried him into Abraham's bosom. But the moral fact remains, Dives' opportunity was gone. They had had their own opportunities of showing mercy and doing kindnesses, were they passing by on the othcr side ? It may be that there were some who were very near to thera,whoin they could help just by a look or a grasp of hand. How often was it that in a family a man only wanted a little help and they were afraid to give it. In conclusion the rev, gentleman said he thanked God that there are many men living in the spirit of the good Samaritan, many carrying on their business in the spirit of Christ, and many men carrying the spirit of Christ to the °nice on Monday morning. If they could not carry the spirit of Christ into their business perhaps it would be better they did not enter business at all. RECOGNITION MEETING AT BEWSEY-ROAD</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>CHURCH.</head>
        <p>CHURCH.</p>
        <p>A hearty welcome was extended to the Rev. Blanshard Keeling at a meeting held at Bewsey-road Church on Monday evening and attended by over 400 members of the congregation and friends. Mr. J. W. Hayward presided, and was supported by various officers of the church. In the gallery sat the elder Sunday school scholars and members of the Wesley Guild who rendered special music during the course of the gathering. Mr. Havwaltri said they were there to welcome the twelfth minister of Bewsey-road Church, and they ware hoping he was the best. (Laughter.) They had resolved at Bewsey-road to be a homely church—a church where they could shake hands with strangers. (Applause.) A circuit officer who had come especially to introduce the Rev. Blanshard Reeling, then spoke. He said he found Mr. Keeling in almost as smoky a town as this. He had come from a &amp;lurch in which there was a great deal of life and vitality—from Middlesborough. They had come there that night with the intention of carrying on that work. As the first note of the new ,Connexinnal year ha would ask them to give him their help, and to receive him into their hearts. The harvest was big enough if only there were lahourers enough. He hoped they would take Mr. and Mrs. Keeling and Miss Keeling into their hearts. (Apnlause). Miss Silcock rendered the solo " Nearer, my God, to Thee," and Mr. J. Hannan also contributed a solo. Mr. JOHN UPSON said it was his duty to welcome the new pastor. A week ago, in the interregnurn of the work of late super aed the super who had come amongst them—when they had the reins of the govern- ment of the church in their own hands—they held a ' meeting at which it was decided that everyone who attended Bewsey-road, or who had done so, should be visited, and as a conseillence of that they had that splendid meeting that night. The friends who were beginning to lose their faith in the brotherhood of Cnristianity had had that faith revived, and many of them were present. Bat the greater good had been done to those who had done the visiting, and many of them had received a rich blessing.. He would have the presumption to suggest to Mr. Keeling that that tem- porary expedient should be continued by those who had found such a rich blessing in the work of visitation. (Applause.) They also took a solemn resolution in the face of God that they would do what they could to ' welcome those who came among thorn. He was assum- ing that those present that night were the strong ones, but there were mauy weak people they would have to carry. At such a magnificent gathering as that it was a grand thing to make a now beginning, but was it going to end with only a beginning ? Were they going to drift back into the old ways ? They would unless they sought strength at the perennial source of all strength and at the foot of the Cross found their inspiration. He believed there were great things in store for them. (Applause). The Rev. B. Keeerso, who had a good reception,said that although he had been in the ministry for 33 years that was the healthiest reception he had ever attended. (Applause). Mr. Upson bad referred to him as their captain, but to be a good captain one must have an absolutely loyal army. One generally issued orders with the comfortable feeling that people would do pretty much as they liked. (Laughter). But he trembled at the work a minister of Jesus Christ had to do in these days. One of their laymen (Mr. Dawson of Huddersfield), said that their responsibility lay not to Christianity but to Jesus Christ. He was somewhat glad that he was not at their Church on the first Sunday of his ministry for he thought he generally preached his worst sermon at the commencement. (Laughter). But he honed to meet them next Sunday and he hoped I they would all join him at the table of the Lord, and pledge themselves to Jesus Christ. The sacrament of the Lord's slipper was, he thought, one of the most friendly services of the chnrch. It was a bond between them and their blessed Redeemer. He earnestly prayed that they might have a glorious time at Bewsey-road.</p>
        <p>He thanked them very mach for their reception. (Applause). Mr. Cortilrrn also spoke, asking the members of the church to consecrate themselves to Christ that the work of their minister should be blessed. _</p>
        <p>After the orgmist IV. Pearce) had skilfully 'rendered "The Storm," Dr. FERGUSON also addressed the gathering after which .fr. J. MITCHELL prmosel a comprehensive vote of thanks.</p>
        <p>r- The gathering concluded with the singing of the Doxology.</p>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>The Rev.T. GIADDING, thenewly-appointed minister was welcomed on Tuesday evening at a recognition meeting held in the Wesleyan Schoolroom, Padgate. Mr. William Bennett presided over a good attendance, and the proceedings throughout proved to be most enjoyable. tongs were well rendered by Miss Lillie Ward and Miss Mason, and Miss Edith Bennett officiated as accompanist on the piano. The CEIIIRNIAN, in opening the proceedings said that personally he had a great deal of sympathy with Wesleyan ministers. They came by invitation with the consent of the Conference, but they had had to leave friends and acquaintances and were dumped among perfect strangers, but he did not think Mr. Cladding would find the people of Padgate strangers, but rather friends. (Hear, hear.) They had a large Sunday school, a Guild, and a Bible ela.ss, so that there was plenty of work for all to do. He hoped they would code 'your to make Mr. and Mrs. Cladding feel at home, so that when they left them they would be able to go on their way rejoicing. (Applause.) Mr. NEWTON observed that they had met with glad- ness and pleasure and were hopeful of the future. They looked upon the advent of a few minister with the ' idea that they were having a fresh lease of life and that what Was languishing would be revived. (Applause.) Mr. DucKwortur said they wanted Mr. Cladding to feel that he was not coming amongst strangers but among brothers and sisters, lie hoped that at the close of his ministry they would be able to speak of him in I the same kindly terms they were able a few days ago to speak of Mr. Mellor. (Applause.) Dr. BENNETT, as an officer of the Church also addressed the meeting and gave Mr. Cladding a hearty welcome. The advent of a new minister revived the energy of some christian people vvhose spiritual force required a good deal of fanning to keep it going. These were opportunities of reviving their own christian character and of strengthening it. (Applause.) The Rev. T. GLADINNG, who was heartily received, in the course of an interesting and amusing speech said he had not come to a place where there was a piece of machinery which had been allowed to rust between a long interregnmn between two pastorates. The machinery had not had an opportunity of getting out of order, but it did him good to hear so much said in praise of the minister who had just left the circuit. That meant that he had not only done his work well but that the people of Padgate had gifts of appreciation, and he hoped he (Mr. Cladding) would be useful while he stayed with them. (Hear, hear, and applause.) Ile would take it for granted that those who filled the various offices in church would do their best for Christ and Methodism. He had no programme ant no hints to give. He hoped to see the members of the congregation in their own homes, and Mrs. Cladding and himself would be glad if they would call and see them' and little by little they would get to know each other, and the bonds of sympathy would draw them closely together. Whatever he might be able to do for the sake of the Master to lead them nearer to Wm would be more effectual because of the bonds of sympathy which had been evoked by the meeting that night. (Applause.) Refreshments were then served, and Mr. and Mrs. Cladding made the acquaintance of the ladies and gentlemen present, and were accorded a hearty welcome to Padgate.</p>
        <p>RECOGNITION MEETING AT STOCKTON HEATH</p>
        <p>A recognition tea and welcome meeting to the new ministers took place at Stockton Heath on Wednesday evening. A large number of friends sat down to tea, ;trays being given by Mesdames Meldrum, King, Sammons, Deakin. Bailey, Q•line, Weaver, Wilcockson, Messrs. Rowe, Maile and .1. Whitfield, son. At the meeting addresses were delivered by Dr. Bennett (chairman), the Rees. E. Blanshard Keeling and F. Walwyn Kernick, Messrs. R. Miller, Daakin, Brereton, A. Mounfleld and others. Special anthems were ren- dered by the choir under the conductorship of Mr. F. E. Penketh,and Miss M. Isherwood sang a solo.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>WATER POLO.</head>
        <p>WATER POLO.</p>
        <p>WIDNES V. WARRINGTON.—This match was decided at the Widnes Baths on nonday ei,ening before a moderate attendance. Warrington brought a fairly good team, which included C. Ashton, of Hyde Sed, the international half-back. Widnes had a full team out. The home team won the toss and elected to defend the shallow end. In the first moment Warrington were dangerous, and Hurstfleld was cal led upon, saving nicely. Ashton shone by clever ploy early on. From a melee in the Warrington goalmouth J. Peers gathered and scored. Ashton retaliated by shooting almost the full length of the bath, Hurstlield just saving. Grayson missed aline chance by holding the ball too long, and, when tackled, he shot over. Peers was the most prominent man in the bath, and scored twice in fine style. Half-time : Widnes. three goals ; Wirrington, nil. On resuming the visitors at once attacked. Widnes did not negotiate the difficulties of the deep end with much confidence, and Ashton soon put through with a terrific shot. He soured again in a few moments. Widnes were quite outplayed. Hnrstileld's form seemed to desert him, lace souring twice with only moderate shots‘ Ashton got another and Pepper defeated the home keeper with a hot one. Result: %Torrington, six gods ; Widnes, three. Teams :—Warrington: Goal, Daintith ; back. Duna and Ince ; half-back C. Ashton ; forwards, T. Pember- ton, H. Woolstencroft.ond R. Pepper. Widnes: Goal, J. Rurstfleld ; backs, T. Ildrner and U. Colgreave; half- backs, J. Peers ; forwards, Loverick, J. 'Grayson, and P. Sutton. . . .</p>
        <p>A squadron race was very keenly contested,and ended in a victory for Warrington by two yards. Ashton gave a clever display of life-saving methods during the evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>Baptist Chnrch, Goibarne-street.</head>
        <p>Baptist Chnrch, Goibarne-street.</p>
        <p>The opening meeting of the Brotherhood was held in the Upper Room, Regent-street on Sunday afternoon. There was a capital attendance so that increased seating accommodation became an immediate necessity. Mr. E. Wynne Jones took the chair and the lesson was read by Mr. A. S. Elliott. The President (Rev. R. E. Letheren) gave a hearty welcome to those present and touching humorously on some features connected with the room used them to illustrate the highest things they had come to think about. He would set before them as their pattern a Brotherhood that had succeeded in impressing the world more than any that had gone before it and that had influenced all the ages that followed it. From an account given by St. Mark (iii. 13-19) of the founding of that Brotherhood they would learn the principles and purposes that would best make this Brotherhood a blessing to themselves and others. Here was some principle of selection at work that called men from the crowd to climb to Christ's side that from that height they might gather force to lift the mass to higher levels. Christ's influence distributed over the crowd was as , that of water on a marsh. The men He called to Him became a river to drain the marsh and drive the mill. They were called to study the mind and methods of Christ so as to bring them to bear on the manifold activities of life. Specially from being with Christ they would enter into sympathy with His spirit. What those men became Christ made them and He made them by means of that Brotherhood. He called them with a view to sending them forth to serve their fellows. Mr. Letheren hoped the Brotherhood would help men to utter what was in them, that it would be a school for ' citizens where they would study the facts of life and learn how to apply Christ's mind and methods to the n The room was available for social intercourse. He hoped they would get from it all the benefits of a club and none of its disadvantaces. Mr. J. A. Heywood and Mr. W. Clatworthy took part in the discussion that , folio +ed. Mr. T. Davies (secretary) explained the conditions on which the room was taken. Mr. S. !tickers was in charge of the music. A good number of names was taken for membership.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>St. James' Church.</head>
        <p>St. James' Church.</p>
        <p>At this Church on Sunday the annual sermons on behalf of the Society for the Propagation of the Gospel were preached by the Rev. S. Foskett, missionary at Madras, and his addresses on his work in India proved very interesting to I trge congregations. The collections amounted to over ZI Ss. On Monday evening a mission- :ary meeting was held in the schoolroom, when an address was delivered to a fairly large audience by Mr. Foskett. He described his work on the mission field at Madras, and the manners and customs of the people, also their temples and gods. lie also related the culties which had to be contended with on account of the castes, the customs of which he described. The lecture was very interesting and instructive, fully illustrated with limelight views. At the close the Vicar (Rev. IL B. Firth) proposed a vote of thanks to the lecturer, and asked the audience to assist the mission- aries abroad by their prayers and almsgiving.. Mr. T. E. Gibson seconded, and the vote was heartily passed. The lantern was manipolated by Mr. J. J. Firth (police court missionary), assisted by Mr. J. Pargher. The collection on Monday night realized .£1 Ss. sd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>St Peter's Church.</head>
        <p>St Peter's Church.</p>
        <p>On Sunday evening the Rev. James H. Garnett, the newly-appointed carats at St. Peter's, preached his first sermon to a large congregation from the words " With God all things are possible." He said he had come to be a fellow worker with them in that parish and in that town. It there was to be any result they must day by day be fellow workers with God. He asked them to make apparently impossible tasks possible by working together with God, for with Him, and Him only, could all things be possible.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>FACT.</head>
        <p>FACT.</p>
        <p>Fact is what we want. Opinion is not enough. Opinions differ. Here's a Lowton fact. You can test it.</p>
        <p>...... - Mr. John Itosbottom, 33, Stone Cross-lane, Lawton, Newton-le-Willows, says :—" I suffered for three months with cold in the kidneys, and all the time I was bad, work punished ma severely, especially bending work, for I had a sort of stiffness in the back which made it difficult for me to rise ; then there was a con- stant tired fc ?ling that made matters more unpleasant 3tiii. _</p>
        <p>" Doan's pills acted wonderfully quickly in my case. After taking them for a short time I was cured of all these ills. lam please.' to recommend them because I am sure they will do anybody good who suffers with backache. (Signed) John Rusbottom." . .</p>
        <p>Pains in the back are a sign of kidney disere ; the pain is caused by the kidneys, which lie under the smell of the back. Other common symptoms of kidney dis- order are dizziness, headache, watery swellings in the ankles and beneath the eyes, dimness of sight and urinary troubles. Doan's pills are a special kidney land bladder medicine • they act directly on the kidneys— no action on the bowels—thus curing the cause of back- ache, dropsy, gravel, rheumatism, and other kidney troubles.</p>
        <p>Doan's Ihcltache Kidney Pills are two shillings and ninepence per box (six boxes for thirteen shillings and ninepence). Of all chemists and stores, or post free, direct from Foster-McClellan Co., 8, Wells-street, Oxford-street, London, W. Doan's are the pills that cured Mr. llosbottom.</p>
      </div>
