        <p>311 t:</p>
        <p>ATHERTON v. WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>These teams met at the Warrington Swimming Baths on Thursday evening, before a moderate attendance. The teams were well matched and an exciting game resulted. C. Ashton, of the Warrington team, played a prominent part, and was applauded for some useful work. Half-time arrived with Warrington 2 goals, Atherton 2 goals. In the 'second half Warrington warmed to the game, goals being repeatedly scored by the home players. The scorers were :—C. Ashton, 4 ; Wolstencroft 1, and W. Ashton 1 for the home team ; Harrington 2 and Farnworth 2 for the visitors. The final score was :—Warrington, 6 ; Atherton, 4. It may be mentioned that the Atherton team had not been beaten before this season.—A 100 yards' members' handicap resulted :-Ist, Weir; 2nd, Pemberton; 3rd, Ice.</p>
        <p>An interesting incident is told by the "Manchester Guardian" concerning President Roosevelt. Over forty years ago a young woman left the Bacup distric and emigrated to America, where she married. At sister whom she left at Bacup soon lost trace of her. After all the years, this sister lately tried to find out the address of her relative, but every expedient failed. At last she wrote to President Roosevelt, asking him to help her in the search. For six months no reply came ; then, a few weeks ago, a letter bearing the Presidential seal was delivered to her, and enclosed in it was the address of the long-lost sister. No doubt President -Roosevelt decided that he could do it, and did it; but what agencies were set to work and at what cost one can only conjecture: The Bacup sister a few days ago left England to join the sister in the United States.</p>
        <p>THE</p>
        <p>It is now so long since Lord</p>
        <p>JOHN RUSSELL "wrote up</p>
        <p>"HOLY WAFER" 'No Popery' and ran IN THE STREETS, away," that the conditions under which a new reign of toleration was established in this country have almost passed out of the public consciousness. It was at a Coiasistory held in Rome in 1850 that Dr. NICHOLAS WISEMAN was appointed Cardinal and nominated Archbishop of Westrainater. It was believed at the Papal Court, as it seem to be believed in a more modified degree to-day that England was merely waiting for some signal sign to return to the spiritual jurisdiction of Rome. It was determined to appoint a new Catholic hierarchy with territorial designations. This was the only material thing done by the famous Bull of Pins IX. There were already Catholic Bishops and Archbishops ; but nobody outside their own communion took note of their titles. Now, however, there was to be an Arch- bishop of Westminster,a Bishop of Salford,a Bishop of Beverley, and so on. The Protestantism of England took alarai,being particularly exasperated by a pastoral in which Cardinal NEWMAN said : "Catholic England has been restored to its orbit in the ecclesiastical firmament, from which its light has long vanished." Then it WES that Lord JOHN RUSSELL wrote from Downing-street his celebrated letter to the Brsrror OF Dunium, in which be said that the Pope was assuming a pre- tension of supremacy over the realm of England, and spoke, further, of "the danger within the gates from the unworthy sons of the Church of England itself." The result was the Ecclesiastical Titles Act of 1851,which prohibited under penalties the appointment of "Bishops of pretended pro- vinces," never acted upon, and repealed in 1871. Acts of Parliament which are ignored alike by their authors and by those against whom they were directed may nevertheless become sources of public mischief. An Act passed in the reign of GEOIte E IV.—the very Act of Catholic Emancipa- tion, in fact—is now in danger of being defied in a manner to which encouragement is undoubtedly given by a recollection of the perfect safety which attended defiance of the Escleciastical Titles Act, described by Mr. RDEBUCK as "one of the meanest, Wiest, and most futile measures that ever dis- ;raced even bigotry itself." Most of us had forgotten that the Catholic Emancipation Act expressly declared that "if any R sman Catholic ecclesiastic shall excercise any of the rites or ceremonies of the Rmau Catholic religion, or wear the habits of his Ocder, save within the usual places of worship of the Raman Catholic religion, or in private houses, such ecclesiastical or other person shall forfeit for every such offence</p>
        <p>the SUM Of 250." Public attention is now called to the fact by Sir CHARLES McAdam:Jß, M.P., on behalf of certain Protestant organisations, and in connection with the Eucharistic Congress, and the intention to carry the Host in procession, to-morrow, through some of the streets in proximity to the new Roman Catholic Cathedral at Westminster.</p>
        <p>There can be no doubt that Mr. AlcAurntat has hit on a grave reason why such a procession should I not take place. It would be a breach of the law, l and that it might be a dangerous breach of the law is proved by the fact that arrangements have been made to give the processionists the protection of a large force of police. It is possible, it is even probable, that those who have arranged for this feature of the Roman Catholic Congress were unaware of the legal prohibition of the contemplated proceedings. In that case they will be all the more ready to listen to goad advice on the subject, and this they are receiving from various quarters. Obviously, the object of including such a provision in a statute of toleration was the prevention of public disorder. Whatever may happen in London it is beyond question that there are places in the kingdom in which the Host could not be carried in procession without th 3 certainty of riot. And yet I it is in every way likely that the London example would be followed elsewhere. If the law were I ignored in one case it would be certain to be pre- sumed on in others. It is, therefore, for the heads of the Congress to consider whether the gratification is quite worth the risk. The Eucharistic Congress is being held in England, as the POPE admits, because this is the most tolerant country in the world. It is surely injudicious, then, to say the least, to put its tolerance to an extreme test, and until there is some specific declaration on the subject, or until the procession has actually taken place, we shall not believe that this is what is really intended. The POPE says that the "Holy Wafer " can be carried in safety through the streets of London. It is a safety, however, that can only be guaranteed by the police. The prOper course is to repay hospitality and toleration by the observance of the law, and then to follow up forbearance by agitation for the repeal of a provision which is not in consonance with the temper of the times,</p>
        <p>though it has on its side that sort of wisdom which aims at the prevention of disorder.</p>
        <p>The Belgian Senate has TAKING OYER THE declared its readiness to Cons°. take over the Congo Free State. Presumably, it is prepared to accept all the responsibilities which that implies, and to respect the title of the Powers creating the Free State to be well satisfied that the moral and material welfare of the natives will</p>
        <p>be ensured under the new conditions. Wa have "our own right to have some say before we definitely recognise the transfer." That Sir Edward Grey has shown to be inhersnt in the nature of the case. We entered into the Inter- national Convention to secure free trade and ' the native wellbeing, and our rights to have these objects realised are, Sir Elward Grey has said" the very foundation and origin" of the existence of the Free State. Toe Belgian Senate has passed the Treaty of Cession and the Colonial Law which will regulate the government of the wide territory to be annexed to the Kingdom Reservation of large tracts for the enjoyment of monopolies is not equal freedom of trade. Forced labour and the entire withdrawal of their beneficial titlo to land do not conserve the welfare of the unhappy native. Oa these points the Belgian Government must speak with no uncertain voice. She can absolutely satisfy every observer. Rer assurances will win her the unqualified approval and admiration of everyone who to-day sees her taking up the burden of colonial administration.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. EL M. Wharton, the TEE LODGER revising barrister, who has VOTE. been holding his annutl</p>
        <p>Court at Warrington this week, has created somewhat of a sensation by his ruling with regard to lodger votes. We give Mr. Wharton every credit for desiring to administer the law strictly and conscientiously but we cannot look favourably upon a policy wh'ch makes it as difficult as possible to get on the list. In order to obtain a lodger vote, the law says that a person must have the exclusive occupation of rooms of the clear annual value of £lO. Many barristers appear to be content to interpret this as meaning that the rooms should be worth £lO to the lodger and so recently as Wednesday last a vote was allowed at Bolton in a case where the rent of the house was only 4s. 3d. par week and the claimant said he w ts paying 10s. per week for rooms. Not so with Mr. Wharton. Tae point which, as be himself puts it, makes a"very great impression" on his inind,is the ratable value of the premises. Having ascertained the ratable value and the number of rooms in the house, he proseeds by arithmetical division to ' arrive at the value of the claimant's apartments. If on this test he considers they fall short of £lO he almost invariably refuses the vote. The result is that the average lodger in Warrington will stand a very poor chance of exercising the franchise, for it requires a very good class of house indeed to include a separate tenancy of a ratable value of £lO. Even Mr. Wharton appeared to see the injustice of drawing a hard and fast line, and in one or two cases he did strain a point. Bat in the majority he was inexorable, and if the value of £lO could not be worked out to his satisfaction the claim failed. We think Mr. Wharton might well regulate his pro cedure by a more generous interpretation of the law and not make the path to the franchise more thorny than it already is. The genuine lodger is worthy of the kindliest consideration and because it is not his good fortune to occupy palatial premises he should not deprived of the rights of citzenship. It is only by looking at the matter in its broad aspect that equitable decisions can be arrived at and undue punctiliousness must have the effect of keeping many deserving people off the list.</p>
        <p>The recent wonderful THE PROGRESS achievements of Dalagrange OF AERIAL and the Brothers Wright NAVIGATION. mark the consistent</p>
        <p>advancement of study and experiment in mechanical flight. The latest and most significant development, however, of this uew science —for such it has already become—is likely to take place in Italy. It is proposed to establish a section at the Royal Polytechnic School of Naples, in which young engineers may be trained in the theoretic elements and practical knowlege of the problem at present available for collection. Toe proposal, we understand, has met with most cordial support, and is likely soon to be carried into effect. As yet there has not been, so far as we are aware, any serious attempt to classify and preserve accurate record of the innumerable experiments which have been made during the last 150 years. The greater proportion of them were, no doubt, wholly valueless ; but yet, among so much effort and expenditure of time and money, many observations must have been made and lost which would now be of very considerable assist- ance and save useless trouble. Academic study is almost certain to give coherence and order to the science. Text-books will be prepared, and a logical progress may be looked for as natural and regular, as has happened in medicine and surgery, for example. The Italian precedent will be watched with great interest, and no doubt will soon be followed by colleges of other countries, if it is found that the results produced are of sufficient value. The people of Italy are taking the keenest interest in the problem, and are very anxious to boast a really important step toward its solution.</p>
        <p>- -</p>
        <p>We regret to note that the LABOUR AND THE Labour party has decided NEWCASTLE to intervene in the contest VACANCY. between Liberal and Con-</p>
        <p>servative at Newcastle. At the General Election the Labour man, Mr. Walter Hudson, headed the poll, with the late Mr. Cairns (L) a close second. The present vacancy belongs in all political fairness to the Liberals unless the Conservatives can wrest it from them in fair combat. The nomination of Mr. Stephenson (Lab.) to try conclusions with Mr. Shortt (L) and Mr. Renwick (C) shows a want of consideration which is calculated to do serious damage not only to the Liberal but to the Labour party. Precisely the same fight-at-any-price policy was adopted by the Labour party of Dundee, though the sitting Labour Member, Mr. Alexander Wilkie, gave it no support,and it is fresh in all minds how thoroughly Mr. Winston Churchill beat both his opponents. There may not be mach more danger to the Liberal c mse in Newcastle, but the Conservatives there candidly admit that their chances are greatly improved by the intervention of a third candidate. The Labour party in trying to grab the seat, is not likely to advance its own cause one iota, and the policy of greed may ultimately result in the isolation of the Labour candidates at a future General Election. They owe more to Liberal support, especially in two-member constituencies, than to any other force or circumstance. So far as Newcastle is con- cerned, however, we are convinced that the hard- ! headed electors of the North will consider that a Liberal candidate has the best claim to a Liberal seat, and hammer home the lesson which Labour only imperfectly learnt at Dundee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>Something went wrong with the carillon at Peel Cliurch, Isle of Man, the other day, and on a Sunday morning it cheerfully chimed the tune of " Now the day is over." Perhaps it meant the season. * * The lodger who desires to obtain a vote in War- rington will, in most cases, find it as easy to worm his way through the eye of a needle as convince the present Revising Barrister of his qualifications. Mr. Wharton appears to look upon each applicant :as a criminal in embryo.</p>
        <p>* * * The Town Clerk has received instructions to apply for provisional orders to enable the Corporation to acquire property to complete the" circus " at Market Gate and also to amend certain local Acts, one of the objects being the alteration of the gas discounts. * * * Beyond the appointment of the Old .A.g3_ Pensions Committee, nothing has yet been done in Warrington to administer the Old Age Pensions Act. A meeting of the committee will, however, very probably be held next week and the machinery will then be put in motion for dealing with the applications which may be received.</p>
        <p>* * * The detection of some poachers due to the quacking of ducks, as told in the Northwich Police-court on Tuesday, is a modern variant of the old story of the saving of the Capitol by the quacking of the geese, It also reminds one of the story of the man who had stolen a duck and hidden it in a capacious pocket. At a critical moment the duck protested, the man was arrested, and all he could say in defence was, "I wish I'd never seen the beastly dock! "</p>
        <p>RU</p>
        <p>Cases of Nom3onformist ministers going over to the Church are rare, but by no means unknown. The late Rev. G. S. Reaney (formerly pastor of Wycliffe Congregational Church, Warrington), and subsequently vicar of Christ Church, East Greenwich, provided one instance. Another is that of the Rev. G. Buchanan • Ryley, formerly a leading Congregationalist in the East End; while the R 317. Warwick Pearse was formerly a Wesleyan. Among the candidates for Holy Orders at the forthcoming Michaelmas ordination ill ' St. Paul's Cathedral will be the Rev. Henry Bennett, who is resigning the Congregationalist pastorate at Hendon to eater the ministry of the Anglican Church. * * * We hear that the Corporation will in all probability apply for a provisional order to acquire land on the river bank, necessary for the erection of the new foot bridge at Howley. The Bridges sub-committee has another site in view and it will be brought under the. consideration of the General Purposes Committee at their next meeting. We hope something practical will quickly be decided upon as a bridge is sorely needed to relieve the traffic over Warrington Bridge and provide a ready means of communication between )47 Howley and Latchford.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>* * * It is doubtful whether there is a greater lover of his native town than our friend Alderman Arthur Bennett. He can see beauty spots in it which many otlairs fail to detect and at all times, whether in season or out of season,he always talks of Warrington and its virtues —as well as of its "smoke." At the reception of the resident Wesleyan Minister in Padgate schoolroom he stated that Warrington was the most interesting town in England, and for his part he would not change it as his birthplace for any other town in England, or even in the world. * * The Secondary School prize distribution—the first to be held as a separate and distinct function—proved I most interesting and successful. What was particu- larly encouraging was the creditable record of the year's work which the Principal was able to present. no pupils have done themselves credit in a number of examinations and notably in that for the certificate —or the King's Scholarship as it used to be called the distinctions achieved being a high tribute to the soundness of the instruction imparted by the staff. But the achievements of the pupils are not confined to the scholastic world. There were several gratifying successes at musical festivals and what is a source of special pride to the students is that two of their number have during the year been instrumental 111 saving human lives and one has received the Royal Humane Society's certificate. * * Crosfield's band is living up to its new name of Perfection. If the members of this organisation have not attained perfection in their playing, they cannot fall very far short of it, judging by recent contest successes. They have been keeping first class com- pany all through the season and last week end they capped their achievements by winning the champion- ship shield at tho Welsh National Eisteddfod any' the third prize at Belle Vue. The latter contest is perhaps second to none in England and Crosfield'a proved themselves to be instrumentalists of brilliant capabilities by coming in third. Black Dyke gained the top position. By the way it is curious how ofted the Warringtonians have been pitted against the famous Yorkshire band and they have not beaten them yet. They will meet them once again at Matlock to-day. We have the strongest admiration for Black Dyke and that is all the more reason why we should like Cros- field's to beat them. It would indeed be something to be proud of.</p>
        <p>* * * Thursday was the eightieth anniversary of the birth of Tolstoy. He was born at Yasnaya Poliana in 1823. His mother died before he was two years old. His father fought in the campaigns of 1813 and 1814, rose to the rank of lieutenant-colonel, only to resign hie commission because, " out of self-respect," says hie son, "he considered it impossible to serve during the latter (reactionary) part of Alexander's reign or under Nicholas." His mother's family, the Volkonskye, were descended from Rank, and through them Tolstoy is connected with many of the principal RUSSiail families. Tolstoy, in his twenty-third year, entered the military service and in 1853, when war broke out between Russia and England and France, he proceeded to Sebastopol, where he served on the staff of Prince Gortchakoff. He resigned his commission at the close of the war, and has devoted himself to literature and social reform ever since. Sir James Crichton-Browne is energetically pursuing his crusade against the housefly. Undoubtedly, as he told the conference of Associated A Sanitary Engineers at Liverpool on Tuesday, the-t evidence against the fly as a carrier of disease has accummulated enormously during the last few years. 3P It is so prolific that one fly, on the snowball system ' may leave twenty five millions of descendants in one'4 season. Its natural enemies, the spiders and the wasps, cannot be depended upon to keep the nuna—l hers down. The housewife dislikes the spiders more .. than the flies ; and the wasp, when he appears at ally prefers jams and sweets to his natural food. 11, , therefore, the fly is to be suppressed it must be done by getting at the breeding places. This is the method which has been adopted with so much success ill tropical countries, and its efficacy is being proved in a remarkable manner by the American health scientist, who has done so much to render even the Isthmus of Panama a habitable place for white men to work in.</p>
        <p>* * The people of Warrington do not patronise the highest class of music as well as they ought. Whether it is that they have so many other classes of enterta.o3"/ ment we do not know, but doubtless the increasine facilities for evening amusement have an injurious effect on the work of auch societies as the Warringtell; Musical Society. The annual meeting has Well held this week, aad the treasurer had regret- klly to announce a loss on the copper? for the past season amounting to Much of this adverse balance can be traced te a falling away of subscriptions to the series. .Ise 1904-5 they totalled £122, and they have steada fallen since then to £75 in 1907-8. There is a° implied reproach here to the upper middle-class i 3 Warrington. If they become indifferent to the succese lof the Musical Society, then the prospect of its e 00" tinuance and maintenance at such a high musical standard will be remote. The subscribers are appealed to to look upon the support of the Society as, a duty as well as a pleasure. They are in danger 0' being convicted of an apathy greater than could ever be laid at the door of the alleged-to-be heedless multitude. The support of the general public bee been distinctly encouraging, and with a strong membership and a profitable opera branch,the Society has no reason yet to despair.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>t- , ,gre• t- • rtø • .iant ter_e_l3): do• e'. -n.st</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>EUROPEAN BAPTIST CONGRESS.</head>
        <p>THE EUROPEAN BAPTIST CONGRESS.</p>
        <p>Rev. J. F. Matthews a Delegate.</p>
        <p>Altogether the unique series of meetings held in the Concordia-Saale Andreasstrasse, Berlin, in connection with the European Baptist Congress, have been 600- larly successful. The great Hall, capable of holding 2,000 people was far too small for its purpose. Besideo the 600 delegates from Great Britain, there were Russians, Germans, Poles, Swedes,Norwegians, Frenobr Swiss, Portuguese,. Roumanians, Bulgarians, ItalL etc. The hymns were printed in about 8 differ Bl. tongues, while the papers and addresses were in Englisi; and German. The British delegates travelled in four parties. The Rev. J. F. Matthews of Latchford join the one leaving Grimsby on Wednesday evening,Augrn 26th and reaching Hamburg on Friday, August 28tb: after a very stormy passage. The party received by the Baptists at Hamburg WI great cordiality. They provided breakfast en dinner. During the morning and afternoon .r,‘ tour of the harbour, and the historic building' of the beautiful port was made. A noteworthy feature of the tour was a visit paid to the Hamburg CemeterY2 where rest the remains of Johann Gerhardt OnckenAb' great pioneer of modern Baptist Churches in Germanll Denmark, Russia and Scandinavia. His persecution; were narrated and much was learned about the progreS of Baptists in Europe that was not known before tn. most of the delegates. Rev. J. F. Matthews and. Watson, who both spoke in German, voiced the feeling: of the Grimsby party for the royal hospitality of Hamburg friends. It was discovered while in Bert that some 4,000 Baptists bad been sent to Siberia ,„ , refusing to conform to the Orthodox Greek Church ii Russia. The subjects dealt with during the Conference vre.ll.g the relation of the Baptists to education and the soli the work of women ; the history, and development ,t Baptiste in Europe ' • Baptists and freedom of thou.e, and speech; Baptists and labour problems • BnPtirr, and the present outlook, etc., etc. The key. dap, Clifford gave his presidential address on Mondea, August 31st. Much, very much repression was nee seo while listening to one of the most masterly addrresthe ever given by the great Baptist leader. Germans are not given to applause, esPee'tu:' the religious gatherings. An epoch has been marked In __L history of the Free Church movement, which cla,nro but have a far-reaching effect both upon Bajtig,7oo home and abroad, as well as upon the Free ulunr' generally.</p>
      </div>
