      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>HE POLITICS OF THE WEEK.</head>
        <p>HE POLITICS OF THE WEEK.</p>
        <p>[FROM OrE LONDON CORRESPONDENT.]</p>
        <p>rho policy of Liberalism is often charged, by its rersaries, with the sacrifice of all other rights and srests of the community to the claims of Labour. dit is just as often, if not oftener, accused of swing such a lack of sympathy for Labour that the rking man can expect no more from a Liberal than 1:o a Conservative Government. It would be possible to bring home the one accusation without illy refuting the other. There is, consequently, trong presumption that neither can be true. The 9 essence of Liberalism requires a complete Partiality between class and class, as between creed creed. If millionaires were subject to proscrip- a or peers to unfair disabilities, the Liberal party aid be bound by its principles to become as snuous a defender of the peer or the millionaire Oast the grievances from which he suffered as of workman or the workless against another set of evances. The "masses" have absorbed a larger Lre of attention in Liberal legislation than the lasses," not, of course, because they are the aass.es," but because there were more inequalities I injustices to remedy in their case than in any .er.</p>
        <p>THE TRADE UNION CONGRESS. t is a matter deserving of especial note how ?ortant a part of what used, year after year, to be programme set before the trade unionists in coo- rice, has now been eliminated because it has been en in hand by the present Government. The tde Disputes Act has effected what had become, r since the Taff Vale Decision, the most urgent aim ,rade unionists' agitation—that is, the protection in a pious aspiration into an actuality shortly to be heed. The Miners' Eight Hours Bill has passed second reading. It is true that, as Hr. Shackleton nted out in his presidential address at the Trade ion Congress, the Labour Party, while recognising Old Age Pensions Act as a great step forward, :mai it as only an instalment. But that is exactly v the Government regard it. The reduction of the limit is an end to be desired and sought. A jority of the town population is worn out before age of seventy is reached. It is, however, by con- trating itself upon an end that, though difficult, feasible, instead of pursuing one that 13 desirable itself but out of reach, that the Government has n able to make the "great step " which is repre- ted by the Act.</p>
        <p>LABOUR AND THE LICENSING BILL. Vhile Mr. Shackleton welcomed the Old Age isions Act as a great step forward, he described Licensing,Bill as "the great political and social aaure of the,Session." His view of it runs entirely nter to that which its opponents profess to take eA they represent it as a concession made to Iperance reformers in disregard of the general ad of public opinion. On the contrary, Mr. ,ckleton assorted that it appeals much more directly lie great mass of public feeling than to the temper- a reformers. The main purpose of the Bill, as he atly insisted, is to recover for the nation control r the drink traffic. Vehement efforts have been ia to prejudice the working-man against it by the ununtain ad erumenant the pocket-logic that his r will cost him more. But if the President of the ,de Union Congress has diagnosed the attitude of O,nrn class correctly, the working-man either does believe the warning or, if he believes it, he also eves that he will gain more than he will lose in the 3 run. If the Licensing Bill makes any one laeal "—as Lord Hugh Cecil would say—it will not the working-man.</p>
        <p>THE OVERHANGING CLOUD. the present moment the problem of unemploy- it looms very large. There may, possibly, be le alleviation of the gloomy prospect which seems hreaten us for the winter months. Signs of lval have appeared in the iron and steel trades. parations have been made to put additional laces in blast, and shipbuilders and engineers ect to be busier. But the protracted strike on the rth-East Coast has set up a condition of distress, only amongst the workers and their families, but he retail trades dependent on their custom, which be felt for a long time, even should the efforts being made prove successful. In Lancashire an atrial conflict on a gigantic scale appears to be ainent. Already the volume of unemployment has I:).ed unusual proportions. Trade, as is well- 'we, moves in cycles, and to a cycle of booming le has succeeded a cycle of depression. It is not, revev, merely the shrinkage in the oversea trade eh is responsible for the congested state of the 3nr market. Though there has been a very con- !rable falling-off in British exports for the first 1-tlirds of the year—a decrease of 30 millions, or over 10 per cent., as against the same period of 7—a comparison with 1002, declared by Mr. imberlain in 1003 to be one of the best years tish trade had ever known, shows an increase of millions. The state of the export trade is only a le factor in the complicated sum of causes which }duces prosperity or depression.</p>
        <p>NO•SHORT CUT TO A SOLUTION. ust as it is impossible to trace the origin of trade cession back to a single cause, so it is equally )ossible to find a single and complete remedy which Ldo away with the evil of unemployment. Prudent distrust a panacea, whether it 19 proffered for the of the human body or for the ills of the body tin- Every trade has its periods of expansion and Periods of cmtraction, and every trade therefore ites its unemployed. Neither Free Trade nor Pro- can insure a country against depression and raPloyment. All that the advocates of Free Trade rn for it is, that it acts as a lever or a spring to lk the fall of a declining trade, and to accelerate uPward movement when it begins again. The 'll Reformer may claim, if he likes, that Protection guarantee against unemployment, but as be cannot US any protectionist country which has no rnployed, the claim would only brand him as a cit. It is to be feared that the dream of banishing naPlnyment by legislation is as illusory as the al:a of keeping it at bay with Tariff Reform.</p>
        <p>"MAKING WORK." ,tr. Shackleton thinks that the " Right to Work " Should be introduced again next session. But, rt from the impracticability on the score of costs scheme which aims at "creating " work almost artably results in creating, ultimately, fresh unem- Ment. Production which is uneconomic aggravates teed of allaying the evil. There is, perhaps, more be said for Mr. Shackleton's other prescription— 'educe the hours of those who are in employment. Pourse, if the hours of labour are to be reduced; 'le the same wages are paid, the cost of production 1 be increased, and trade will go to the places 4'o the cost is at the lower level. Thus we should e the same vicious circle again. But where workers ee to work, and be paid for, short time in an 'tosperous season, it is possible to avoid cutting rn the number of workers when the industrial rltt 45 slack. Mr. Shackleton believes that by l'urig all systematic overtime ranch can be done Itevent unemployment. This is a different question that of the redtiction of ordinary hours. A 1410 amount of overtime may operate as a preventive, tas a cause of unemployment. If in busy times Ile overtime is worked, bad times will mean not so eh dismissal of workers as the cutting down of Dvertime.</p>
        <p>THREE KINDS OF PROBLEMS. Mere is a general recognition now of the funda- -0141 cleavage, in what is indiscriminately labelled , flemployment question, between the problems I relate to the treatment of the unemployable and 'Ele which relate to the treatment of those who are I,able of work if they had it to do. But there is a very frequent confusion between the problems affect skilled and those that affect unskilled °hr. In the case of skilled labour the difficulty is lented by the need of a surplus stock of labour, as which is really required when trade is alldiag, but for which there is no use in a period .elepression. The case of unskilled labour is trellt• There the supply is always much in excess e demand. This disparity can, to some extent at !civellis, be re-adjusted through an improved system ' coation to repair the harm done by the decay of 'vrenticeship, by holding ont inducements (as the etornent have done in the Small Holdings Bill) to hagricultural population to remain on the land, by Oaeans as afforestation for making fuller use of id, and reviving some industries that are sub- t; t , agriculture, and by a policy such as Mr. ' pressing on the local authorities of carrying Pc r building operations and other works, as far tsible, when trade is slack. There is no royal etb-0 the solution of the Unemployed problem, [Oll7 it concerns skilled or unskilled labour, but by ,at Tg many lines of advance simultaneously a ceal of progress may be made.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>TRADE UNION PROPOSALS.</head>
        <p>TRADE UNION PROPOSALS.</p>
        <p>The proposals of the Trade Union Congress Com- mittee for dealing with unemployment coincide, as regards the distribution of work by public bodies, with the line which Mr. Burns has always taken. The experiment of labour colonies has not answered well in the past, but the Committee seem to think that it might be more successful if the colonies were utilised as a training ground to pass the men on to small holdings. Unfortunately the class of men who find their way to the colon:es are seldom adapted to the kind of life which is necessary for those who wish to make their small holdings remuneiative. They have neither the energy, nor the endurance, nor the interest in the work which are essential for success.</p>
        <p>THE NEWCASTLE ELECTION. Surprise has been felt at the Conservative choice of a candidate to fight the seat rendered vacant at New- castle by the death of Mr. Cairns. Sir Walter Plummer has in the past showed himself to be a stronger candidate than Mr. Renwick. But it is on Mr. Renwick, who sat as junior member from 1900 to 1903, that the Conservative choice has fallen. The explanation is said to be that Sir Walter Plummer does not feel himself free to take up the standpoint of " the Trade" towards the Licensing Bill. Newcastle, the constituency which grew tired of the honour of being represented by Lord Morley, is not an easy con- stituency for a stranger to woo. Mr. Shortt, the Liberal candidate, is not exactly a stranger in New- castle, for he is a Newcastle man by birth. But he can hardly fail to be handicapped by the lack of these local advantages which Mr. Cairns possessed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Index to Inside Pages.</p>
        <p>Page. 2. Women's Chat and Fashions. Short Story : " Wanted a Chaperon." 3. Liberal v. Conservative : An Assault. The New Wesleyan Ministers. Musical Society's Meeting. Photographic Notes. G. How to Get Your Pension. District News. Crosfield's Band Successes. 7. Football, Cricket, Bowling, &amp;c. The Hippodrome.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Watch Committee on Thursday the licence of the Palace and Hippodrome was renewed for another year.</p>
        <p>Found Dead In a Field. A man named Walter Smith Cant, a wood turner, of no settled abode, was found dead in a field belong- ing to Mr. James Newton, at Howley yesterday after- noon. A bottle containing laudanum was lying by his side.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>Warrington Co-operative Society.</head>
        <p>The Warrington Co-operative Society.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the employs was held in the Co-opera- tive Hall on Thursday, to distribute the certificates to the successful members who obtained passes in book- keeping last session, when 15 received the certificates, three with honours. It was decided to start a class again nest week. The meeting was presided over by Mr. Fairer, chairman of the Educational Committee, who spoke words of encouragement to the employs and asked them to persevere and to aim for the higher positions in life, and not to be content with their present lot. Mr. J. Jarman, the teacher of the class, spoke upon the work of the class, and its objects. An I impromptu concert was given and was taken part in by Miss Lily Fairhurst, Mr. Moon and Mr. N. Cook, sen. Mr. A. Trow, the accompanist, gave a pianoforte sketch. Mr. Haigh, Mr. Bibby and Mr. Hall thanked all who had contributed to make the evening so enjoyable. Light refreshments were served.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT.—UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LA.NCASHIRE.--AdVt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>Wedding at Winwick.</head>
        <p>Wedding at Winwick.</p>
        <p>There was a pretty wedding at St. Oswald's Church, Winwick, on Wednesday, when Mr. Oswald Manning, son of the late Mr. W. Manning, Nantwicb, WAS married to Miss Mary E. Lnngworth, daughter of Mr. and Mrs. G. A. Longworth, Winwick. The Rector (Bev. Canon Penrbyn) officiated. The bride, who was given away by her father, was charmingly attired in cream voile, trimmed with white silk, with veil sur- mounted with wreath of orange blossom, and carried a shower bouquet of cream roses and white chrysan- themums from Cholmondeley Castle. Her ornaments were a gold locket and chain and a diamond ring, the gifts of the bridegroom. The brides- maids were Miss Olive E. Longworth (sister of the bride) and Miss Edith Whiting (cousin of the bride) who wore pretty dresses of white lawn trimmed with lace. Their bouquets were composed of pink and white sweet peas, and they wore gold diabolo brooches, the gifts of the bridegroom. Miss Lilian Longworth also attended, attired in a cinnamon brown mohair dress trimmed with Japanese embroidery. Mr. George A. Longworth, jun. (brother of the bride) dis- charged the duties of best man. After the ceremony a reception was held at the house of the bride's parents and later the happy couple left for London where the honeymoon is being spent. The bride's travelling dress was a brown cloth coat and skirt trimmed with silk, and hat to match. Numerous handsome presents were received, including a beautiful illustrated family Bible, the gift of Canon Penrhyn, Oddfellows' Bowling Prizes.</p>
        <p>The sixth annual bowling handicap of the (` Prince , of Wales" Lodge of Oddfellows has proved highly successful, and there was a large gathering of members at the Pelican Hotel on Thursday evening when the , distribution of prizes took place. The proceedings took the form of a smoker and a plesant evening was spent. Bro. T. P. Bradshaw presided. G.M. Lot Brooks of the Cadishead Lodge kindly consented to distribute the prizes, and he carried out the duties in a praiseworthy style. The committee were Bros. T. P. Bradshaw, J. Massey, G. F. Freeman, T Wadsworth, W. King, T. Reddish, J. W. Willett, and E. Clayton (secretary). The first prize con- sisted of the handsome cup and medal pre- sented by Mr. A. H. Crosfield, f.P., and was won by W. Roughley ; 2nd, china soup bowl, W. Cotton; 3rd, silver cruet, J. Warburton ; 4th, silver cruet, H. Jones ; sth, gold mounted umbrella, , T. Gibbs ; 6th, pair of boots, W. Fox ; 7th, copper kettle, H. Cook ; Bth, parcel of clothing, J. W.I Finch ; 9th, weather glass,Thomas Wadsworth ; 10th, time piece, J. Parsons ; 11th, leg of mutton, J. Lister ; 12th, silver jam dish, Mr. Reddish ; 13th, a hat, R. W. Cook ; 15th, neck tie, S. Pearce ; 16th, A. E Thornley. During the evening,solos were given by Mr. Tynan, Ur. T. Brooks, Bros. Alban Boyle, McLachlan, and F. Warburton. A duet was also rendered by Messrs. Willett and Clay. The Chairman (Bro. Bradshaw) proposed a vote of thanks to G.M. Lot Brooks for presenting the prizes, which , was passed with musical honours. TENNIS, CROQUET AND GARDEN REQUISITES.— J. S. GARLAND, Ironmonger, Bridge street, ‘Varrington.—Advt. Recital by Mr. David ciegg.</p>
        <p>An organ recital was given at Bold-street Wesleyan Church by Mr. David Clegg on Monday evening before a large and appreciative audience. This was the second occasion on which Mr. Clegg had played on the Bold-street organ, for it will be remembered that he gave recitals when the instrument was installed in July of last year. Whatever opinions may be held as to his methods, Mr. Clegg undeniably possesses the power of swaying the multitude. He plays to please the popular taste and always succeeds in doing so by giving his audiences light entertainment, whereas the performer who devotes himself conscientiously to a classical programme is often voted dull and heavy. There is a boldness and absence of the conventional about his style and he knows how to display the full resources of an instrument and to produce tone colour and orchestral effects. On Monday even- ing he held the close attention of his audience for two hours and was frequently and warmly applauded. The, programme was as follows : Dialogue for organ and wiad instrument (founded on an old carillon melody), David Clegg ' • symphony for organ (and orchestra) o. 1, David Clegg ; fugue for the organ in E minor, Sebastian Bach ; improvisation (founded on well-known hymn tunes and introducing church bells played in an adjoining vestry) ; Introitus fur die Orgel,Franz Liszt ; transciptions from various works of Richard Strauss (arranged and adapted to a modern organ by the performer); chorale and fugue from the sixth organ sonata, Mendelssohn ; a song of melody, David Clegg; grand fantasia on French themes, Halvey ; the famous storm piece, Moritz.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>WIGAN DISASTER. MAYOR of WARRINGTON'S SHILLING</head>
        <p>THE WIGAN DISASTER. MAYOR of WARRINGTON'S SHILLING</p>
        <p>FUND. The following is a list of subscriptions received up to last evening : Shillings. Amount previously acknowledged Children's Bazaar, "Lion Hotel" 462 Collected at "Lion Hotel" 22 431 Collected at ‘VycliEe Congregational Church l3l Collected at Conservative Club (second subscription) R. J. H. P. Turner Robert Chadwick Thomas F. W. Dixon W. Johnson lO Stephen Donoghue Box collection W. IL Bellian 5 R. Davies 5 W. H. Robinson T. J. L 5 S. J. Hewitt . Thomas H. Sutton A. J. Alleu Allred Jolley 5 T. Mason Thomas Hughes 5 Under ss. :—A.T. 2s. 6.. W.S. 2s. 6d , L.V. 2s. 6d., P.N. 25., R.H.D. 25., F.B. 25., J.B. 29., F.T. Is., W.S. Is., B.f. Is., W.T. is., J.H. is., J.S. Is., W.B. Is. Samuel Kershaw Joseph Allison B. Cunningham Every Success Samuel Wakefield, Lodge of the "R.A.0.8." Midland Hotel Cleansing Department l2l Councillor Craik Councillor Fulton Smith lO Warrington and District Caretakers Federation John Cann Mrs. R 5 Joseph Hedgcock 5 Warrington Window Cleaning Co., Ltd, Mrs. Peter Chrimes 5 Under Is. :—The 13.85„ Mrs. H. 25., S.G. 2s A.W.P. 15.,E.C.25.,M.W. 2s. 6d.' F.BM Is., E.D.P. is., L.H.F. is., E.J. 25., Sankey Boys is., R. is.</p>
        <p>3243110 d</p>
        <p>CLOSING OF THE FUND. The Mayor has decided to close the fund on Tuesday, September 22nd. It was originally intended to close it on September 19th, but as the Perfection Soap Works Band is giving a concert at the Hippodrome on Sunday, the 20th, the period has been extended to admit of a contribution from that source being included. TO-MORROW'S SACRED CONCERr AT THE</p>
        <p>_ HIPPODROME. We would remind our readers of the sacred concert to be given at the Palace and Hippodrome to-morrow (Sunday) evening in aid of the relief fund. Tee programme will be contributed to by Miss M. Houghton (soprano) who will sing " The promise of life," Mr. J. B. Poole (bass) who will sing "Thou art passing hence," Mr. J. Rabbitt, tenor, who will sing" Lend me your aid," Mr. W. Ryder (cornet) will play "The Lost Chord," and the augmented Palace Orchestra of 30 performers under the dire3tion of Mr. Ernest A. Widdop. The doors will open at 7.45 and the concert will commence at 8. The concert will be under the patronage of the Mayors of Warrington and Wigan and the directors af the Pearson and Knowles, Coal and Iron Co., Ltd. CONCERT BY THE PIARFECTION SOAP WORKS</p>
        <p>BAND. A sacred concert in aid of the relief fund will be given at the Palace and Hippodrome on Sunday evening, the 20th inst., by the Perfection Soap Works Band, assisted by Mr. J. Willett, tenor vocalist. The plan of reserved seats can be seen at Wadsworth's,Sankey-street. MARKET STALLIIOLDER'S GENEROUS ACT. The novel and praiseworthy way in which the 160 shillings standing to the credit of Samuel Kershaw in the above list were raised is worthy of special mention. He is a banana merchant, and on Saturday he loaded a stall in the Market with that tempting fruit and placed over it a notice to the effect that the whole of the takings of the stall would be handed over to the Mayor's fund. Only about half of the bananas thus offered were sold, but the proceeds amounted to the substantial sum of £B. _ _ .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>£lOO SENT TO WIGAN.</head>
        <p>£lOO SENT TO WIGAN.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday the Mayor sent a second contribution of £5O from the Warrington relief fund to the Mayor of Wigan. His Worship has received the following acknow- ledgment in respect of the first £5O forwarded "The Mayor's Parlour, Wigan, September sth, 1903. Dear sir,—Maypole disaster relief fund. I beg to acknowledge receipt of your contribution of £5O to the above fund and to thank you most sincerely on behalf of those who have suffered by this terrible calamity.— Tours faithfully, SAM WOOD, Mayor." CHILDREN'S SALE OF WORK. On Saturday afternoon a children's sale of work in aid of the relief fund was opened at the Lion Hotel by the Mayoress, who was accompanied by the Mayor. It was got up by Miss Lee (daughter of Mr. John Lee, the proprietor of the hotel), whose arrangements left noth- ing to be desired, both with regard to the arrangements of the stalls and the dresses of the children which were very effective. The Mayoress, having been briefly introduced by Mr. Lee, said she was pleased to see so many children so daintily attired, and she hoped the kindness which animated them in the present instance would be continued throughout their lives. The work in which they were then engaged was an excellent one. It had her warmest support, and she hoped a substantial sum would be raised for the widows and orphans of the men who had lost their lives by the disastrous colliery explosion. (Applause.) A hearty vote of thanks was accorded to her on the motion of Mr. Lee, his Worship briefly returning thanks on behalf of the Mayoress. The total sum realized was over £2O. PROCEEDS OF CHILDREN'S BAZAAR. . .</p>
        <p>Yesterday the Mayor received the following gratify- ing letter :—" Ye Olds Lion Hotel, Warrington, September 10th, 1903. Dear Mr. Mayon—Enclosed pleased find cheque for £2l 45., being £23 2s. proceeds of the children's bazaar, and £1 2s. collected in the hotel for your Wigan disaster fund.—Yours sincerely, JOHN LEE."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>We do not know how the respective prominence of the line on the " bill " is arranged but we should think Marie Jones merits a bigger line than she gets on the local hoardings. Her quick-change duologue episode is one of the smartest things of its kind we have seen. The artiste first appears as Madge Weston, a pert soubrette. She then disappears into the wings and after apparently walking round the back of the stage appears from die other side (successively) as an old Irish woman, a street Arab, a flower girl, a lodging house slavey and a coon boy. Each part is represented to the life not only in make-up but in voice and gesture, for Marie Jones is no mean actress. She has a sense of humour also, and with each role she contrives to raise a hearty laugh from the audience. We have wondered what a " trancka wonder " is and we are still wondering, though we have seen Alfonzo who is advertised as such. There is no question of his skill as an equilibrist and the origin- ality of his turn, but why, when and Yew he is a " trancka wonder" we know not. The dictionary fails to help us, and as we haven't met Alfonzo the matter will have to remain in mystery. Perhaps that s what it is meant to do ! Harry Cotton describes ihimself as an eccentric comedian, and the description fits. Harry is so eccentric that the crowd seems to " cotton" on to him at once. His song "It's just beginning to sprout" caused roars of laughter, while his personation of the battle-scarred veteran was screamingly funny. The Denwartz troupe of juvenile acrobats also contribute a popular number to an excellent programme. Their tumbling and posing are very clever, and the youngsters earn hearty rounds of applause. Lily Eyton is an acceptable character comedian, and the sisters Brendon as vocalists and sabot dancers are smart and amusing. Little Dando, described as the smallest comedian on earth, is exceed- ingly grotesque, particularly in the ri.)le of policeman. He is not much higher than his helraet,but he has a lot of fun in a small compass. An unusual item is sub- mitted by Gus Harris, the Hebrew character delineator, who poses as the late Lord Beaconsfield before a statue of the dead statesmen, with the Abbey in the background. Harris pings and recites in the first person and presents Disraeli as speaking a monologue, sketching his own career and his title to fame whether Jew or Gentile. The idea of the whole thing did not appeal to us but it pleased the bulk of the audience, which is the main thing. Mr. Widdop's orchestra interpreted all the musical items with skill, and the biogtaph presented an entertaining series of pictures. NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>An attractive programme- is announced for next week, the bill being headed by the ten Loonies in their celebrated mad musical sketch entitled "Dotty " which is said to be absolutely the funniest musical production every presented. The other artistes will be : —Lawrence Barclay, the p3pular character c:median, in his latest successes ; Georgina Middleton, soprano; Jimmy Laurie, patter comedian ; Gibbon and Belle in their novel acrobatie pot-pourri and dancing speciality • Jack Lane, the Yorkshire rustic at the piano; Mabeile May, dainty camedienne.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>By " Ovalist."</p>
        <p>One little dream with regard to Warrington's prospects has already been shattered, i.e. that they would win every match this month. They failed at the first hurdle, and we shall perhaps have to be con- tent if they are successful at home, for a while. Four easier matches for getting the team in form could hardly have been selected than Leigh,Widnes, Sminton and Rochdale Hornets, and they ought to be capable of winning the lot. The defeat at Leigh was only by a single point, but it sufficed to convince Warrington supporters that a team of veterans will not do when opposed to younger and fiercer blood.</p>
        <p>It must, of course, be recognised that the Warring- ton officials were in a predicament, with such men as J. Jenkins, Thomas, Shngars, and F. G. Jenkins, all absent in Wales. Three such forwards as the latter could ill be spared by any club, and their absence necessitated the bringing in of old stock. It is long since Warrington put as slow a forward team in the field as that of Saturday. Of the six who played, A. Boardman, P. Boardman, Belton, Naylor, Swift and Jolley, three if not four have passed beyond the state when they were eligible for first class football. The paucity of the Club's reserves was thus exemplified in the first match and it is a pity that a new forward or two has not been discovered during the close season. Meantime, the Club cannot afford to leave its best scrummagers in Wales a day longer than is avoidable.</p>
        <p>The blame for Saturday's disappointing result at Leigh must not be entirely laid at the door of the forwards. They might have been a lot better, , certainly, but they gave the backs quite enough ! chances to win by. The threequarter line was, how- ever, even worse than the forwards and such an experienced and skilled quartette probably never gave a more disjointed exhibition. It was a mistake in the first place for Taylor to have been put on the wing. He had nothing to do there, except occasion- ally kick the ball,and was certainly wasted. Dicken- son played erratically at right centre and Isherwood was trots° on the left. With Taylor right and Dickenson left there might have been something like last season's cohesion and under- standing, but the arrangement of the line at Leigh was ill-advised from the start. Lloyd and Brooks played very well at half or Warrington would have met with a worse defeat. Tilley also did some excellent defensive work, though he was not the equal of Clarkson' who bids fair to be the best full back in Lancashire before long.</p>
        <p>As is usual between meetings of these old Lancashire rivals the match was keenly contested. There was a good deal at stake —with points in the Northern, Lancashire and South Lancashire Leagues —and both sides were anxious to win. Last season Warrington missed the S.W.L. Cup through an early defeat at St. Helens and it looks as if the two points dropped at Lei'gh on Saturday mny be very badly wanted later on. Wigan and Runcorn bid fair to be strong rivals of the Wilderspool men. I leave Leigh out of the reckoning. They did not strike me as a team likely to win many matches away from Mather-lane, and they have only once appeared in the final for the " old tin pot."</p>
        <p>On the home side there were no new faces, if we except that of Bob Bate, who was more familiar to the Warrington people than to the home supporters. He did not play at all a bad game for his new club, and certainly handled the ball well. Whittaker backed him up smartly, but on the whole the Warrington pair were the best. Brooks was very much the superior of the other three, and his try was a beauty. In point of merit it was worth all the other scores in the match put together. Neville was a failure on the left wing. He has 'generally struck me as a man who makes very poor use of his speed and weight. The two Johnsons were smart, and for once had the better of the duel with Jack Fish. It is some time since the latter failed to get a try against Leigh.</p>
        <p>Fortunately for Warrington they are not likely to be very highly tried to-day. Widnes failed to beat Barrow with the advantage of ground, and cannot therefore be considered very dangerous foes at Wilderspool. Their last visit brought 20 points to Warrington, and the score should not be much less this afternoon if there is any of the old form left among the wirepullers. _- There are a couple of change§ in the team to meet Widnes, and they are only what might be expected after last Saturday's match. Taylor takes his old position in place of Isherwood, and a trial is being given to Harries (not Harris), the Blaeravon three- quarter, on the right wing. Harries scored a couple of tries for the" A" team on Saturday and displayed fair form. Anyhow there cannot be much harm in trying him against Widnes. He certainly requires experience. Naylor is on the injured list and stands down for Kettle.</p>
        <p>The team will be : Tilley, full back ; Fish, Dickenson, Taylor and Harries, threequarters ; Lloyd and Brooks, half backs ; A. Boarchnan, P. Boardman, Belton, Jolley, Swift and Kettle forwards.</p>
        <p>The Widnes Committee have made several drastic changes in the team to oppose Warrington. Kinsella has bean dropped, and R. Jones, the clever full back, who enjoys only indifferent health, has been persuaded to turn out again. Davies is replaced at centre by Harry Taylor, a younger brother of Ike Taylor, (the Warrington player), who makes his first appearance.</p>
        <p>The team is as follows : —Jones; Barber, Taylor, Lambert, Ditchfield ; Lally, Hicks: E. Aspey, Carr, Lloyd, Lynch, M`Carthy, and Rigby. Reserve, Curtis.</p>
        <p>The " A " team to meet Warrington " A" at Widnes : Kennedy; Loftus, Eccleston, Kinsella, Twist; Noon, O'Gara; Anderson, Johnson, liorg In, Sinnott, Turner, and Webster. Reserves, Byrom and Ryder.</p>
        <p>Warrington "A" has been selected as follows : laclntyre, full back ; Jordan, Isherwood, Cartwright, and Landor, threequarters ; Kaye and Daintith half back ; Forster, Harmer, Hewitt, Bradshaw, Chester, and Fairhurst forwards.</p>
        <p>In the match against Seedley Hornets, the young local forward, Chester, played a capital game and should train on into a useful man. At full back, Mclntyre justified his selection and should improve rapidly with practice.</p>
        <p>There is not much news of the missing Welshmen yet, but it is honed they will soon return. Thomas has asked for his transfer, but that his been refused, and a little reflection should convince the crack forward that lais best prospects are at Warrington. Jack Davies, the old Penygraig half-back, has been transferred by Warrington to Mid IThonnda. Evan Watkins has reported himself fit to play again, and will probably be seen wearing the jersey by the end of the month.</p>
        <p>Another item of news is that J. W. Morton, the amateur sprint champion of England, will be a spectator at to-day's match Morton is understood to have a fancy for playing Northern Union football. If so, Warrington ought to seriously consider the ques- tion of signing him on. A man of Morton's pace has more than half the requirements of a wing three- quarter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>Cricket.</head>
        <p>Cricket.</p>
        <p>The little red ball has nearly said good-bye to the playing pitch for this season. Only three matches are reported from Warrington and district as having taken place on Saturday.</p>
        <p>The Municipal Gainers entertained Grappenball 2nd, and won by 51 to 33. As the villagers could only muster nine men, however, there is some excuse for them. C. Hackney, 16, was the top scorer in the match. Green took G wickets for 9 runs.</p>
        <p>Grapponhall finished up the season in fine style at the expense of Weaverharn, who were all tumbled out for 11 runs, W. Carter did great execution with the ball, securing 0 wickets for 7 runs. The homesters rattled up 52, of which C. G. Hutchins hompiled 10.</p>
        <p>Latchford Baptists had a hot struggle at home with the Linotype men. J. R Conway took 7 wickets for 4 runs, but the Baptists were beaten in the end by the narrow margin of 2 runs. Not so much can be said of the batting, seeing that the totals were only 34 and 32. Last Friday evening the Pearson and Knowles team, the winners of the Warrington Works Championship, were entertained in fine style by Mr. James Dodds at his residence. Penketh House. A party of twenty players and friends sat down to what some of the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>WARRINGTON SECONDARY SCHuOL. ANNUAL PRIiibISTRIBUTION.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON SECONDARY SCHuOL. ANNUAL PRIiibISTRIBUTION.</p>
        <p>There was a large gathering at the Gymnasium yesterday afternoon on the occasion of the annual dis- tribution of prizes in connection with the Warrington Secondary School. The room was bright with choice flowers which had been very tastefully arranged. The Rev. Canon Stevenson presided, and was supported on the platform by the Mayor and Mayoress (Alderman and Mrs. Smethurst), Councillor Fulton Smith (chair- man of the Education Committee), Alderman Bennett, Councillor Pemberton, Rev. Father Wilson, Mr. A. J. Joliet', Mr. E. Plinston and Mr. E. S. A. Robson (principal of the school).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>"AN EDUCATIONAL OASIS."</head>
        <p>"AN EDUCATIONAL OASIS."</p>
        <p>The CIIMILMAN congratulated the school on having established its own prize day. Lie observed that the school stood in what might be called an educational oasis. There was the Secondary School itself and then in the same building when the Secondary School had gone home to enjoy itself in the evening, the place was again filled with large numbers of those who attended the excellent classes of the Technical School. Side by side they had the gymnasium in which the physical education of the people of the town was cared for and then, only across the way, was the School of Art which had done such excellent work and had produced so many great artists in the past and which they hoped and believed had a great future before it. Then there was the splendid Museum and Free Library to which the citizens of the town could resort for information, instruction and culture, and there was the Parr Hall which provided them with recreation and instruction of another character. In case their spirits might be too much elated and they should be, so to speak, intoxicated by all those educational advantages, they had the sobering effect of two buildings close at hand, one of which put its hands into their pockets at the beginning of every year, while the other kept them in good order when they did what they ought not to do. (Laughter.) The holding of that prize distri- bution marked an epoch in the history of the school which marked it out more clearly than ever as an independent educational institution. They congratu- lated the principal doubly because with wisdom and forethought he had recently taken steps greatly to add to his own happiness by the security of a helpmeet in his work. (Applause.) They congratulated the staff whose earnest labours and endeavours they all recog- nized and appreciated, and also the children who were present in such large numbers and who were trying to I take every advantage of the opportunities offered to them in the school. They had every hope that the school in the future would provide educational advantages for many and many of the rising generations of the good old town in which they lived. (Applause.) The PrtiNctPAL then presented a lengthy report on the work of the school and the successes achieved.</p>
        <p>THE PRIZE LIST. Councillor Fulton Smith then distributed the prizes to the following : Foam L—Ciertrnde Bell, best progress, drawing ; Mabel Robbins, needlework ; Arthur Greenough, English, history and geography • Thomas Johnson, French and mathematics ; Fred Holland, woodwork ; James Ballati, general science; James Groarko, class prize. FORM IL—Hilda Westcott, English ; Nellie Little- wood, needlework • Charles Hill, class prize, mathe- matics and French ' • Charles Milne, best progress ; Archie Hodgson, history and geography ; Norman Fernley, woodwork ; John Oakes, general science ; Harold Greenhalgh, drawing. FORM Ill.—May Warburton, French and physics ; Clarissa Hamiiton, English ; Lillian Johnson, history and geography ; Doris Parkinson, needlework ; Ilelen Wallington, Lain • Albert Dambill, class prize, mathe- matics and chemistry ; Thomas Cale, best progress; Samuel IL Reynolds, drawing ; Herbert S. Hicks, woodwork.</p>
        <p>_ FORM IV.—Florence Cutler,class prize,mathematics and French; Francis Woodhouse,English and physics ; Ethel Harris, best progress ; Eveline Butterworth,history and geography; Kathleen Ormsby, drawing; Winifred Fawcett, needlework; Edith Bradshaw, Latin and chemistry; John Foulkes, woodwork. FORM V B.—Ethel Cooper, class prize, English and French ; Hannah Blundell, best progress ; Mary Plinston, drawing ; Ada Atherton, needlework ; Lucy Marron, physics and chemistry ; Nora Brock, Latin t Dorothy Collin, Botany ; Robert R. Bunting, mathe- matics and woodwork; Wyndham K. Witts, history and geography. FouNt V A.—Mary Hackett, best progress, Latin; Violet M. Brown, English ; Martha Wright, French ; Miriam Russell, needlework ; Percy Clark, drawing, history and geography ; Reginald Bell, mathematics and woodwork ; George Whitfield, class prizo ; Edward Reddish, physics. FORM Vl.—Monica Vernon, class prize, French, physics ; Ruby Witts, English ; Helena Robbins drawing ; Florence Clark, needlework ; Ellen M. Shuflebotham, Latin; Joseph Malone, mathematics and chemistry ; William Furness, history and geography ; Thomas Allender, woodwork ; Thomas Groarife, best progress. FIRST YEAR PUPIL TEACHERS.—Dorothy Johnson, class prize, history and geography ; Elsie Swaine, mathematics, English, needlework ; Annie Upson, French ; Evangeline Dell, best progress ; Ada Baker, science ; Wilfred Barton, drawing ; Oswald White, manual training. SCHOLARSHIP PUPIL TEICHERS.—Lticy Fawcett, mathematics, history and geography; Annie Bell, English ; Edith Isaac, French ; Mary Hawkesworth, best progress ; Lilian Wright, drawing ; Marion Price, needlework ; Percy Boardman, class prize, physics.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>COUNCILLOR FULTON SMITH AND THE SCHOOL AGE.</head>
        <p>COUNCILLOR FULTON SMITH AND THE SCHOOL AGE.</p>
        <p>Having distributed -6;3 plizes. Councillor FULTON- SMITH delivered an address, and gave some sound advice to the pupils. He impressed upon them that the object of their lessons was that they might lay the foundation of knowledge—not the knowledge which the teacher gave thorn, but the knowledge which they acquired themselves. The teacher could only point out the way, and he expected that they would get their information by careful reading and study, so that when the time came they would be able to turn th3ir know- ledge to account and become useful citizens. (Applause.) Mr. Robson, in his report, regretted that so many left school at the age of fourteen. It certainly had been a very vexed question how best to deal with it. On the part of the parents there was a natural wish that their children should embark on business as early as possible, and employers wanted young lads rather than boys of sixteen or seventeen to begin because a boy of fourteen could pick up know- ledge in an office or works with greater celerity and in a manner which ho never forgot all his days. The only solution which presented itself to his (the speaker's) mind was that if a boy left school at four- teen and went into business, he ought to be compelled to attend school in the evening for a certain number of years so that he would not lose the knowledge gained in his school days. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>VOl:l3.S' OF THANKS.</head>
        <p>VOl:l3.S' OF THANKS.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR proposed a vote of thanks to Councillor' ,Fulton Smith who, he said, rendered great service to the town by his work in the cause of education. His Worship impressed upon the children the duty of love and gratitude to their parents. Mr. HENRY ROBERTS in seconding, said he was glad to take that opportunity of saying that the Education Committee owed to men like Mr. Robson and his staff their very sincere thanks for the zeal they put into their work. One had only to look back a year or two and compare the position of things then with the position to-day to see very marked and substantial progress. The Secondary School had become an institution of which they were immensely proud, and which was doing a great and good work the full effect of which would not be seen for many years. He would invite those friends of his who took a somewhat dour view of things to come into the schools and notice the equipment and the teachers as well as the physical and intellectual improvement of the children of to-day as compared with their predecessors, and he thought they would be satisfied with the material to which the future govern- ment of the country was to be entrusted. (Applause.) On the motion of Mr. JOLLEY seconded by Mr. PLINSTON a vote of thanks was passed to the Chairman. During the afternoon the school choir sang "Out in the Sunshine" (Ciro Pinsuti), and "Forty years on" (J. Farmer), very creditably. Miss Violet Brown interpreted "A may morning " (L. Denza), with taste and a trio "Sweet and Low" (Sir J. Baraby) was pleasingly given by Archie Hodgson, Mary Hackett and Dorothy McNeil whose voices blended splendidly.</p>
        <p>EYESTRAIN arises from small unnoticed defects of focus and causes Headache, Inflammation, Weak Eyes. Inability to stand the Light, Neuralgia, &amp;c. Glasses are the only cure. Glassos should only be worn after careful scientific testing, otherwise the sight may be permanently injured.—JoaNsroN WATSON, Eyesight Specialist, 84, Bridge-street, Warrington. Let us test your sight as a prudent precaution.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>Music in the Park.</head>
        <p>Music in the Park.</p>
        <p>The League of the Cross Band will occupy the stand on Sunday next under the conductorship of Mr. S. Hynes. Programme :—Hymn, " Oratory," arr. S, Hynes ; march, " Enchanter," J. H. White ; selection, "La Vestale," Spoutini; selection, "La Sonnambula," Bellini ; selection, " Daughter of the Regiment," Donizetti ; selection, "Recollections of Carl Rosa," H Round ; "God save the King."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>TIIE MAYORALTY OF WIDNES.</p>
        <p>At the close of the usual business of the Widnes Town Council on Tuesday, the members went into com- mittee to choose a successor to Alderman S. Owens, who has held the office of chief magistrate for the past two years. The choice fell upon Councillor Tom Smith, the popular member for West Bank, who promised to give his decision a few days later.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>MIDNIGEIT SCENE AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>MIDNIGEIT SCENE AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn, on Wednesday, a young man, who described himself as John Jones, of Wigan, was sentenced to a month's hard labour for "lodging out " at the Wigg works of the United Alkali Company. About midnight Police-constable Hulse found the prisoner and another man in the cook-house at the works. Prisoner said he was a tester, and had given the other man permission to stay there. Alfred Hough, a fireman, said the prisoner had previously asked him to "put him down" for the night, and when he refused he threatened to throw him in the Ship Canal and attempted to strike him. Hough lifted an iron bar to defend himself, when Police-constable Hulse appeared on the scene and arrested the prisoner. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>"KILLING ME BY INCHES." WIDNES PRISONER'S COMPLAINT.</head>
        <p>"KILLING ME BY INCHES." WIDNES PRISONER'S COMPLAINT.</p>
        <p>At the Widnes Police Court, on Monday, John Cooney, of no fixed abode, was charged with assaulting Police- constable Dickinson. He was further charged with being drunk and disorderly, and with assaulting Frederick Evans, an insurance agent. Michael Keegan was also charged with attempting to rescue prisoner, and obstructing the constable whilst in the execution of his 1 duty. Both prisoners pleaded not guilty.—Police- constable Dickinson stated that on Saturday evening he saw Cooney in Waterloo-road in a very drunken I condition, and lying on the pavement. He told him to get up and go home. He refused, and commenced Ito kick and fight with the constable. He had to call the assistance of Mr. Evans, who helped the constable to get him to the police station. When they got to Waterloo crossing the prisoner Keegan attempted to rescue Cooney, whereupon be was told to go away. He continued his struggling up to Victoria-road, where he was arrested, and charged. Frederick Evans, Police- sergeant Moseley, and Police-constable Huntingdon I gave corroborative evidence. Both prisoners have a bad record. Cooney was asked if he had any question to ask. He said, " You are killing me by inches. 1 have only been out of gaol three weeks.—Cooney was sentenced to one month for drunkenness, two months for assaulting the constable, and one month for the assault on Evans—four months in all. Keegan was fined ss. and costs, or, in default, fourteen days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>UNEMPLOYED.</head>
        <p>THE UNEMPLOYED.</p>
        <p>DEPUTATION TO WIDNES COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Many years have elapsed since industrious Widnes was troubled with the problem of unemployment. Owing to acute depression in the chemical trade, and the consequent necessity of the closing down of the yards, the townspeople have recently felt the pinch of want. With the knowledge that the local Council had the new Ditton-road scheme on hand, a large number of men assembled in front of the Town fall on Tuesday afternoon, just before the Council meeting, and a deputation was selected to wait upon the Mayor and councillors and urge that the project should be hurried on.</p>
        <p>The Mayor (Alderman S. Owens) received the deputa- tion in his parlour. Mr. Dan Garghan acted as spokes- man, and read a resolution petitioning the Council to take up the matter. The trade prospects were very black, and the men represented were looking anxiously to the coming winter months. The Mayor said that such a scarcity of employment in the town pained him. He was glad that they had formed a channel of communication with him. A few weeks ago he had ascertained the number of unemployed, but only that afternden he had been assured that early in October work in Widnes would brighten and find its normal level. In the meantime the Council would take the matter in hand and decide what course to adopt. The question was asked about the construction of Ditton-road.</p>
        <p>Alderman Quinn stated that it was impossible to go on with the Ditton-road work without the sanction of the Local Government Board. They were calling upon the owners to do the work, and if at the end of the month they did not comply with the notice, the Corpora- tion would have to borrow the money and do the work themselves.</p>
        <p>After an expression of thanks, the deputation with- drew.</p>
        <p>Urban District Council of Newton-in-Makerfleld had ,at its meeting on the 6cla July instant, appointed the Public Library Cammittee—the whole of the members of such Council and the Revs. J. Allatt and S. C. Hall, and Messrs. A. Peake and J. H. Profit being the members of the committee for the current year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>' VIADUCT INSTITUTE EXAMINATION RESULTS The following results have recently come to hand Science.—Building Construction—James Bedworth, Wm. Burrows and John Parr, Stage 11, 2nd class. lien Engines—Albert W. Bowden, Sidney F. Gould, Alf. J. ;Houghton Harold H. Marsh, Thos. Parkinson, James H. I Seddon, Henry Sweetlove, Stage TI, 2nd class ; Albert , G. Bygate, Harold Dring, Robert W. Mawdsley, Stage 1, Ist class; Wm. Pearson, Stanley H. Sedoskie, Norman R. Blinn, Stage 1, 2nd class. This completes the returns for 1908. MINERS' MEETING AT HAYDOCE. An open-air meeting was held at Haydock on Wednes- day night in connection with the effort that is being made by the Miners' Federation officials to induce all the men employed at the local collieries to join the union. Mr. S. Fletcher presided, and Mr. J. Parkinson made an appeal to the men to pay 24. levy which had been asked for in aid of the sufferers of the Maypole disaster, Wigan. He said he was there also to emphasise the fact that a number of the men had lapsed in their membership of the federation. They wanted them all to rejoin in order to show that Haydock VMS like the rest of Lancashi re,tho roughly organised.— Mr. Stephen Walsh M.P., made a lengthy address, from which it was evident that at Haydock and in different districts of the district the federation intended to pursue a vigorous propaganda with the object of keeping the miners' lodges up to full strength,but that for the present there was no intention of calling for an immediate strike. Mr. Walsh said it was a thankless task to have to talk to a lot of men about the necessity of organisation, but the need was always very real, and he strongly urged all the men to support the federation. He knew that 95 per cent, of them were members, but they were anxious—yea, they were determined—that the remaining 250 or 300 men should be brought into the fold, and that nothing should be left undone to maintain the strength and the integrity of their union. HAYDOCK COLLIERS AND THE NON-UNIONISTS. Mr. J. A. Seddon, M.P. for Newton Division on Saturday addressed a meeting of miners at Haydocii.— The chair was occupied by Mr. Dan Lawrenson who dealt specially with the crisis at the local collieries. Under the direction of the Miners' Federation they had for a considerable time refused to ride up or down the shafts of any of Messrs. Evans's pits with non-union workers.—This form of coercion had failed, and the federation had recently taken a ballot on the subject. Of the federation men no less than 98 per cent, had voted in favour of stopping work at pits where the non-unionists still refused to join the federation. He said it was clear that everybody was in earnest on that matter, and if the men did not all join there would certainly be a strike. The objections of the non- unionists were most flimsy,and he was sure that no one need fear that he would lose anything by following the lead which the Labour party was now giving to the workers of the country.—Mr. Seddon dealt chiefly with the question of old-age pensions,on which subject, he said, the Labour party hoped soon to force the Government to reduce the age to sixty and increase that ss. to 7s. 6d. a week. The Labonr party were far from satisfied in this matter, and they hoped soon to see the great wealth of a considerable section of the people contributing a fairer share to the aged veterans and to the working class generally. NEWTON URBAN COUNCIL. The monthly meeting of the Newton Urban Council was held at the Earlestown Town Hall on Monday evening. Mr. T. Ormand presided, and there were also present Messrs. W. Thompson (vice-chairman), C. B. F. Borrou, W. Collingwood, E. T. Evans, M. Seton, J. Naylor, E. L. Bent, G. F. Clarke. and C. Cole (clerk), R. T. Surtees (water engineer),and A. Bowes (surveyor). —A resolution of sympathy with the family of the late Mr. Houghton was carried, all the members of the Council standing. EDIIC iTIONAL M.tTTgRS. At a meeting of the Education Committee, a letter was read from the Clerk of the County Council enclosing copy of corresporklence which that Council had had with the Local Government Board with reference to the form of agreement as to the pro- posal of the Lancashire County Council to make agree- ments with non-county boroughs and urban district councils to enable the latter to raise loans for higher ' education purposes. He also enclosed a copy of the agreement, as settled by the law officers of the Crown, with a clause added by the County Council to indemnify them against the payment of any instalment of principal and interest in respect of borrowed moneys in the event of a borough becoming a county borough, which clause the Local Government Board have deJined so far to admit into the agreement. It was resolved that with reference to the application made by the Council to the Local Government Board on the Bth May,1907, for their sanction to the borrow- ing of money requisite to defray the cost of erecting the proposed technical school, and to the recent corres- pondence between the County Council and that Board, the Clerk be instituted to inform the Board that this Council were of opinion that, so far as they were con- cerned, the indemnity clause which the County Council were desirous of including in the proposed agreement appeared to them to be just and equitable, and urged them, in the educational interests of the district, to settle the matter without delay. The minutes were confirmed. LIBRARY COMMITTER. At a meeting of the Public Library Committee on July 20th the Clerk to the Council reported that the</p>
        <p>MIME</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>DIED FROII A BROKEN LEG.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brighouse" held an inquest at St. Helene, on Satsr- day, on the body of Thomas Hamilton, aged fifty, of Oldfleld-street, who died on Thursday morning at the Providence Hospital. The evidence was that the man was a furnaceman at Messrs. Quirk, Barton's Lead- works, and on Monday, June I,he was assisting in carry- ing out boxes of solder through the works to a lurry. The boxes weighed about a hundredweight, and in pass- ing a furnace he unfortunately stepped into a fire-hole and fell heavily. He sustained a compound fracture of the left leg, from which he died.—A verdict of" Acci- dental death " was returned.</p>
        <p>ORCHARD THIEVES FINED. Five youths living in the neighbourhood of Gerard's Bridge, St. Helens, were charged on Monday with raiding an orchard at Mr. Gibson's, Gerard's Bridge Farm. John Daley, James Baines, and Peter Kenny pleaded guilty, but Nathan Floyd and John M`Cawley denied stealing. Police Constable Weaver said that at a quarter past five on Sunday morning he saw the lads coming along College-street,and when he was approach- ing them they ran away. He followed and recovered dahlias, &amp;e. that had been stolen. The lads made statements incriminating one another, and -were tined ss. and costs each. They had been out all night.</p>
        <p>HOME LIFE IN ST. HELENS. STABBED BY DRUNKEN HUSBAND. At St. Helens, on Nlonday, Thomas Henry Penning- ton, collier, of Parr-street, St. Helens, was charged with stabbing his wife in the neck on Saturday afternoon.— Sergeant Fairhurst said that on Saturday he was in the neighbourhood of Smithy-brow, when some men told him that a man had cut his wife's throat. He went to the rear of Pennington's house and found a nerthhonr attending to Mrs. Pennington. lie went into the house and spoke to the husband, who said he had not touched ' his wife. He suggested that she might have fallen on a knife, but he knew nothing about it. Later a knife was handed to the officer by defendant's daughter. When charged on Sunday morning, when he was sober, he yid he knew nothing at all about it.—Chief Inspector Goodall said the trouble had been going on since Friday afternoon, when the couple quarrelled, and the wife cat her husband's head open by throwing a broken mug at him —Dr. Davies said the woman had a wound three- quarters of an inch deep in her neck. If she had fallen on a knife the wound would have been very much deeper.—lie was fined 40s. and costs or a month.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE TRAINING COLLEGE. Many Suitable Applicants Denied. At a meeting of the Cheshile County Education Com- mittee at Crewe on Monday the chairman (Dr. Hodgson) referred to the proposed new training college for the county. The Committee had, he said. received ' more applications for admission to the college from pupil teachers and ex-pupil teachers than from bursars. That was a serious matter, for everybody knew that it was physically impossible for ex-pupil teachers who ought to go to college to find college accommodation. The Committee had before them exceedingly good applications which it pained them very much to refuse. Owing to applications having been received from qualified bursars in excess of the accommodation pro- vided at the college it was impossible to admit any candidates except bursars. The Committee had felt so strongly on the subject that they had appointed a sub- committee to consider the possibility of some feasible scheme being evolved for the continuity of the education of ex-pupil teachers who are unable to gain admission to a training college. It was not lack of sympathy but lack of accommodation which they suffered from, and the time would come when they would have seriously to consider this question. A sub-committee, consisting of Professor Gonner, the Rev. J. Grant Bird, and the Chairman was appointed to go into the subject. A sub-committee was also appointel to consider the question of free places at secondary schools in the county,with a view to arriving at some basis for securing greater conformity in the conditions under which free places are awarded and held, and in regard to the examinations in connection therewith. It was decided to increase the grant male to the Cheshire Nursing Association from £l5O to £2O) in order to enable the Association to provide for the training of additional nurses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>CROSFIELD'S BAND. Successes at Llangollen and</head>
        <p>CROSFIELD'S BAND. Successes at Llangollen and</p>
        <p>Belle Vue:</p>
        <p>Crosaeld's Band have added to their laurels by carry- ing off the first prize at the Welsh National Eisteddfod at Langolien and the third prize at the great Belle Vue contest. _ _ _</p>
        <p>They journeyed to Llangollen on Saturday and found an audience of over 5,000 keenly interested in the -day's programme which consisted exclusivbly of brass band contests. During the morning there was decided in the crowded thoroughfares of the little town a quick-step contest, in which five bands participated, . viz. :—Crosfleld's Brass Band (Warrington), Nantlle Vale Royal Silver Band, Ferndale (Rhondda Valley) Prize Band, Royal Oakley (Festiniog) Silver Band, and Rhos Silver Band.</p>
        <p>. O. Shehperd, who adjudicated, awarded the first prize to Ferndale, and added that the Royal Oakley was a very close second. Later the same five bands again competed within the pavilion in the rendering of a grand selection front " Tutti in Maschera." The first prize (£25) was supple- mented with a cornet, a certificate signed by the adjudi- cator, and a silver challenge shield to be held as a. challenge by the victorious band for the ensuing twelve mouths. _</p>
        <p>Other bands had entered, viz. :—Gilfach Goch (Glamorganshire), Fonyrefail (Glarnorganshire), Irvrell Bank, Mountain Ash, Abertillery, Morgans Cwmtawe, Longridge's St. Lawrence Gossage's Soap, and Birken- head, but none of these put in an appearance. In the course of a brief adjudication, Mr. Sheppard said they had heard five really good bands. There had been no bad playing that day, but one or two bands seemed to have forgotten that they were playing inside and forced a little too much. Three out of the five were extraordinarily near each other in merit, and if the same three competed again next day on the same test piece, the awards might quite possibly be reversed. Every one of the five bands heard that day was capable of winning a prize ins very good class. The first prize was awarded to Cresfleld's Brass Band (Warrington),the second to the Royal Oakley (Festiniog) and the third to Ferndale.</p>
        <p>On returning to Warrington. Crosfield's band had a cordial reception. They paraded the principal streets playing selections and the shield was proudly displayed. On Monday the Band took part in the annual September contest at Belle Vue, which is the leading event of i. kind in the North of England. The twenty bands that annually compete there invariably include the cream of Lancashire and Yorkshire musicians, and this is, happily, tantamount to saying that the very best bands in the country struggle in keenest rivalry for the Belle Vue Challenge Cup. Monday's contest saw no falling off in either the strenuousness of the contest or the uniformly high standard of music attained by the various bands engaged, these bands being Perfection , Soap Works, Black Dyke, Stalybridge Borough, Danne- more Steel Works (Sheffield), Nelson Old, Goodshaw, Pemberton Old, Irwell Springs, Shaw, Lincoln Malle- able Iron and Steel Alloy Works, flebden Bridge, Pendleton Old, Wingates Temperance. Lea Mills, \Vyke, Lindley, Rushden Temperance, Linthwaite, King Cross Subscription, and Royal Oakley. Time was when the name of Besses-o'-th'-Barn was one to conjure with at Belle Vue contests. The last four years, however, have seen this combination an absentee from the annual gathering, owing, of course, to its having become professional in its character. The chief interest of the contest seemed to centre around Wingates Temperance, which, by virtue of its having won first prize for the last two years in succes- sion needed only to come out on top to secure absolute ownership of the Belle Vue Challenge Cup. As events turned out, the Wingates band sadly disappointed its many followers, not even figuring in the first six. It is interesting to note, by the way, that since this cup was first offered, twenty years ago, no band has ever secured it outright, though Black Dike and Wyke Temperance, as well as Wingates Temperance, have each won it twice in succession. The test piece consisted of the grand fantasia, "A Souvenir of Grieg," specially arranged -for the contest by Lieutenant Charles Godfrey, who with Carl Kiefert and Mr. J. 0. Shepherd, adjudged the results.</p>
        <p>From one o'clock to almost seven the great hall was crowded to suffocation by hundred of keen partisans of the different organisations competing. Scores of hands held copies of the test piece, the attentive following of bar after bar, and the quick, half-uttered, half-repressed breath of commendation or censure from listeners all over the great hall, bespoke eloquently enough the well informed musical minds of the larger section of the audience.</p>
        <p>It was nearly half-past seven when the waiting crowds in the ball wele rewarded by seeing the announcements posted in front of them. Cheers greeted the name of Black Dike as winners of the contest, these being renewed when it was seen that Rnshden Tempe ranee came second. The Perfection Soap Works came third, Lindley fourth, Linthwaite fifth, and Irwell Springs sixth. The " sensation" of the contest was the absence of Wingates Temperance from the list of names, an absence due more to musical slips incidental to such crucial ordeal RS the band was passing through than to any general deterioration in the tone of the combina— tion. For the rest, there were no surprises, as the self-constituted critics all over the hall confessed their inability to "spot the winner "for themselves with any degree of certainty.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <p>V.• g: „ . ?7s. /;`—</p>
