      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>HOW TO GET YOUR PENSION.</head>
        <p>HOW TO GET YOUR PENSION.</p>
        <p>[COPYRIGHT.]</p>
        <p>THER E are many eyes, less lustrous by age, but now brightened by new ' prospects, looking to January Ist, 1909. They can see a measure of State pro- vision for the remainder of their days. The ,deserving veterans of British toil never had such an outlook before. There is an assured New Year's gift for all who qualify by compliance with conditions which Parlia- ment has fixed. How to prove their right is doubtless perplexing many old folk and their friends. Acts and Departmental regulations are not generally read in the average home, and if they were enlighten- ment might not be ensured. It is the pur-.1 pose of this article to furnish information in as useful a form as possible. Mr. John Burns, M.P. President of the Local Government Board, has kindly supplied me with a copy of the Pensions Act and of the Regulations issued from his Department, and also forwarded unsolicited a Blue Book and oirculars, so that as I write I have before me all the official publications on the subject. Then to business. The Act applies to England, Ireland, Scotland, and Wales, and to the Scilly Isles, to men and women, married persons as well as single, widowers and widows. Here we are not concerned with the United Kingdom, but only so much of that Kingdom as is com- prised in our pension area, the area within the jurisdiction of the local Council. By the Act each Council, or a part of it, will be the Pension Committee, and the Pension Officers—the inquiry agents—are Inland Revenue officials set apart for this work by the Treasury. Take for granted that the Pension Committee is formed, and the Pen- sion Officer (no savour of Poor Law in this designation) is appointed and ready for duty at his office. A third force comes in— the Postal Department. Each Post Office in every city, town, and village is to be used to supply application forms and to pay the pension weekly—on every Friday. This is the machinery.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>Those Who Can and Those Who Cannot Claim.</head>
        <p>Those Who Can and Those Who Cannot Claim.</p>
        <p>Pension's are not for everybody, not even if the qualifying age of 70 is reached, but only for those who attain to certain stan- dards of thrift and other conditions stipu- lated by Parliament. A person cannot obtain a pension if he (and he throughout the Act also means she) has received Poor Law relief since January Ist. But there is no disqualification if that relief only takes the form of medical or surgical assistance (including food or comforts ordered by the medical officer), a dependent being main- tained by the Guardians in a lunatic asylum, infirmary, or hospital, or being buried at the public expense. It is r. fatal bar to a pension if a person has "habitually failed to work according to his ability, opportunity, and need for the main- tenance or benefit of himself and those legally dependent upon him." But this n. to.try test has one material modification. The foolish notion of "too old at forty" is, of course, not recognised, but the age of 60 is accepted as a time when human nature and difficulties of employment may seriously operate. Thus our legislators have enacted that if a claimant—being, of course, 70 or more—has since he was 60 Lot been able to obtain or retain employment he shall not thereby be disqualified if he has continuously for ten years up to at- taining the age of 60, by means of pay- men to to friendly, provident, or other societies, or trade unions, or 'other ap- proved steps, made such provision against old age, sickness, infirmity, or want or loss of employment as shall entitle him to any of the following benefits : (1) The right to receive during any period of sickness, not less than seven shillings and sixpence a week during the first twenty-six eeks (or alternatively not less than fifteen 'shillings a week for the first thirteen weeks) of the period, and not less than two shillings • a week for the remainder of the period; (2't The right to receive not less than five shil- lings a week during want or loss of employ- ment; &lt;3 The right to receive not less than three shillings a week for life either on becoming permanently incapacitated or upon the ttainment of any specified age not exceeding</p>
        <p>seventy: (4) The right to receive not lees than five shillings a week upon the attainment of any age not exceeding sixty-five until the attain- cf the age of seventy; (5) The right to receive not less than two shillings a week upon the attainment of any age not exceeding sixty until the attain- ment of the age of seventy; ._(6) The right to receive a capital enm of not less than fifty pounds upon the attainment of any specified age not exceeding seventy; (7) The possession, upon the attainment of the age of sixty, of accumulated savings, or of property purchased out of accumulated savings, to the value of not less than fifty pounds.</p>
        <p>Let it be noted that any one of these pro- visions, when made by the husband in the ea4e of a married couple "living together," will, as respects any right of the wife to a pension, be treated as provision made by the wife as well as by the husband. If a person has been in gaol under a sentence 4-)f imprisonment without the option of a fine, or of penal servitude, he cannot claim his pension till ten years after release. And magistrates convicting a person over 60 under the Inebriates Act may, if they think fit, order that he may not become a pen- sioner for any period not exceeding ten years.</p>
        <p>The Residence Qualineation. • Two more conditions of vital import: To claim you must be a British subject— by birth or by having your name on the ' Register of Naturalised Aliens—and have had your residence in the United Kingdom for twenty years up to the present time. "Residence " means actual presence in the country, uninterrupted otherwise than by temporary absences. And a person will be deemed only to have been "temporarily ab- sent" if before his absence he was living in the United Kingdom, and throughout the absence was employed in the service of the Crown as soldiers or sailors or other- wise engaged, or serving on board a vessel registered in the United Kingdom. With- out loss of title other persons may have been absent for a maximum of three months at a •stretch for twenty years, provided the total does not exceed eight years, and that the claimant has throughout had his home in the United Kingdom. Engineers and machine fitters who have worked abroad will do well carefully to consider this clause before filling up the application form. Filling Up the Form. The man or woman who desires a pension —a pension for life if you prove your title and your position remains unaltered—must first, of all think whether my of the dis- qualifications apply. Remember that knowingly to make false representations in- ' solves imprisonment. If you feel that you are entitled to a pension go to the most con- lenient Post Office—the office at which vein</p>
        <p>WOURI fiKe to receive your iveemy paymeni —and the Postmaster, on any date aftei about the end of this month, will at youi request supply without charge a form and, if need be, give assistance in helping to fill it up. But if you cannot write, or appre- hend other difficulty, the better plan will be to obtain the presence and active aid of a friend—indeed, one is necessary to witness your signature, or mark, and himself to sign. The following information will be re- quired ' 1. Full name of claimant. 2. Home addrees. 3. Occupation.</p>
        <p>4. Sex.</p>
        <p>5. Are you single or married, or a widower or widow? If a married woman or a widow, what was your maiden name? 6. Age. Date of birth. 7. Place where born. 8. Are you a British subject? 9. Have you lived in the United Kingdom for the whole of the laSt twenty years? At what place or places did you live during those years? 10. How much have you coming in per week in money? What are your other means of subsistence (if any)? Do you pay rent for the house or lodging in which you live?</p>
        <p>'The document from which these questions are taken is official. These are the ques- tions—stated as they are in the plainest language—which will appear on the appli- cation forms to be had later froln the Post Offices or the Pension Officers. Intending claimants should cut out the sample form given above and begin to pre- pare each answer. It will be a great help when the time comes for making the claim. You may have to undertake research to prove your age and to be able to give the requisite replies to other questions. The first question is simple. For the second furnish the full postal address. Nos. 3,4, and 5 present no difficulty. The next two, 6 and 7, are related—age, date of birth, and place where born. For the latter the Pension Officer desires if possible that the exact address be given. As to age, regard may be had to any of the following docu- ments to determine the matter:- 1. Certificate of birth.</p>
        <p>2. Certificate of baptism. 3. Cettifteate c.f service in any of the Forces of the Crown. •</p>
        <p>4. Certificate of tnembership of any friendly or provident soefety or trade union. • 5. Certificate of marriage.</p>
        <p>Where you cannot furnish any of these pan-erg—though in most eases one or other will be procurable—there is still another method. "Any other evidence which ap- pears sufficient for the purpose" may b 3 acazpted. Such evidence may be the tes- timony of some old relatives or. friends or •employer. Question 9is easy. , But in order to proverthat you havelived in the United Kingdom for 20 years look up your old rent books or other papers, state what you 7 employment, if any, has been during that time or obtain a note from employers, or be prepared to supply the names of two rezwas to whom if need be the Pension Officar can apply for confirmation. .Tha chief point is to make sure of things and to have too much rather than too littla evidenca.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>Question of Means.</head>
        <p>The Question of Means.</p>
        <p>This is important. The three questions •In paragraph 10 are meant to ascertain what you are receiving as personal income fuarn all sources to enable you to live. Supply this fully and have ready for pro- duction to the Pension Officer any bank book, or _any other documents relating to shares, loans, etc.., or any capital laid by and not invested. Sick pay or super- annuation must be stated. 'Prey come under the head of what you have "coming in per week in money." If the total ex- ceeds £3l 10. per year—or over 12s. 21d. a week—you are not eualified for a pen- sicn. In calculating income for married people living together in the same house "the means shall not in any ease be taken to be a less amount than half the total means of the couple." The appended table shows exactly the rate of pension ac- cording to income as governed by the</p>
        <p>Annual Weekly Weekly Income. Income. PeaQion. £ d. s. d. s. d. Not exceeding 21 0 0 ... 8 1 ... 5 0 23 12 6... 9 1 ... 4 0 99 26 5 0 ... 10 1 ... 30 99 2517 6 ... 11 1 ... 20 99 31 10 0 ... 12 2i ... 1. 0 Thus if your weekly income is not more than Ss. Id. a week you get the maximum pension of 55., and if your income exceeds l2s. 2,12 d. a week you cannot be placed ou the pension list..</p>
        <p>When the Claim is Ready.</p>
        <p>IVhen the application form is filled up hand it to the PoStmaster at the office where it is desired the pension, if granted, shall be payable. He will forward it—or advise you to send or take it—to the Pen- sion Officer, who, if he considers it neces- sary, may require you to supply him later with further information. Then he will report on your claim to the Pension Com- mittee, who will decide whether you are entitled, or not entitled to a pen- sion. If they think your claim should not be allowed or that fur- ther proof is required, they will notify you to this effect, and you may attend before the Committee and speak in • support of your 'application. If the Committee refuse to grant a pension you may appeal within seven days by means of a written state- ment (which may be sent without stamp) to the Local Government Board, whose de- cision is final. But assuming your ap- plication is granted the Committee will fix the amount of the pension and inform you by letter stating how much you will receive --whether one, two, three, four, or five shillings weekly, and from what date on- wards. Then will follow a book of pension coupons or orders from the Pension Officer, and upon presenting one of these coupons at the Post Office where the pension is pay- able each Friday the allowance will be paid. The law permits a claimant to make application four months before he is 70, so that if granted the pension will fall due immediately the qualifying age is reached. The pension is subject to revision from time to time according to your altered cir- cumstances, and may be increased, diminished, or withdrawn should occasion require.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCH.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON SECONDARY SCHOOL V. GREAT SAN!CRY Played at Padgate on Saturday. The visitors pressed and N. Ray scored. Half-time : Great Sankey, one goal ; Warrington Secondary -chool, nil. On restart- ing, both ends were visited in tarn and Rice scored for the locals. Just on time N. Ray received a pass from the left and scored with a fast drive. Final : Great Sankey, two goals; Secondary school, one goal. LIVERPOOL AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. WARRINGTON ALBION'S v. PRESCOT ATIILETIC.</p>
        <p>The opening game in the Association code in Warrington attracted a large crowd to Liverpool-road on Siturday. The first half was very hotly contested and the interval arrived without a score. In the second half the game was again of a give-and-take nature. The home side were the first to penetrate the defence, Gregson scoring with a fine shot, but Prescot rallied and Ashcroft equalize], the result being a draw of one goal each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>DAYLIGHT SAYING BILL.</head>
        <p>THE DAYLIGHT SAYING BILL.</p>
        <p>MR. HOLMAN KINGDON'S VIEWS.</p>
        <p>The longest day of the year has come and gone ; the evenings are already rapidly getting shorter, and soon the daylight will no longer last until we get home after work is over, and dan out flannels for that game of tennis, cricket practice, or an hour's run on the cycle, or in the motor car, as our tastes and circumstances may prescribe. How often is the remark made at such times, " What a pity it gets dark so soon !" or" It's too late to do that now as it gets dark so early !" Let us think for a moment. Why does it get dark, and what is time? As everybody kuows, our earth takes a certain period in which to make one revolution round the stin,which, for convenience, we term a year, and the result is the seasons following in rotation Sping, Summer, Autumn, Winter ; also, our earth revolves on its own axis every year approximately three hundred and sixty-five times, giving us this number of smaller periods called days, each of which we divide into twenty-four hours, some of which are light and some dark according to whether the particu- lar spot where we live at that particular time is turned toward the light-giving sun or away from it. But does everybody realize the importance of light to health and even to life itself? People who live in bright, sunny places are, as a rule, more cheerful, happier, of a more sanguine temperament, and less easily depressed, than those whose surroundings are not so favourable. Science has proved that sunlight is often an effective remedy against disease. There are certain forms of skin affections which have only yielded to that form of artificial light which is the nearest approach to actual sunlight that man can devise. All nature depends on sunlight, those wonderful changes and alternations whose numbers are incalculable and which surround us on all sides. Even the blind realize the difference between daylight and darkness, and are cheered by the former and depressed by the latter. Time, moreover, is purely relative, and has generally been nettled by calling the moment when the sun is at its highest, at any particular place, mid-day at that place; but, inasmuch as the sun cannot be in two places at once, it follows that, to be accurate, no two places can have precisely the same time. Manchester would be a few seconds ahead of Liverpool, and both a few minutes later than London. The reason why this is not so is because such an arrangement would render life impracticable, and all engagements—even those of a matrimonial nature —would be impossible. To take a single example; it would require a mathe- matical calculation to find out when a train staited from any place and when it reached anywhere else. For the purpose of convenience, therefore, it is agreed that within certain degrees of longtitude time shall be uniform, and thus we get Eastern European, Mid- European, and Western European time, each differing from the other by about an hour, and our even more familiar expression" Greenwich time." Most people know that when you go to Ireland you have to alter your watch by about twenty-five minutes, because Irish time is not the same as English. Time, therefore, is not only relative, but also purely arbitrary, and should be arranged to give the greatest benefit to the greatest number. To put his in another way, time should be arranged so that, having regard to the working day— that is the period between when we get up and when we go to bed - there should be during this period as much light as possible and as little darkness. During the winter months, little or nothing can be done, because we cannot alter either the number of dark hours or the number of light ones, and, provided we are up .and about when the light commences and when it ends for each day, no human agency can effect any improvement. But in the summer months how different is the case. Here, except, perhaps, for the second half of June, it is dark long before we go to bed, and yet it is broad daylight many hours before the world at large awakes. Now, by taking off some of the wasted hours of daylight in the eruly morning in spring, summer, and autumn, and adding them t the end of the day, the number of hours of daylight during the working day would be largely increased. Here is an improvement which is not only possible but practicable. How is it to be done ? It is pro- posed to put the clock forward one hour on a certain Sunday in April every year, and to put it back again by an equal amount on a certain Sunday in the October following, by which means the above result would, to a very considerable extent, be achieved. With this object in view a Bill, called, in the happiest manner' " The Daylight Saving Bill," has been introduced into the House of Commons, and, after approval by a Select Committee, who sat for a number of days taking evidence on the subject, has been read a second time.</p>
        <p>All sections and all grades of society, with the natural exception of those companies and Corpora- tions engaged in the manufacture of artificial illu- minants, and some portions of the Stock Exchange and Cotton Trade, who fear that the facility of tele- graphic communication with America might be affected, have expressed themselves in favour of the Bill ; and even the Railway Companies and the Post Office, who were, at first, inclined to oppose it owing to difficulties with Continental Time Tables and Foreign Mails, are prepared to admit that such difficulties are not insuperable. No legislation was ever introduced without some attendant disadvant- ages. Let us exercise our sense of proportion and see how enormously the advantages of the proposal outweigh the latter. Mr, William Willett, of London, to whom the ,credit of the suggestion is due, assumes that the cost of artificial light for, each unit of the population averages one-tenth of a penny per head per hour. , Under the proposed Bill, the number of hout s during ' which the cost of artificial light would be saved would be approximately one hundred and fifty per annum. This would mean a minimum net annual saving of two million sterling per annum, so that fourth-fifths of the interest on one hundred million pounds sterling of the National Debt could be saved by the people of Great Britain and Ireland at the cost of moving ths hands of the clock once in the Spring and once again in the Autumn.</p>
        <p>Some people seem to think that, in return for these advantages, we shall have less time in which to sleep. Such is not the case, but, on the contrary, we may sleep better; for sixty minutes more daylight in our waking hours will leave sixty minutes more darkness for our sleeping hours, and by those who do not sleep too well the advantage of not being wakened so early in the morning by the daylight, as they some- times are, will not be be unappreciated. Over two hundred members of the Lords and Commons have pledged themselves in support of the Bill, and a number of Chambers of Commerce through- out the country, including those of Manchester, Liverpool. and Warrington, have done the same. The object of this article is to try and bring home to the commercial and industrial community that it is they who are going to be the gainers more than any other section of the public by , this Bill going on the Statute Book, and to ask them to try and use their influence amongst their friends , and connections to help it to do so. It won't mean that you have to get up any earlier in the morning, but it will mean that you have more daylight and sunshine for your play when work is over. Just think for one minute. When a man is twenty-five years old he will have gained a whole year of daylight ; at fifty, two years, and at seventy- five, three years. Remember, too, that more daylight means less strain to eyes, nerve, and brain. Can such advantages possibly be over-estimated, and when, remember, they can be had, not only with- out price, but at an actual saving in our yearly expen- diture?</p>
        <p>One of the arguments advanced against the Bill is that the cows of the cwntry will not be persuaded to. give their milk an hour earlier thari usual. Perhaps, under all the circumstances, we might take the risk of this.</p>
        <p>FIOLIIAN KINGDON. (la the" Dawn.")</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>REVISION COURTS.-NEWTON</head>
        <p>REVISION COURTS.-NEWTON</p>
        <p>Mr. Charles Henry Marriott Wharton, Barrister-at- Law, has arranged the following dates and places for his annual revision of the voting lists for Newton Division :—September 21st, at St. llelens sad Rainhill ; Penketh and Warrington ; 23rd, at Newton Town Hall, at 10.30 for Burtonwood, Myddleton, Arbury, Southsvorth-with-Croft, and Winwick-with-Hulme ; at 10 a.m. and 3 and 6 p.m. for the five wards comprising Earlestovvn and Newton ; 24, Ashton ; 25, Billinge and Holborn° ; 26, Haydock. The respective agents of the political parties, Councillor W. Forshaw, 93, Hardshaw-street, St. Helens, and Labour Club, Earles- town, and Mr, J. H. Bottomley, Conservative Office, Newton, will supply any information to claimants.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday September 12, 1908. Weak and Gloomy.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday September 12, 1908. Weak and Gloomy.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Choral Union for Stockton Heath.. i Men, victims of Acute Debility. At a well attended meeting held in the Wesleyan School, Stockton Heath, under the presidency of Mr. --- J. Quine, it was decided to form a mixed choir with Find New Vitality and Sound Nerves the title "The Stockton Heath Choral Union." The I organization will be un-sectarian and already a in Dr. Williams' Pink Pills. membership of between fifty and sixty has been i -- obtained. It is hoped to increase the number to "It was only due to the consideration and sympathy eighty. All who join will be required to pay an of my employers that I was not dismissed as incom- entrance fee of ls. and a weekly contribution of 2d. potent," remarked Mr. GEORGE LTAIIGIITON, of 19, St, and 3d. for ladies and gentlemen respectively. Mr. James'-road, London, SE., to a journalist, recently. Harry Berrey has been appointed conductor and Mr. The explanation which followed this remark quickly F. E. Penketh is the secretary. ! disclosed that Mr. Houghton had been a victim of Increased Cost of Milk. 1 Acute Debility, the trouble that robs so many men of all energy and ability for work ; and this evil works so insidiously that often the victim does not realize At a well-attended meeting of local farmers, held at the Blue Bell Inn, Warrington, on Wednesday the cause. evening, it was decided to form a branch of the i Dr. Williams' Pink Pills cured Mr. Houghton, as Cheshire Milk Producers' Association. A resolution they have cured many more debilitated nervous men, was passed in favour of increasing the price of milk by making Rich, Good Blood that gives men owing to the increased ccst of 'production, the price., strength and invigorates their muscles old nerves. recommended for winter being 101 per gallon whole- 1 "For a long period," sale and 4d. per quart retail, and for summer Bd. said Mr. Houghton per gallon and 3d. per quart. A petition was signed —quite eighteen appealing to co-operative societies to raise the retail months—l did not price instead of undercatting other retailers. Mr. know, what it was Sadler, the secretary of the Association, said many to be free from a farmers last winter produced milk at an actual loss, dull, heavy feeling PUBLIC BENEFIT.—UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST of faintness. A suc- BOOTS IN LINCASHIRE.—Adn. tcession of sickening Mr. F. J. Lord. msations b rough t Holy Trinity" Parish Magazine" says : —We dare ly health down to say the members and friends of St. Luke's Church have low ebb, and grad- noticed how h:.antifully the churchyard has been dry reduced me to planted with trees and shrubs and flowers during the condition of weak- past two years, adding very much to the appearance nis and lethargy. of the church. Perhaps it is not so well known that cannot adequately we are indebted far all this to the kindness of Mr. F. indicate the gloom J. Lord, who up to very recently used to live in that surrounded me. Winmarleigh-street, and who, notwithstanding the I could take no interest fact that he attended a neighbouring church, watched in pastimes, but avoided over St. Luke's Churchyard with a most kindly the society of others and beneficence. Mr. Lord has within the last few days left the town for the South of England, and we feel it is only right that we should at last make some acknow- ledgment of the gratitude felt by the clergy, wardens, and friends of St. Luke's Church for the kindness which caused him unsolicited to do so much for the church.</p>
        <p>dragged out a miserable existence. " often, in the street, I would become so giddy that I would reel along like a drunken man—a hot flush, a sensation as though all my blood was rushing to the head, and then a cold sweat and great beads of perspiration. In time I settled down to my depression and misery in the belief, that for ins there was no relief.</p>
        <p>CLEARANCE SALE OF CYCLES, big. reductions.— GARLAND,SI-55,Bridge st.,Warrulgton.—Advt. Death of Mr. F. K. Barnes. The death is announced of Mr. F. Kynaston Barnes (father of the Rev. J. S. Barnes, curate of Daresbury), which took place at Gwynfa, Llandriadrod Wells, the residence of his daughter and son-in-law. The deceased gentleman, who was in his 81st year, was at one time a distinguished Admiralty officer. In 1864 he was appointed assistant constructor of the navy, Sir Edward E. J. Reed, K.C.8.. being the chief con- structor at that time. In 1870 he was appointed a member of the Council of Construction at the Admiralty, and in 1872 surveyor (latterly director) of dockyards, which post he held until his retirement from the services in March, 1886. He was hon. vice- president of the Institution of Naval Architects, and was the author of several papers on naval matters,and he was stated by the late Sir William Pearce, M.P., "to be the ablest designer of ships" of his time. TRAVELLING TRUNKS, ETC., compare our quality and prices. Dress Baskets from GARLAND, 51, Bridge et., Warrington.—Advt. P.S.A. Meetings. A meeting of the Wycliffe P.S.A. Society was held on Sunday and was well attended. Ur. David Owen, who vesided, spoke upon "The nearness of God," and Mr. Charles Ackerley, of Lymm, upon " Sowing and reaping, or foolish deception," Mr. Diane also took part in the meeting. Miss Bancroft sang sweetly "There is a green hill " and "Nearer, my God.' Five new members joined (making a total of 477) and were heartily welcomed. The Secretary moved a vote of sympathy with Mr. Royle whose wife had died suddenly, and it was carried in silence. The Seers. tary referred to the case of Daisy Lord, who some time ago killed her child, and was sentenced to death, but which had been commuted to penal servitude for life. The country was being moved on her behalf for the purpose of further mitigating the sentence. A. petition with that end in view was largely signed in the vestibule and will be again submitted for the signatures to-morrow.—The first meeting of a new half-year was held at Buckley-street on Sunday, when there was a large attendance. Mr. T. Dale presided and delivered a very interesting address. The lesson was read by Ur. J. Urmston. Miss B. Hayes's fine soprano voice was heard to advantage in severalsol and Mr. F. Moon (bass, also rendered several solos. Mr. W. Shaw (president) welcomed several new members. Miss .d. Plinston was the accompanist. PUBLIC BENEFIT UNDER TAB CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS n LANCASHIRE.—AdVt. Warrington Women's Circle. The attendance at the first gathering on Sunday afternoon augurs well for the future usefulness of the Circle. A large meeting was presided over by Mrs. Ben Few, who in a few well-chosen words welcomed Mrs. James Dodds in the Lame of the members. She said that women's work at Golborne-street was an experiment, but no work undertaken with God could be looked upon in the nature of an experiment. Mrs. Simpson offered prayer, and Miss L. Plinston rendered the solos "Sun of my Soul" and -4' Nearer my God to Thee." -The President gave a beautiful and practical address on " The Ministry of Little Things." She liked the term "Circle " for given reasons, and wanted to be associated with all that was good in Warrington. She wished the members to think of her more as a fellow-worker ihan as president. It is an easy thing to give money for those who have it, but but another thing to be thoughtful, kind, sweet and true every day. God gives to every woman, He puts into this world a message of her own to give to the world, and if one of us fails to do her part the world is the poorer for lack of that message. The gracious sympathy of Mrs. Dodds and her evident sincere desire that the Circle should become a power for good in Warrington won the hearts of all present—Mrs. Robert Letheren outlined the idea and aim of the Circle, and the reasons for calling the union a " Circle." Various ways of helping were suggested, and the majority of those present registered their names as regular members. .A Girls Choir has been formed for the meetings An offering was taken to pay for hymn books, and it was announced that Mrs. C. E. Puler had given 1.2 towards this purchase. TEA SERVICES, Dinner Services, Toilet Ware, Large stock, all prices.—J. S. GARLAND, Iron- monger, Bridge st.,Warrington.—Advt. Tea and Concert at Legh-street. The quarterly tea and conceit in connection with the above place of worship was held on Thursday and was provided by the married men of the church. The tea, which was a very enjoyable one,was well attended after which the concert was proceeded with under the presidency of Mr. J. Starkey, who in the course of his remarks spoke of the good done by these meetings in getting the friends together, and encouraged them to continue the good work. The first item was a chorus entitled " Aldiborontiphoscophornio," the title of which was as meimingless to the audience as the words which followed and caused much amuse- ment. Mr. W. Watson (tenor) was very successful in the songs "The Romany Lass ' and "Come into the garden Maud." Mr. George Gough was well received in " The Lowland Sea " and " To-morrow will be Friday" each being much enjoyed. The cornet solos " Faraiare Polka" and "Home sweet home " (with variations) were splendidly executed by Mr. Hodgkinson who proved himself to be an artiste of no mean ability. The , song and chorus "Happy married life" sung by Mr. J. Phillips caused much fun, particularly in the last verse where he advises all single men to get married quickly. Two Lancashire recitations were amusingly rendered by Mr. J. Shaw. The quartettes "Simple Simon" and "A Catastrophe" sung by Messrs. Atty, Philips, Spruce and Gough, was much enjoyed. The song the "Skippers of St. Ives" was well sung by Mr. Spruce, the concert party taking up the chorous lustily. The trio " A Little Farm well Tilled," Messrs. Parkes, ,Phillips and Spruce, provoked much laughter, while the duet "Two of the unemployed" taken in character by Messrs. Phillips and Spruce fairly carried away the audience, the grotesque dance at the end being exceedingly funny, An amusing duet was rendered by Messrs. Ilimsworth and Packer, entitled " The Cobbler and the Crow." The closing item was a humorous dialogue " D Diaculam,' the parts of which were well sustained by Mr. A. Whittle as the doctor. Mr. Dodd as Phil, his assistant; and Mr. Parkes as Sympson, the intended partner; and' Mr. Himsworth as the policeman. The accompaniments were in the hands of Mr. Joseph H. Foster, who added much to the success of the evening by the way he carried out his duties.—The Rev. J. B. Buglass proposed a vote of thanks to all the performers, after which the meet- ing was closed by singiag the Doxology.</p>
        <p>" In a moment of inspiration (I call it nothing else) I decided to try what Dr. Williams' Pink Pills would do in a stubborn and deep-rooted case like mine. I bought a bax and took the Pills as directed. It was some days before I noticed any change at all, the first sign being the cleansing of my tongue. Then came a feeling of brightness. '['he dull, heavy languor vanished, and I soon realised that I was freed from my old enemy. "I continued with the Pills, and before very long realised that I was indeed cured. You see me now in a condition of buoyancy such as I have not felt for many a month. This is proof of the sterling worth of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills." Men need Red, Rich Blood in their veins to fit them for the day's work, and that is just what Dr. Williams' Pink Pills supply. They give strength and steady nerves to weak men—they make New, Good Blood. These Pills have cured General Weakness, Nervous Debility, Palpitations, Headaches, Loss of Vital force, Indigestion, Spinal Weakness, the Effects of Over- work and Excesses of all kinds, Rheumatism, and Paralysis. Sold at shops, hut see that you get the genuine Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People (full name seven words on the wrapper). If in doubt send direct to Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, 40, Holborn Viaduct, London, EC., enclosing 28. 9d. for one box, or 13s. 9d. for six, post free.</p>
