      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>BAND OF HOPE PROGRESS.</head>
        <p>BAND OF HOPE PROGRESS.</p>
        <p>Lancashire and Cheshire Work.</p>
        <p>The quarterly meetings of the General Committee of the Lancashire and Cheshire Band of Hope Union and Temperance Union held at Winsford on Saturday revealed the progress which the society is making in temperance work in the two counties. Mr. Herbert Knott, of Wilmslow, presided at the afternoon business meeting, which was attended by representatives from 68 towns and districts. The busi- ness included the appointment of Mr. Peter Taylor, lately of Southport, as campaign and district delegate, and also the introduction of the Misses Donaghy and Phenix, who are organising a three counties bazaar for the autumn of 1909 in aid of the work carried on by the Union in the day and evening schools. Some time was devoted to the consideration of arrangements for a rally of county workers in Manchester in October, at which representatives of over 1,000 bands of hope from the villages and towns of Lancashire, Cheshire, and North-west Derbyshire will be present. The object of the gathering is to begin a great effort to double the membership by a systematic canvass of all homes in January next, and to raise a fund equal to Is. per mem- ber, or in all £12,500. The Todmorden District Independent Order of Rechabites were received into the Association, bringing up the membership of 85 town and district organisations with 2,510 bands of hope and juvenile temperance societies and over 275,000 members. Before the meeting closed returns were presented showing that since January Ist lectures in connection with the Union had been given in 554 schools to 1,667 teachers and 45,491 elder scholars. Of these 32,994 had written essay reports in competition for certificates of merit awarded by the Union, and 19,218 had been successful in obtaining awards. At the subsequent public conference, over which Mr. George Booth (president of the Winsford 8.H.U.) pre- sided, Mr. W. Chandos Wilson (general secretary of the Counties Union) opened a discussion on " The Band of Hope forward movement; our opportunity," and a resolution was passed pledging the Conference to do its utmost to realise the forward movement in its effort to increase the membership and provide the shilling per member.</p>
        <p>_ Dr. Leak (Winsford) presided over a large public meeting in the evening, and was supported by Mr. Chandos Wilson, Councillor W. G. Tormend (Mayor of Middleton), and Mr. E. C. Bainbridge (Ashton-under- Lyne), all of whom gave addresses, in which stress was laid upon the value of child life arid the important place which the children occupy in the public mind at the present day. During the evening selections were rendered by the Manchester District Band of Hope Choral Union.</p>
        <p>, GOLF.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The sixth monthly competition for the captain's prize resulted in a win for Dr. Peacocke in the first division, and for B. S. Newcombe in the second. P. W. Wareing took the first sweepstake, and the second was divided between J. Hepherd and H. Kirkconnell. Scores :</p>
