        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>ISIICS. ZOOM: vAughters are a great anxiety." Mrs. Tinison: "how so, Mrs. Nooch?" Mrs. Nooch: "if you don't dress them handsomely they are not attractive; and if you do dress them hand- somely men are afraid to flurry them." A mar, with a bow on her hat doesn't feel quite happy without she also has a beau on her arm. MAtimn: " Miss Oldengay says she was 22 her last birthday." Alice; " Her last? Oh, yes; I see. She stopped having them about 18 years ago." TRAGEDIAN "I should think you would make a bit in Shakespearian roles." Hainakter: "No; I tried 'Hamlet' once. Thera were some hits made, but different members of the audience were respon• ocole for them." ‘v RAM" TT 11.1.1 E " Vats our golf champion:. New Recruit : " Wot's his record ?" Weary Willie : "He's stole ten clubs, four balls, two caps and six medals in four weeks."</p>
        <p>" Wniii I was away, Kitty, you saved some house money, of course?" " Yes. Ifarry, I saved a lot; but I spent it all on ice cream." " 3lr boy Jimmie, aged nine, is a corker in psycholegv—and it's only his second term at it too." " Indeed!" "Yes. The other day he said he was certain that the higher moral influence Ind nothing to do with my being a good citizen." "Then how thd lie account for it?" "lie said I was afraid of the police." "I MUST admit," said the mannish girl, "that I'm very fond of men's clothes. You don't like them, do you ?" "Yes. I do," replied the girly girl, frankly, " when there's a man in them."</p>
        <p>FATHER FLARITY called on Pat Murphy and asked him for two dollars to help buy coal for the church; but Patrick said he had no money. " Begorra, Mike, said Pat to a friend after the priest left, did he think I was fool enough to b'lave he wanted money to buy coal to heate the church wid when I knows its heated be st ham° ?"</p>
        <p>PIrTSBURG MAN: " What would you folks do if a mob of rioters should come charging down Broad- way ?" New Yorker (busily): "Start spaceman to shooting at a dog." HUSBAND: "Everything in this house is out of place. Ben having an earthquake?" Wife: "I've been putting things iu order.' , SUMMER BOARDER: "I hear that there used to be a great deal of fever and ague around here." Host: " Yes ; but none here now, not a bit. We're all got acclimated."</p>
        <p>FAIR CUSTOMER : "Are you sure this sticky paper will catch flies?" Dealer: "Yen, indeed, mum. I sold half a dozen sheets to a boarding-house keeper yesterday, and in two hours she caught enough for a huckleberry pie." MANAGER: Is your new play an American - drama?" Writer: "Thoroughly American. Nearly all the characters talk with a broizile."</p>
        <p>WU% (midnight): " Ooo! Woo! Wake upl There's a man trying to get in." Husband (sleepily): "Nonsense! Uri to sleep." Wife (as a last; resort): Maybe he's got a bill?" husband: "Whoop? Where's my gun ?" FRIEND: "Do you try to make yourself a daily and hourly necessity to your husband, as I advised ?" Bride: "Yes, indeed. I keep everything in its place. so he can never find a thing without my help." GURST "What do you think of this idea of min- pulsory arbitration?" Waiter: "Don't keer within' 'bout, dat, boss. Wot we tins want is compulserry tipMn'," Tor intoxicated gentleman made his way un- steadily into Wun Lung's Laundry, and delivered a huge bundle of linen with the remark: " Zhere, ol' man I Un'stan' you' ki'lin' mishnaries. All ri'. Corns 'roun' my houshe. My muzzer-in-law use' be taisle- na-y." Ctrttri (to employer): "What, shall I mark that new lot of black silk ?" Employer: " Mark the sell- ing price at :idol. a yard." Clerk: "But it only cost Idol. a yard." Employer: "I don't care what it Cost. lam selling off regardless of cost," " MY parents may come between us," she faltered. "If they do," he exclaimed, hotly, "they must he pretty small." And he pressed her still closer to his manly breast. A PEROXIDE Juno interviewed a San Francisco fortune-teller on the usual subject. • " Lady," said the seer; "you will visit foreign lands and the courts of kings and queens. You will conquer all rivals and marry the man of your choice; he will be tall, dark and distinguished looking." "Young?" 'Yes, and very rich." The fair visitor rose and in her eagerness grasped the fortune - teller by both shoulders. "How, oh, how shall I get rid of my pro sent husband ?"</p>
        <p>" WILLIAM, go up to my room. Back of my ward- robe there are " "Cigars, sir?" "Yes. How did you find them?" "Oh, very good, indeed, sir !" .AN Atchison woman of forty finds by reading over back diaries that her heart has been broken just, 17 times, and that she longed for the cold tomb twice • year during her hammock days. " BIMINI/A, if my salary were raised we could b. married to-morrow." "All right, Augustus. I'll write your employer an anonymous letter and tell him we both think he is a mean old thing." " LIMY say Biddleson once saved hie wife from a watery grave, before they were married." "You don't know the Biddlesons very well, do you ?" "Why do you ask?" " Because you thought it necessary to add that the rescue was effected before their marriage." HARDUPP "I'm very sorry, but I can't pay you to-day. You see, the grocery man has just been here, and—" Butcher (interrupting): "Yes, I just met him, and he said you put him off because you had to pay me. So here's the bill.", AMATEUR Amor.: "What character am I to talcs in the play?" Manager: "You aro to personate the villain." Amateur Actor: " How do I make up?" Manager: "You don't have to, that's why you were chosen for the part." "I MIGHT have known better than to trust my money to that broker." "Why so? Are appearances against him?" 'No, confound him; It's his disappearance." "You must know Jenkins, surely," said Mr. Stodge "he's a sandy-haired man; had smallpox when a lad, and he's deeply pitted." "Anyone who's had smallpox," I said, "deserves to be deeply pitied." Stodge gazed at me in indignant surprise. "De- serves to be deeply pitted," he echoed; "why, hang it all, you seem to blame the man for his misfor- tune." "Oh, but you don't understand me," I re- joined with a smile, "what I meant was—" " Well, it's a funny thing tisk say," he muttered. "I meant it to be funny," said-I. "It seems hardly in good taste to me," rematked Stodge, who is a suc- cessful business man and a church member, "to make fun of a man's appearance." "But I made no fun of his appearance; you know I would not do such a thing." "Oh, excuse me, you certainly did," retorted Stodge. " I said Jenkins had had the smallpox, and you said he deserved to have it. The man never did you any harm, and yet you want to see him and his family stricken by a fell disease." "Quite so," I shouted savagely. "I want all his re- lations and friends to be swept off the earth, his house raised to the ground and sown with salt. I wish his.husiness to go to ruin, and his name to be lost for evermore." Then I went away. The next time 1 make a joke I shall try it on the dog; if he doesn't laughs, I shall know it is too subtle for s practical man like &amp;oder+.</p>
