      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>Wirrington Lose at Leigh.</head>
        <p>Wirrington Lose at Leigh.</p>
        <p>Warrington and Leigh maintained their practice of opening the season together. On Saturday it was Leigh's ttrn to be at home and they just got the better of their visitors in the hard-fought match. R. Bate ef Warrington made his first appearance on the Leigh side, while Warrington were short of their Welsh forwards. A fine day favoured the match and there was a gate of quite 5,000, several hundreds of whom came from Warrington. The teams were composed as follows Leigh—E. Clarkson, back ' • S. Johnson, T. H. Johnson, H. Bennet, and R. H. Neville, three-quarter backs; S. Whittaker and R. I3ate,half-backs; P. O'Neill, A. Lee, H. Woods, W. Winstanley, W. Ashton, and E. Jones, forwards. Warrington—Tilley, back ; Fish, Dickenson, Isher- wood, and Taylor, three-quarter backs ; Brooks and 'Lloyd, half backs ; P. Boardman A. 13oardman, Belton, I Harmer, Swift and Jolley, forwards. Referee, Mr. J. H. Smith, Whines ; touch judges, Messrs. A. E. Gregson, Salford, and W. Gregory,Runcorn. Belton set the ball in motion against a good breeze, and Leigh were soon on the attack as the result of smart punting. Lilley was unable to clear, and Leigh scrimmaged in the Warriugton quarter. The homesters got possession and Whittaker kicked across to T. Johnson who dribbled over with the first try of the I season near the corner. Clarkson missed the goal. Warrington continued to be pressed, and after a penalty shot against them had gone astray. Swift made a great save when the Warrington goal seemed certain to fall. By Natty of a change Fish accepted from Lloyd, and the Warrington men for the first tire° became the aggressors. Too premature work lost the advantage, for the free kick gave the Leigh men a footing in Warrington territory, and though Fish regained some of the lost ground 'when he ran to halfway before being bundled 1 into touch by Clarkson and S. Johnson. Lloyd saved well at a critical moment. Weak play by Dickinson gave Leigh another try, T. Johnson being again the scorer. The home men, imbued by their early successes, attacked in great style, but Swift proved a stumbling block. But for a great save by Tilley, a mistake of Isherwood's would have given Leigh a further lead. A series of free kicks gave Warrington the desired relief,and Fish further improved matters by a great run and kick. Lloyd made a dribble three-parts the length of the field, Neville saving just in time. At last Warrington's chance came when Lloyd got cleverly away from a pack and then gave to Brooks, who ran more than half the length of the field and scored a try under the posts. Fish landed a goal, and with only a point in arrears Warrington buckled to their work in grim earnest. Leigh 7( ere awarded a free kick in a good position, but S. Johnson made n poor attempt. Half-time : Leigh, six points ; Warriagton, five points. With only one point behind Warrington warmed to their work, and a hasty visit ,a as paid to the Leigh line, Clarkson being repeatedly called upon to save. The forwards were repeatedly in possession, and Lloyd and Brooks frequently opened out the game, but the three- quarter line was disjointed, and many opportunities to take the lead were allowed to slide. By easy stages the Leigh men got to the ‘Varrington end, and for some time the "Wirepullers' " goal was imperilled, but Fig:, ever alert, secured near the line,and racing along touch he soon placed a big gap between himself and several of his pursuers. Reaching half-way he turned to the open and baffled several of the opposition, and got to within a few feet of the line, where he made an effort to leap for the try, but in this he was not successful, for an opponent secured a firm grip of his ankle and the brilliant effort came to naught. Warrington continued to hammer auay at the Leigh line, and Tilley came very near doing the trick when he took a huge drop at goal, the ball just travelling outside by inches. Neville next ran well for the home side, and he certinly appeared likely to score until Isher- wood appeared on the scene and successfully tackled the Leigh three-querter, Warrington again got ' dangerous, but all their efforts to cross were futile. Tilley anl Taylor tried to drop goals without success, whilst another effort by Fish was checked at its most dangerous stage. Final : Leigh, two tries (six points); Warrington, one goal one try ((Ire points.)</p>
      </div>
