      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. Authors of letters intended for publication rauat in all cases send their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith. [GAS WORKER.—We will try to fiad room fur your letter nest week.—ED."-Ex."l</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>" Daisy Lord."</head>
        <p>" Daisy Lord."</p>
        <p>Sir,—"l am instructed by my branch of the I.L.P; to farward to the local Press, the Member for Warrington, and the Home secretary, copies of the following resolu- tion which was passed unanimously at a meeting held on the 7th instant "The members of the Warrington Branch of the Indspendent Labour Party strongly protest against the further detention of Daisy Lord, condemned to a life sentence for causing the death of her illegitimate child under circumstances of great pathos and loneliness. We sincerely believe that this severe penalty is both inhuman and unjust, and request that she may soon be released." CHARLES WALKER (secretary). -- Councillor -Stott's Journay.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l shall esteem it a favour if you will permit me, through your columns, to thank all my numerous friends in Warrington and district for their kind expres- sions of appreciation of my services, and for the I splendid testimonial which I received and which was ' .subscribed to by such a lame number. I trust to be able to devote the money received towards providing a new home in New Zealand, so that I may have a permanent reminder—if that were neces- sary—of the generosity of the friends I have left behind. The heat here is very intense, and somewhat trying to an Englishman. We have a splendid company on board, amongst which are some very good instrumentalists, who are, at all times, willing to entertain the passengers. Sports and concerts are held quite frequently to entertain us. We have not, up to the present, had any Unemployed or Socialist demonstrations, although, of course, there b a large number of us unemployed. My temporary address is—care of Mr. J. Arnold, Lower Hut Settlement, near Wellington, N.Z.—YoursA"c. FRED srorr.</p>
        <p>s.s., Surrny, Madiera, August 29, 1908.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>Daylight Saving Bill.</head>
        <p>Daylight Saving Bill.</p>
        <p>- - Sir,—ln his reply to my letter on this subject it appears to Ise the contention of the President of the Chamber of Commerce that the end justifies the means. He may have my name and address from the Editor if he chooses, but it would be a perfect farce to enter into a discussion of the subject with him after he has succeeded in persuading the Chamber unanimously to pass a resolution in favour of the measure as it stands. I am sir,— Yours Faithfully, A FELLOW ob"rim ASTRONOMICAL SOCIETY. 7th September, 19OS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Mr. Holman Kingdon's able letter about the "Daylight Saving Bill "shows all the good things this] wonderful Bill will bring about—for the landlord. No doubt it would be a good thing—under the Single tax; but until we have solved this "first thing first" all the benefit from it will go the same way as all the inventions and improvements of the last few centuries have gone, into the pockets of the landlord, and all the quicker since such a Bill would make the thing compulsory to all ! I will just explain a bit more. The toll over the Waterloo Bridge amounted to d. each way, so that the workingman who had to use it twice a day paid Gd. par week in toll. The London County Council, eager to help the working classes, bought off this toll with £500,000 of the rate- payers' money. Effect : within a few weeks all rents in the neighbourhood rose exactly Gd. per week. The ratepayer had involuntarily for ever made a present of half a million pounds to the landlord ! Our good hearted late Premier Sir Henry Campbell-Bannerruau found the wages in Woolwich Arsenal strikingly bad and raised them all round. Effect : all rents in \Woolwich rose by exactly the same amount! The fight against Land Monopoly is inter- national, and it is only a shrewd move of the landed proprietor class to keep the people's mind fro n year's end to year's end busy with palliatives and quack remedies, so that they do not see while tinkering along the one great cause and the one sovereign remedy the appropriation by State and community of the rental value made by State and community and the abolition of all taxes and rates, or as Henry George expressed :- 44 We would simply take for the community what belongs to the community—the value that attaches to land by the growth of the community ; leave sacred to the individual all that belongs to the individual--I have the honour, Sir, to remain, yours truly, OCKEL.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>PENKETIL</head>
        <p>PENKETIL</p>
        <p>LOCAL NVEDDLNG.-DAVIES-ATUERTON.</p>
        <p>The wedding of Mr. Walter Davies and Miss Eva Annie Schofield Atherton, eldest daughter of Captain Sam Atherton Station-road, Penketh, took place at Sankey Church on Saturday at half-past two o'clock. The bride, who was given away by her father, wore a pretty ivory white cashmere dress trimmed with Chantilly lace, a Brussels net veil surmounted by a coronet of orange blossoms. The wedding service, which was fully choral, was performed by the Rev. J. R. Jones, M.A. Miss Standish ofnciated at the organ. Mr. James Dodds, whose coachman the bridegroom is, sent his brougham for the use of the happy couple. Miss Bella Davies was bridesmaid and Mr. Peter Davies beat man. After the wedding service was over a recep- tion was held in the Infants' School when over a hundred guests were present. Hr. J. J. Foden acted as M.C. for dancing in the evening. The following is a list of the presents : Bride to bridegroom, set of gold studs Bridegroom to bride, gold chain Father and mother of bride, piano, mats, and a basket of cutlery Mr. and Mrs. .Tames Dodds, set of table cutlery Miss Dodds, table centre and silver photo frames Miss M. Dodds case of salt cellars and spoons Mr. and Mrs. Dawson, silver fish eaters Miss Wheeler, marmalade jar and stand Mr. T. Waterfiald, cruet Mrs. T. Waterfleld, cut glass and silver salts Miss E. Rogers, pair of vases Mr. T. Davies, set of china flower stands Mr. and Mrs. John Leigh, set of vases Masters Jim and Fred Athorton,china flower pots Miss W. Rogers, set of vases Mrs. Heath, grandmother of bride, clock Mr. and Mrs. F. Smith, picture Mr. and Mrs. A. Fosely, set of carvers Mr. and Mrs. J. Pearson, glass bowls, salts and glasses Mr. F. Barker and Miss Cheatham, carvers Mr. Albert Cheatham, vase Mrs. Wheeler, China teapot Mr. and Mrs.Foden, hand-worked satin cushion Mr. and Mrs. Evans, pictures Miss Bella Davies, silver-mounted fruit dish Miss F Whitfield, cress dish and stand Mrs. Norcott (Widnes), glass cream jug and sugar basin Mr. and Mrs. John Pusill, China teapot Mr. and Mrs. Wharton. mincer Mr. Harold Cheatham, bronze flower stands Master B. Smith, bread and butter plate Miss Clara Hill, set of glass dishes Mr. H. Davies and Mr. C. Atherton, flower vases Mr. and Mrs. N. Hatton, set of vases Miss Martha Atherton, piano case Mr. and Mrs. John Atherton, set of brushes Mr. and Mrs. Roberts, Port Dinorwic bath towels Miss Makin, silver butter knife and table cloth Mr. Clapton, china fruit dish Mr. and Mrs. James. Davies, hand-worked taper holder Nurse Plempton, hand-worked table centre Mr. and Mrs. Holden (Liverpool), set of large vases Mr. and Mrs. J, Leigh, flower vases Mr. S. and Miss Atherton, bed linen Mr. Livingstone, fruit dish Miss Hushes, glass sns.ar and cream Miss E. Clark-, fruit dish Miss Dailey Heath, butter cooler Mr. and Mrs. Heath, jam cruet Miss Mary Heath, silver butter dish Mrs. Atherton, half dozen tumblers Mr. and Mrs. Turton, set of jugs Miss Potts, flower vase Miss A. Heath, out glass cruet Miss S. Atherton, sot of jugs Mr. and Mrs. Daniels, brass for hearth Miss B. Hall, table linen Mr. and Mrs. W. Leigh, punch bowl Mr. and Mrs. G. Leigh, glass cake dish Irs. Hunter, glass flower stands Miss Molly Daniels, vases Miss E. Daniels, glass cower vase Miss L. Tarbnton, pillow cases Mr. and Mrs. E. V. Walker, set of carvers Miss S. Atherton, 1 dozen wine glasses</p>
        <p>Miss Jackson, set of jugs Mr. W. Walker, cheque Mr. and Mrs. Lowe, clock Mr. and Mrs. Keyan, crumb brush and tray Miss Gibbons, cut glass sugar basin and cream jug The teachers at the St. Mary's Mission Sunday school, Penketh, oak clock.</p>
        <p>SPECI 1.T., VISIT TO LIVCICPOOL.-Mr. Thomas Ison, Ocnlarist and Aurist (of Ison's Eye and Ear Dispensary, Leeds, established 1871), will visit the Union Hotel (Parker-street, Clayton-square), Liverpool, on Wednes- day, September 16th, hours 12 to 7 ; Albion Hotel, Blackpool, on Monday, October sth, hours 12 to 6, end imery be consulted on all cases of failing sight and bearing. New treatment without operation. Sufferers should avail themselves of the present opportunity of consultation. Spectacles adapted for weak or defective sight. Artificial eyes supplied. Those unable to avail themselves of a personal consultation should write to Leeds for printed form of questions to answer, for- warded free. Letters to be addressed to 71, CiREAT Gtortoz ST., Leeds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>AT ST. MORITZ.</head>
        <p>AT ST. MORITZ.</p>
        <p>More News of the Member and His Wife. The following notes from St. Moritz have appeared in the Paris edition of the "New York Herald.' Much music indeed is in the air at present. M. and Mme. Serge von Derwies had a few friends to break- fast on August 18th at the Grand Hotel, including Mr. and Mrs. A. 11. Crosfield and Mrs. Huger Pratt. Afterwards M. von Derwies sat down at the piano, and played offhand dhopin's "Sonata," with the " Funeral March " (op. 35), so exquisitely that it not only enchanted his own party, but attracted many others besides to listen. _ • .</p>
        <p>Sr. Montrz, Wednesday, August 19th.—A. bogey competition was played on the Samaden links yester- day for a prize offered by Mr. W. W. Barton. It was won by Conte Fah di Bruno, who with an allowance of ten strokes was one down. Bogey score, by the way, is 74. Mr. Harry Kent (15 strokes) was 3 down, Mr. J. Howard Smith ao strokes) 4 down, Mr. A. fl. Crosfield (3 strokes) 5 down. Professor Lindsay and Mr. S. Sykes were also 5 down. A tie was the outcome of a mixed foursome handi- cap, for which ten couples entered. Here are the scores : Gross. Hcp. Net Mr. IL L. Gaw and Miss Moseley Mr. J. A. Henderson and Miss Maule 7l 7 61 Mr. S. Sykes and Mrs, Weil Bl 11 67 Mr. E. H. Holm and Mrs. Beer 69 69 Mr. B. A. Beer and Mrs. Lassetter 77 G 71 Mr. A. H. Crosfield and Mrs. Pratt BO 8 72 Mr. J. Lindenbaum and Miss Sewall Mr. S. C. M. Malik) and Mrs. Thayer 93 13 80 In the first round for the mixed doubles champion- ship on the Kuhn lawn tennis courts yesterday Dr. Frese and Frau Neresheimer beat Sir M. Mcllwraith and Baronne Goiffier d'Hestroy 6 —l, 6—o ; Mr. S. NI. Hall and Mrs. A. H. Crosfield beat Mr. P. Morrison land Miss M. Morrison 6-4, 6 —2 ; Herr 0. Widmann and Mine. Wyckoff beat Mr. and Mrs. T. H. Lloyd 6-3, 7 5. Sr. MORITZ, August 21st.—The mixed doubles lawn tennis championship of the Engadine was won yesterday by Dr. Freso and Frau Neresheimer in a walkover, their adversaries retiring. In the women's singles Miss Flliadi beat Mrs. A. H. Crosfield 6 2, 6—l ; Frau Neresheimer, W. 0., Mrs. Lloyd retiring.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>MESSRS. PEARSON AND KNOINLES LTD.</head>
        <p>MESSRS. PEARSON AND KNOINLES LTD.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL REPORT OF THE DIRECTORS. The Effect of TTeilaypole Colliery Disaster.</p>
        <p>The thirty-fifth annual meeting of the shareholders of the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Company, Ltd.. will be held in the company's office, Warrington, on Tuesday, September 29th. The following report of the directors will be presented The profits for the year endirag June .10th, 1903, amount to £116,009 17s. 10d., and the balance brought forward from last year is £8,907 15s. 11., together £125,007 135. 91 Au interim dividend at the rate of 6 per cent, per annum on the Preference Shares and 74 per cent.on the Ordinary Shares was paid in March last for the half- year ending December 31st, 1907, amounting to £30,000. The Directors now recommend that a dividend be paid fo- the past half-year at the rate of 6 per cent, per annum on the Preference Shares and of 15 per cent, per annum on the Ordinary Shares, making with the interim dividend 111 per cent. for the year, and a total distribution of .280,537 10s. The Directors further recomrq,Pad that £20,000 be added to the Reserve Fund, raising it to £170,000 ; that the sum of £12,333 Is 3d., which represents the expendi- ture on Capital Account for the year, be written off, and that the balance of £12,037 25. Id. be carried for- ward. . . .</p>
        <p>The conditions of the various trades in which the company is interested have fluctuated considerably during the year. The earlier mouths were marked by unusual activity in all departments, but the widely extended financial crisis in the autumn resulted in a general contraction of business, reduced prices, and in some directions short time and diminished output. The Directors desire to record their deepest sympathy with the relatives and friends of the men who were killed by the recent explosion at the Maypole Colliery, one of the pits of the Moss Hall Coal Company, Limited, in which this Company is the principal share- holder, and to express the sorrow which they feel at the loss of so many valuable lives. They are confident that in this they will have the acquiescence of every shareholder in the Company. The financial loss caused by the explosion, of which as yet no reliable estimate can be ma-de, will fall upon the Moss Hall Coal Company, Limited, but will neces- sarily affect the interests of this Company as large shareholders, and for some time delay the satisfactory results which were anticipated from the purchase of the shares.</p>
        <p>In complianoo with tha Articles of Association .Tr. W. Peter Rylands retires fram the Baard,and is eligible for re-election. _ _</p>
        <p>The auditors, Messrs. Blease and Sous, also retire,and are eligible for re-election._</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>PENKETH WESLEYAN CHURCH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH WESLEYAN CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Opening of tile New Organ. Thursday evening witnessed an event of more than ordinary interest in the history of Penketh Wesleyan Church,the fine new organ being opened and dedicated in the presence of a congregation which filled the building. The organ has been designed and built by Messrs. Alex Young and Sons, Manchester, in the most up-to-date style and will prove a great acquisition in the services of the church. There are two manuals, the great organ having six stops and the swell organ fifteen, and a pedal organ of two stops. The chief feature of Thursday's proceedings was a recital by Mr. T. Burgess Lane, organist of Dgrwen Parish Church and formerly of St. John's, Walton. He submitted a most interesting pro- gramme of pure organ music, four pieces being repre- sentative of the English School of composers, viz. " Overture in D" by John Kinross (a bright composition with well contrasted subjects, fre- quently played by the late W. T. Best), " Lied " and " The Answer" by Wolstenholme, the gifted blind organist and a friend of the recitalist, and the "Reverie" a delightful solo in the somewhat unusual 5-4 time by E. H. Lernare, one of the greatest recital organists in the world. In addition, works by the best classical composers, Handel, Bach (represented by the popular but difficult fugue—the great G Minor), Mendelssohn and Dvorak were performed. The full programme was :—Concerto for the Organ, Haadel ; Largo from the "New World Symphony," Dvorak ; Lied, Wolstenholme ; Allegretto " The Answer," Wolstenhointe ; Overture in D Major, Kinross; Reverie in E flat (5-4 time), Leman ; Grand Fugue in G Minor, Bach ; War March of the Priests from " Athalie," Mc ?Ida ssohn. _</p>
        <p>Mr. Lane's playing was characterised by great taste and skill and demonstrated to the full the beauties of tone and great power of the instrument. We thought his best effort was the Fugue in CI Minor (Bach)—a piece shich made heavy demands upon the performer's powers of execution to which Mr. Lane proved him- self quite equal. In addition to the organ selections, there was a pleasing vocal solo " Come ever smiling liberty " (Ilanlel)by Mrs. Lane, and violoncello solos "Andante " (Goltermann) and " Slumber Song " (Spare) given with feeling and exprossioa by Mr. Edgar Balm ford. The Rev. E. Blanshard Keeling, the newly-appointed superintendent minister, presided over the gathering, and delivered a short address. He said he was very glad to be with them on that auspicious occasion because whatever might happen later, there was certain to be conspicuous harmony at that gathering. Not that harmony meant everything. Some of the great things in the Church's history and in their own Church's history too had arisen out of rather sharp fighting ; sometimes with the powers outside and occasionally with their neighbours. On that occasion, however, they were consecrating their gathering to harmony. Having given some recollections of the opening of an organ at Barnsley in 1852,—" when the people were not so sophisticated as we are to-day with all sorts of fanciful musical tastes "—Mr. Keeling said he had always had a very warm side towards the musical part of the Church's services. The Society of Friends told them—though they were mending in that respect— that the influence of music in worship was altogether illegitimate, that it was purely sensual and had nothing to do with the spiritual, and that their relations with their Heavenly Father ought to be entirely independent of such aids. They were not all as strong as those in the Society of Friends and they felt the necessity of whatever aid they could get, and they as Methodists would be recreant to their traditions if they did not cultivate to the utmost of their power the musical side of their worship. Some of his most precious recollec- tions were connected with the Sunday evening singing in home circles and some of his most sacred memories were associated with the songs of Charles Wesley. The echoes of them were in his memory at that moment. There was a time when the Methodists led England in singing and he was anxious that they should do so again. He was present at a Cathedral service once and the singing was exquisite, but he was , not at home and he asked himself, while he was sitting there, "Is this worship ? " He could not answer positively. It was not worship in his case; it was a concert and he enjoyed it. He was anxious that even while they listened to the music that night they should Mike it worship, and let it move their hearts to those greater and higher things for which they were intended. The Bev. F. Welwyn gernicit offered the dedicatory Prayer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, September 12, 1908.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, September 12, 1908.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Silcock appeared before the Court and said she was tenant of 165, Wellfleld-street and paid the rent. Her husband, however, was tenant of 163. Mr. Woodcock said the two places were all one, being structurally connected. Mr. Howes : There is only one rent paid for the building, and that is in your name. Mrs. Silcock : Yes. Mr. Holbrook : My information was that James was tenant of 103. Mr. Howes : His name is not in the rent book. The vote was not allowed. MORE LODGER CLAIMS DISALLOWED.</p>
        <p>The Liberals claimed a lodger vote for Joseph Whyte, 35, Bewsey-street. The landlady stated that claimant had sole use of a bedroom and sittingroom and paid Ss. for use of rooms, gas,coal, and washing. He found his own food.</p>
        <p>Mr. Woodcock stated that the ratable value of the house was £l4 gross and £l2 net: The Revising Barrister : The mere fact that she receives Gs. a week does not make the value.</p>
        <p>Mr. Howes : What does make the value ? The pay- ment must make it.</p>
        <p>The Revising Barrister: Oh, dear me no. The only way I can judge of the value is by taking a pro- portion of the overseers' value. Mr. Woodcock said the rent of the house was 7s. Gd. a week. _ _</p>
        <p>The Revising Barrister : You choose to let him have two rooms and make him pay 6s. a week. It does not follow that it is the value. You give him all kinds of advantages. The mere amount paid by the lodger is not the only test of the value of the premises. Mr. Howes : What value do you require ? The Revising Barrister : That I cannot tell you. If I tell you I require a particular value then you come here prepared. Ideal with every case on its own merits. Mr. Howes : We want to avoid putting cares in below the value you require. The Revising Barrister : It is no trouble to tie to go into the cases. Mr. Howes : It is a great deal of trouble to us to put in claims and then find the ratable value is not enough. The Revising Barrister : I said on the last occasion that the ratable value had a very great impression on any mind. The vote was not allowed.</p>
        <p>In the case of David Morgan James,l3o, Orford-lano, who also claimed a lodger vote, it was stated that claimant paid Gs. a week for the use of the rooms and the rent of the house was 6s. The T. oto was not allowed.</p>
        <p>Mr. Holbrook asked the Revising Barrister for infor- mation which would guide the agents in these cases. He said he had tried both small and large amounts with a view to getting at what his Honour would accept, so that they would know in future. The Revising Barrister : I will tell you a way in which you can save yourselves a lot of trouble. If you take the gross value of the premises and divide that by the number of rooms, having ascertained how many rooms are occupied by the lodger, you can arrive very near the point ; but you must allow something for the use of furniture and attendance. I cannot go any nearer than that.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Holbrook : That will gin us an idea of what to work upon. . . . . . . _</p>
        <p>The Revising Barrister : in my opinion the law on the point is in a ridiculously uncertain state. Some barristers make a hard and fast lino in order to save themselves trouble, but I am not going to do it. Mr. Herbert Hayes : The two best rooms in the house may be worth all the others put together. The Revising Barrister : 1 shall want evidence why. Mr. Holbrook said be was afraid it was going to be a very light court. What with his Honour's ruling on lodger claims and Mr. Woodcock's good list, they could not find any eases. The Revising Barrister : What I cannot understand is this. Warrington is a very large place. Are there no lodging's of any higher value ? Mr. Holbrook : Very few, sir. Mr. Howes : If we take the rule your Honour has laid down, we shall only be able to find mansions. The Revising Barrister : My opinion is that when this lodger franchise was introduced, the idea was to place the lodger in the same position as the occupier of a tenement, assuming he paid the same value. It is easy enough to manufacture votes in the case of lodgers. It is not easy to manufacture them in the case of occupa- tion.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Howes : It seems to be extremely difficult to manufacture lodger claims in Warrington now.</p>
        <p>EVENING SITTING. QUESTIONS OF TENANCY.</p>
        <p>At the evening sittirrg of the Court the Liberals claimed an occupiers' vote for John Head. 60, Fox-street. In answer to Mr. Helbrook, claimant stated that he came to reside at the house about the week after last Walking Day but one. He had not got the rent book, which had apparently been destroyed. Mr. Holbrook : The house was empty on the 15th day of July last year. Mr. Woodcock said his information was that the house was empty on July 15th, and that the 111312 came into possession on the ISth. Mr. Howes: July ISth was a Wednesday, and prob- ably the house would to all appearances be empty an the Monday when he was moving into it. He must start the tenancy on Monday. The Revising Barrister: lean appreciate that a man may take premises and take a couple of days to get into thorn, but this man does not know when he came in.</p>
        <p>Mr. lloCves : The week after \Valldng Day, and it is a question of whether it was the 15th or the 18th July. Mr. Woodcock: We have him as going into occupa- tion on the 18th. Mr. Howes : The rent started from the 15th.</p>
        <p>The Revising Barrister : I cannot take yot; evidence. The question is can the claimant tell me when he began the tenancy. The Claimant : I cannot. The nearest I can tell you is the week after Walking Day. Mr. Woodcock : Walking Day last year was June 23th, Mr. Holbrook: That makes it worse still. Mr. Howes : It is admitted on all hands that ho was in the house on the 18th. --</p>
        <p>The Revising Barrister : He was not in on the 15th. Mr. Howes: He was moving in on the 15th. The Revising Barrister : We should have the list so beautifully arranged if I could only take your evidence, but what early evidence is there that he was moving in on the 15th.</p>
        <p>Mr. Howes : This is the prima facie evidence that a man won't take a house in the middle of the week.</p>
        <p>The Revising Barrister : What a man won't do is not prima facie evidence. The prima facie evidence is that he did not take the house until the 18th. What is the rebutting evidence? Mr. Howes : I contend that it was impossible to take the house on the 18th. He must have taken it on the 15th or 22nd. The Revising Barrister: That does not follow. If I were a landlord and a man said he would pay the difference between Wednesday and Saturday I would not let him go away. Mr. Howes : I am afraid if you were a landlord in Warrington you would be glad to allow him to have half a week for nothing. Mr. Woodcock said the house was empty when it was surveyed on the 15th and subsequently the people in the house gave the information that they came in on July 18th. Mr. Howes : They might have come in to sleep on the 18th, but they must, have taken it on the Monday. The Revising Barrister said the prima facic evidence was on the wrong side for the claimant and he could not allow the vote.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>WARRINGTON REVISION COURT.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON REVISION COURT.</p>
        <p>A DISCUSSION ON LODGER VOTES.</p>
        <p>Revising Barrister and Dishonest Applications.</p>
        <p>The Revising Barrister (Mr. C. H. M. Wharton) has attended at the Warrington Town Hall this week for , the purpose of revising the voters' lists. The business has been very light, the occupiers' claims numbering ' 35, lodger claims 57, and objections 19. The Court opened on Tuesday morning when amongst those present were :—Mr. Joseph Howes, jun. (representing the Liberals), Mr. W. Holbrook and Mr. Herbert ' Hayes, (representing the Conservatives), Mr. W. H. Woodcock (assistant overseer for Warrington), Mr. A. T. Hallaway (deputy town clerk), Mr. Joseph Smith (assistant overseer for Great Sankey) and Mr. E. C. Cooper (assistant overseer for Winwick).</p>
        <p>SUCCESSFUL LODGER CLAIM. EACH CASE TO BE JUDGED ON ITS MERITS. The Liberals claimed a lodger vote for Joseph Eastwood, 4, Bewsey-road. Claimant stated that he occupied one bedroom and had the run of the house. Mr. HowEs : He occupies a bedroom and sittin,, room. He has the sole use of them and beyond that he has the run of the house. Mr. Holbrook : It is not the sole use of two rooms but the general run of the place. He is one of the family. I Mr. Woodcock said the rateable value of the house was .223 gross. Mr. Holbrook : We don't obje3t to the value. That is all right. Claimant stated that he lodged with his daughter and paid 6s. per week for rooms, fire, gas ald attendance. I Mr. Holbrook : Have you the sole use of the sitting room or is it jointly along with the others? Claimant : The sitting room is used jointly along with other members of the family. The Revising Barrister : I am anxious not to deprive any man of a vote if he is entitled to it but I had such a lot of what I canaot help describing as absolutely dishonest applica- tions on the last occasion that I must really be careful. Mr. Howes : The claimant is a retired police officer and he pays for the use of the rooms, without a doubt. The Revising Barrister : I dare say that is all perfectly legitimate. How many rooms are there in the house? Claimant : I should think there are ten or eleven. Mr. Howes: This will perhaps be about the best lolger claim you will have to deal with in this Court. Mr. Holbrook : The claim is wrong. He states that he has the sole use of a bedroom and sitting room. The Revising Barrister : I should amend it if neces- sary. The point lam thinking about is this : For 6s. a week he has two rooms and for those two rooms he is supposed to pay considerably more than half the rent' of the house. If you divide the number of rooms in the house by the number of rooms he occupies and , deduct something for gas, coal, furniture, and so forth, I it practically leaves you about 4s. I am afraid I cannot allow it. _</p>
        <p>.„. Mr. Howes: May I ask on what grounds you do not allow the claim ? . .</p>
        <p>The Revising Barrister : On the ground that it is not of sufficient value. Arr. Howes : We can put what value you like. As a matter of fact the amount would probably be £i a week for living thera, but we have put 65. in for the value of the rooms. What he pays for gas, food and so forth is included in the remainder. It is one of the most legitimate claims— The Revising Barrister : I do not understand you. The claim is an essential part of the application and the claim says he pays Gs. a week and that must include everything. Mr. Howes : That is what he agrees to show for the use of the rooms which are to be of the yearly value, unfurnished, of £lO. The Revising Barrister : If the whole value is only £25 I don't think the two rooms unfurnished are worth 210. Mr. Howes : To the claimant they are. The Revising Barrister : I said last time and I say again that the question of whether they are of that value to the claimant shall not be taken into consider- ation. I know that something of the sort has been decided in a Scotch case but so long as I have to deal with these cases I shall hold that if the value is to the claimant alone, that is not a consideration. Mr. Howes : This claim would stand good, if we had put a larger amount in. The Revising Barrister : I don't say that at all. We I want to get at the facts of the case. Mr. Howes : We can make this claim next year into anything you like. The Revising Barrister : I know you can, and when- ever you have a relative taking part in these matters you can make the claims anything you like, subject, of course, to the penalties of the Police Court. Mr. Howes : We know that. The reason I am sticking so much at this is because I am perfectly con- vinced that this is an absolutely legitimate claim. The man pays for the lodgings the sum required by Act of Parliament and occupies rooms which are of the clear yearly value of £lO. The Revising Barrister : How much of the Gs. do you say is for the furniture and attendance? Mr. Howes : Nothing. I say he pays that 63. for the use of the rooms. The Revising Barrister : The claimant does not say so. He pays 13s. altogether. The claimant : The furniture in the bedroom is mine.</p>
        <p>The IRevising Barrister : Hive you any contract ? What is your agreement with your daughter ? Claimant : I pay her 18e. a week. She cooks for me and does everything—in fact it is a home. Mr. Howes : I suggested to him that we should take I2s. for the attendance and food and Gs. for the roonas, and he said that would be about right. The Revising Barrister : flow much do you reckon the food costs? .</p>
        <p>Claimant : It will cost very near that (apparently meaning the 125.) rhe Revising Barrister : Thou there is nothing left for the use of the fiwniture and attenlance. Claimant: Bear in mind she is my daughter, and whatever loss there may be on either side, we agree to</p>
        <p>The Revising Barrister : I know you do and that is just the difficulty I have. I should be only too delighted to give you this vote, but there is my difficulty. If sha were not your daughter she would insist on having a considerable amount more. Claimant : Possibly. The Revising Barrister : That is just the thing I cannot take into consideration. _</p>
        <p>Mr.flowes : She could not insist on having anything more for the use of tho rooms. _ .</p>
        <p>The Revising Barrister : She might say " I chnrge my father so much for the rooms without any furniture, and the rest I give him." Mr. Flowes : The amount of Ga. a waek put down for the use of the rooms was arrived at by me..</p>
        <p>The Revising Barrister: You come her-e and say you can livo on 15s. a week and go elsewhere and say 20s. a week is ridiculous. .</p>
        <p>Mr. Howes: That is only at the seaside. (Laughter.) You will (Ind people in Warrington in lodgings who only pay 104. a week for board and lhgings. That is not an isolated case. . _</p>
        <p>The Revising Barrister : It does not say much for the lodgings. (Laughter.) This is a very difficult case to deal with. Of course, you have told me that 12s. would be about the cost of the food and I am told that for 10s. a week you can manage to live, or rather some- body might manage to keep you, if they had other people to keep at the same time. I shall make an exception in this case, but it must not be taken to be a rule at all, and I will allow it. I shall deal with every case on its own foundation.</p>
        <p>Mr. Howes The value of this house would be more but property has depreciated in that district. It is worth a great deal more than it is rated at. The Revising Birrister : I suppose that if I were to deal strictly with this case I should say that in the absence of a definite agreement assigning a certain portion' of the amount paid to the rooms themselves, I could not decide in your favour. You go to the agent's office and he says "We will take so much for food and so much for the rooms themselves," but that is not the point. The law desires a specific contract which can be proved and whenever anyone wants to come to me to obtain a lodger vote, he must come prepared with a contract. If the agreement is' You must pay so much for use of the rooms, so much for the furniture and board," that is a contract. If you say the whole amount is so much for board and lodgings, then I have to decide how much is for the rooms and how much for board.</p>
        <p>. Mr. Howes : You want in each case the lodger to provide you with a contract from the landlord of th 3 house that he pays 6s, for the rooms. The Revising Barrister: I don't say a written con- tract. I want something proved. There was a great mistake made last time I was at Warrington. It was suggested that I wanted all kinds of attendances. I never wanted anything of the sort. In the recent appeal case, Lord Justice Williams said it was a great pity that Revising Barristers did not pay more atten- tion to the strict legal evidence and he thought that if they did that in future, it would save a great deal of trouble. He has simply borne out by that remark what I said at Warrington, which was construed into some- thing entirely different and put into one of the news- papers. I can deal with the thing as a matter of law but I cannot tell whether a man can live on 10s. or ss, a week. I once lived on 12s. 6d, a week for twelve months and I dare say I could do it again, but the question is, what did he live on. Let it be quite under- stood that I lay down no rule at all and I deal with every case as it comes. NOT THE TENANT.</p>
        <p>The Conservatives claimed an occupiers' vote on behalf of James Silcock, 163, Wellfleld-street. Mr. Howes : My objection is that Nos. 163 and 165, WeMeld-street are one tenement, and that the tenant is Airs. Sikook and not James.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>HOW SHE FIXED TIIE DATE.</head>
        <p>HOW SHE FIXED TIIE DATE.</p>
        <p>The Conservatives claimed a vote for Alfred Webb,6, Ripley-street. Mrs. Webb stated that claimant came into possession of the house on July 15th of last year. She could not produce the rent book ; it must have been destroyed. Asked how she fixed the date Mrs. Webb stated that she got married on the Monday after Walking Day and went straight to the house. The Revising Barrister: Who told you it was the 15th ?</p>
        <p>Mrs. Webb : My mother. The Revising Barrister : When did she tell you that? Mn. Webb : When the men came round canvassing. (Laughter.) Mr. Woodcock said it seemed to him that claimant could make a good claim. From inquiries it appeared that claimant came into possession on the second Monday after Walking Day—July sth. The Revising Barrister : I think that is good enough. The vote will be allowed. Mr. Howes : I contend that the case is not half as clear as the last case and you ought to require the rent book for this. The Revising Barrister : I have evidence of a definite date-Bth July. ' Mr. Howes : You had the same there. The Revising Barrister : I am perfectly satisfied that she does not know anything about it. Mr. Howes : And yet you take her evidence as bona fide. The Revising Barrister : She admits that she got the information from her mother. I am not taking her evidence, but Mr. Woodcock's.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>OCCUPIER OR LODGER?</head>
        <p>OCCUPIER OR LODGER?</p>
        <p>REASONS FOR NOT CHANGING TENANCIES. Edward Birchall, 19, Fletcher-street, who applied for a vote, admitted that his mother was the tenant. Mr. Howes said he brought this case to ask his Honour whether applicant should be occupier or lodger He kept the house going, but he (Mr. Howes) was afraid on his Honour's ruling he would be disentitled to a vote as a lodger. The Revising Barrister : He cannot be occupier, , that is clear. _</p>
        <p>Mr. Howes: Would he be a lodger? I have brought the case to ask your opinion in order that I can put in a lodger claim next rear. The Revising Barrister : It is hardly my business to inquire what you can do next year, but I will do so on this occasion. In answer to the Revising Barrister, claimant said the house was occupied by only his mother and himself. There were five rooms and be had the exclusive UB3 of two. The rent of the house was is. Pd. and he paid his mother £1 a week to include everything. The Revising Barrister It looks like a claim I</p>
        <p>might allow.but I am not going to tell you what to do. Claimant stated that the reason the tenancy was not changed was that it was against his mother's wish. The Revising Barrister said he found in many cases that the mother wanted to be the tenant and wanted her son or husband, as the case might _be, to have the vote. He was afraid he could not grant a vote and he advised the claimant to change the tenancy. Reverting to the question of tenancy, the Revising Barrister said there were generally two reasons why a mother wanted to be tenant. One was to avoid the effects of creditors and the other was because it was undignified to have the son or husband aa tenant. He had hundreds of these cases last time. Sometimes a woman took a house in her own name and the object was that if there were any debts she could say "It is my place, not his." She did it for her own conveni- ence and for the man's. _</p>
        <p>--- Mr. Howes : We contend that the " breadwinner" case covers that.</p>
        <p>The Revising Barrister : I am satisned that you are wrong about that.</p>
        <p>A PARTNERSHIP.</p>
        <p>James Hall applied for a. vote in respect of 33, Pinners-brow, being supported by Mr. Holbrook. He stated be was a partner with his fathsr and thre3 brothers in a bottling business. The ratable value of the premises was given at £lO, and the vote was not allowed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>MORE ABOUT THE BREADWINNER CASE.</head>
        <p>MORE ABOUT THE BREADWINNER CASE.</p>
        <p>The Liberals claimed a vote for David Peters, 12, King-street, who said he had been tenant nine years. He was his wife's second husband, and she was his second wife. On getting married the house was in his wife's name and he did not alter it. .</p>
        <p>Mr. Hewes added that the wife occupied the house prior to being married to applicant. He brought the income into the house. Mr. Holbrook said applicant's name 14 as now in the rent book, but on the wife's information. the change of tenancy did not take place until August 21th. Mr. Howes : The landlord informs me that ever since the man came there he has held himself responsible for the rent. . .</p>
        <p>The Revising Barrister said it was clear that when the 1908 rent book was made out the contract was in applicants wife's name. He could not give the vote. Mr. Howes : Is not this a case of the breadwinner ?</p>
        <p>The Revising Barrister : No, it lA not; the mere fact that a person is the breadwinner and pays the rent does not entitle that person to a vote unless that person is a tenant. It is the tenant and not the person who pays the money. Mr. Howes : Then why the breadwinner case at all? The Revising Barrister Why don't you read the breadwinner case ? Mr. Howes I have done. _ .</p>
        <p>. _ The Revising Barrister : It is a different thing altogether In both breadwinner cases, the persons who bad the votes were the tenants. The Claimant: I have never slept out of the house • for nine years. The Revising Barrister : That is not being the tenant. _ .</p>
        <p>Claimant : It's a curious thing to me. Mr. Howes (to claimant on departing) : You are dis- franchised for twelve months. Mr. Wharton (sharply) : One moment. What did you say ? . Mr. Howes : He losos his vote for ar.other twelve months. Mr. Wharton : He doesn't lose his vote, because he has never had a vote and has never been ent.tled to one, so don't say that. Mr. Howes ; If he has not a vote he must be disfranchised. Claimant : I had the franchise before I married my second wife. The Revising Barrister : You should have stuck to it when you got married again. If you want a vote, you must go to the landlord, change the tenancy and have a proper contract made out. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>CCRIOUS POINT.</head>
        <p>A CCRIOUS POINT.</p>
        <p>Harry P. S. [Liggett claimed a service vote in respect of the premises over the shop, 83, Bridge-street. He said he was a member of a limited liability company who owned the premises. He paid £2.0 a year rent for ten rooms over the shop. Mr. Woodcock said the whole of the premises were in the occupation of a limited company of which appli- cant was a member. The Revising Barrister : Who else lives in the plies? Claimant : Mr. Worthington, the partner in the firm. He has two rooms and pays so much a week. I am responsible to the firm for the rent. The Revising Barrister : A rather nice point this. Claimant stated that there was an agreement on the matter before the limited company was formed, but there had been none since. Mr. Howes : The other partner claims as a lodger. The Revising Barrister refused the vote. A MUNICIPAL VOTE.</p>
        <p>Albert Edward Howard was granted a municipal vote only in respect of 28, Fletcher-street. He was a member of the firm and there was no vote attached to the place. The ratable value-221 10s.—was not sufficient to entitle him to a Parliamentary vote. WEDNESDAY'S PROCEEDINGS. A POINT CLEARED UP.</p>
        <p>The Court was again opened on Wednesday morning. The Revising Barrister alluded to the claim of John Head, dealt with the previous day, in which there was a dispute as to whether the claimant was in occupation on the 15th or the ISth July. He satisfied himself that claimant was not the tenant for the qualifying period. Claimant did not prove that he was in possession at the proper time and since then his (the Revising Barrister's) decision had been confirmed. He was saying this because it would give the agents some satisfaction to know that the thing had been cleared up. The assistant overseer had made further inquiries and by a survey which was made on the 13th it was ascertained that the man was not living in Warrington then, but at Stockton Heath. That bore out the suggestion that he really came to Warrington on the 18th. Mr. Howes : HE must have become the tenant on the following Monday. - - -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>LEGAL ASPECT.</head>
        <p>THE LEGAL ASPECT.</p>
        <p>During the hearing of another case the Revising Barrister agreed to accept Mr. Holbrook's personal testimony with regard to the name in a rent book. He said that in a recent appeal case Lord Justice Williams said it would be much better if Revising Barristers would be more particular in admitting nothing but legal evidence. Of course, they were entitled to admit hear- say evidence some times, but it did create a difficulty. He (the Revising Barrister) was afraid that if strictly legal evidence were insisted on in all cases a tremendous lot of names would have to come off the list. SENTIMENT NOT CONSIDERED.</p>
        <p>In another case Mr. Holbrook pointed out that there was no vote at all for the house. The Revising Barrister : You need never trouble yourselves about any sentimental paints with me because I am going to carry out the law and not senti- ment. If the position of the law means a hardship, the sooner it is altered the better.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>OPPORTUNITIES FOR FRAUD.</head>
        <p>OPPORTUNITIES FOR FRAUD.</p>
        <p>The Revising Barrister later remarked that; unfortunately the law had given all kinds of opportuni- ties for fraud. If ever there was an Act of Parliament</p>
        <p>in this world which afforded opportunities for fraud it was this in reference to lodgers. "AN AIR OF GENIJINENES'S."</p>
        <p>The Revising Barrister having granted a lodger vote to an only sou in a case where the ratable value was £l9 gross, Mr. Howes proceeded to note the fact. The Revising Barrister : You must not put that down. It is not because of the .Cl 9. If I were only dealing with it on the rental I should not allow it. There is an air of genuineness ahout it in the fact that it is a good house apart from the rental, and these rooms appear to be the best in the house. Therefore I am justified in coming to the conclusion that they are worth £lO.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>PUBLIC HOUSE CLAIMS.</head>
        <p>PUBLIC HOUSE CLAIMS.</p>
        <p>The claim of Reginald 0. Crompton, The Blackburn° Arms Hotel, for a lodger vote was dealt with. Mr. Howes said there were about five claims in respect of public-houses. His contention in objecting was that the landlord of licensed premises could not surrender control of any part of those premises and therefore he could not have a lodger. The Revising Barrister : That is just the test of a lodger. The landlord does not surrender control, he retains it. That is why the man is a lodger. If that objection were applied to the occupation franchise it would be a very good point but these people claim as lodgers. Mr. Howes r• He cannot have the sole use of the</p>
        <p>rooms. The Revising Barrister : Oh yes, he can. Ele has the sole use during the period of the contract. Mt. Howes : I doubt whether, if this were only an ordinary house, the ratable value would be sufficient to carry anything like it does now. Mr. Eiolbrook : It would be £lO at least without the licence.</p>
        <p>The Revising Barrister: That is a good point, if there is anything in it. What is it worth without a licence ? Mr. Holbrook : £4O or £5O. Mr. Woodcock : It is a big, commodions house. The Revising Barrister : You say that apart from the licence it would be worth about £4O. Mr. Woodcock : It would not fetch £4O as a house. The Revising Barrister : HOW many rooms are there ? Mr. Woodcock: I should say there must be six or seven bedrooms. The Revising Barrister decided that the value of the rooms occupied by the claimant would be quite .£lO and allowed the votes</p>
        <p>DECLARATIONS UNRELIABLE. The Revising Barrister had to deal with a number of cases in which declarations were submitted. He remarked that he did not mind saying that he attached very little importance to these declarations,except what the law compelled him. Half of them he did not believe and he thought—without speaking of these or any particular cases—that if the facts were gone into in regard to a great many cases, half the lodger claimants would be sent to prison. Mr, Howes : That is very hard, because I shall</p>
        <p>have the misfortune to appear before you in the Newton Division as a lodger claimant. (Laughter.) The Revising Barrister: That will be awkward. (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>LODGER CLAIM DIFFICULT TO FIND.</p>
        <p>The Revising Barrister said he thought he had better give instructions to the overseers that in all lodger cases he would like to have detailed information of the number of residents and the number of rooms in the house. He would then be better able to deal with them.</p>
        <p>Mr. Holbrook: It will be difficult to find any lodgers after this. If a £l6 house will not qualify for a lodger, it is hard lines in Warrington. The majority of the houses are not, as you will find them in many towns, over-rated. A 4113 home is a fair one. You will not get forty lodger claims throughout the borough if you take them on the basis you are doing now. The Revising Barrister : I dare say. I cannot help I that. Looking at the object of the Act I should think it was not intended that these cases should be brought I forward.</p>
        <p>Mr. Holbrook: I should have taken it the other way. The Revising Barrister : If it was, they should have been more explicit.</p>
        <p>NOT LOGICAL.</p>
        <p>William T. Worthington, of the firm of Haggett and Worthington, Bridge-street, claimed a lodger vote. Hr. Howes contended that claimant was as much the landlord as Mr. 'Liggett. He was a member of the firm.</p>
        <p>The Revising Thrrister : Mr. Haggett took the rooms from himself and lived in them and then sub-let two of them to a man who had as much right to let them as he had. I cannot sae that there is any logic about this and I cannot allow the claim. A SCHOOLMASTER'S CLAIM. Samuel J. Price, schoolmaster, 53, Gorsev-lane, claimed the occupier's vote which was at present allowed to John Price, his brother, who had ceased to be the tenant. Claimant had put in a declaration but as the statements contained therein did not correspond with the information which Mr. Howes said he had obtained from the landlord, the Revising Barrister decided that the claimant should be sent for. When he arrived The Revising Barrister remarked : I see there is a declaration in which yea say you are the person who is described in the list as John Price. Claimant : No, lam not John Price. . . .</p>
        <p>The Revising Warrister : YO-17;a';YOu are the person who is supposed to have the vote, and the tenant of 53, Gorsey-lan? . .</p>
        <p>Claimant Yes.</p>
        <p>Further questioned tha claimant said he became tenant over IS wonths ago but could not give the precise date. He was prepared to swear that he had been the tenant for twelve months from July 15th last. His brother, John Price, was the tenant before hitn. Mr. Howes: Might I suggest it may have been Mrs. Price ?</p>
        <p>Claimant said his brothor -*had been tenant for quite four years and gave it up on leaving town. He could not say definitely whether the rent book was altered,but he gave instructions for notice to be given to the land- lord.</p>
        <p>The Revising Blrrister : I understand from one of the political agents that he has seen the landlord and he says you have not been the tenant of the house the length of time you say. Tint is why I am obliged to ask you these questions. It is a matter which is rather a serious issue.</p>
        <p>Mr. Howes : The landlord said the tenancy was not changed till November, 1907. Claimant: Perhaps he has confused it with the fact that my brother left town in November. Mr. Howes : The landlord said the house previous to that was in the name of Airs. Price, not John Price, and that it was changed from Mrs. Price to Samuel James Price in November, 1907. The Claimant : Whatever change was made should have been made before that.</p>
        <p>Mr. Howes said that although tha case might appear serious, perhaps at the bottom there was nothing in it, if claimant had given instractions for the tenancy to be altered and the landlord had failed to do it until November.</p>
        <p>Tha Revising Barrister (to the claimant) : You have relieved my mind very c nsiderably with regard to your declaration. lam quite satisfied you could have made an arrangement of this kind and left it to other people, and believed it had been carried out.—His Honour decided to adjourn the case until the assistant overseer had made independent inquiries from the land- lord.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>" TIIE LIIBILITY TO FIZAI7D."</head>
        <p>" TIIE LIIBILITY TO FIZAI7D."</p>
        <p>In a case where Mr. Holbrook remarked: This is the son of one of our Councillors, The Revising Barrister observed : Ido not regard the position of the parties as worth taking into con- sideration. There is as much liability to fraud in well-to-do people as in poor people. " UNCOMMONLY SUSPICIOUS."</p>
        <p>The Revising Barrister granted a lodger vote to Alfred Rogers, of the Feathers Hotel, although he remarked that the case was uncommonly suspicious. In face of the declaration and of the absence of evidence to contradict it, he must allow the claim, but he did not like the look of it a bit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-09-12_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>CLAIMS ALLOWED.</head>
        <p>CLAIMS ALLOWED.</p>
        <p>Out of a total number of lodger claims of 57, his Honour allowed 18. THURSDAY'S PROCEEDINGS.</p>
        <p>STRONG COMIENTS ON T FIE SCHOOL NIASTER'S CASE.</p>
        <p>On Thursday morning the Court was concluded with the process known as starring the lists." Before this business began, the Revising Barrister alluded to the Case of Samuel J. Price, schoolmaster' 58, Gorsey-lane. He said : In that case I understood yesterday that the matter was to be left entirely to the overseer and he was to send to the landlord to obtain information without any interference at all by anybody. I have ascertained that as soon as that order was made Mr. Price, who appeared before me, went himself and inter- viewed the landlord. My object in leaving the matter to the overseer was that there should be no interfer- ence on either side. The landlord has sent a note which I am not going to recognize,and for this reason: I have gone very carefully into the matter .and I am satisfied that the tenancy did commence in November. I have come to the conclusion that when the November elections were coming on, it became necessary to get the vote of somebody. It was ascer- tained that John was away and the next available person was looked for. I am therefore not satisfied that there is evidence of tenanty during the period necessary to qualify and 1 shall not allow the claim. With regard to a declaration of this description, all I have to say is that it is an improper use of a declara- tion and ought not to have been made. I shall not allow the vote. If they like to appeal they can but probably they will fled it a little bit awkward if they do. lam further strongly of opinion that so far as John is concerned, John was never the tenant and John has been deceiving somebody and obtaining a vote when in point of fact the mother has been tenant all along. Mr. Holbroook : In former years this was a claim which came before Mr. Foard, your predecessor, and the vote was allowed,under his ruling, to John in place of the mother. Mr. Woodcock : Yes. It was fairly common in those days. The Revising Barrister : Yes. I suppose so. Unfortunately I cannot take him off. He is bound to remain on now according to law, and I suppose at an election he I would be allowed to vote, but io future I have told the overseers to take note of the position and he won't be on next time. "AFRAID TO APPEAR."</p>
        <p>In a case where the Revising Barrister was asked to strike out a claim because the claimant had failed to appear, M. Howes remarked that after reading the report of the first day's proceedings, the man was afraid to turn up.</p>
        <p>THANKING THE REVISING BARRISTER. MORE ABOUT LODGER CASES.</p>
        <p>At the close of the revision, Mr. W. Holbrook, on behalf of the Cmseevative party, moved a vote of thanks to the Revising B mister for the courtesy with which he had listened to the claims and objections, and to the assistant overseer and his staff for the able manner in which they had prepared the list. The duties of the Court had been light, but that was owing to the good list. As to the lodger claims, he was afraid the agents would not trouble the Revising Barrister with many of them in the future. His Honour seemed to be rather hard on them and they had a difficulty in getting persons to sign lodger claims now owing to the large value required. There were not many houses in and about Warrington which would qualify lodgers under his Honour's decision. Mr. Holbrook drew his Honour's attention to a case at Bolton the previous day which was reported as follows: " There has been practically nothing of a con- tentious character about the proceedings connected with the revision of the lists of voters at Bolton Town Hall, conducted by Mr. Kinghorn, but a singular case was mentioned on Wednesday. A lodger vote was claimed by John Patrick Hogan for a room and bedroom at 18, Urown-street. He stated that he paid 10s. per week for the accommodation, but it was pointed out that the rent of the house was only 4s. 3d. per week. Hogan said he paid 10s. per week for the rooms alone. He was a cousin of the tenant, and could not say whether an ordinary lodger would pay as much as he did. He however, bad arranged to. The barrister said that under such circumstances he could not see any objection to the claim, and he allowed the vote." The Revising Barrister said it was not part of his ditty to criticise his brother barristers on the Northern circuit, but he did not agree with the decision. Mr. J. Howes, junt., in seconding the vote of thanks, endorsed 111 r. Holbrook's remarks. They seemed to I be getting more used to his Honour now and he (Mr. I Howes) remembered the advice his father gave to him on his first appearance in Court : "My lad, study your (barrister before tho law." (Laughter.) He was sure I the list prepared by Mr. Woodcock this year was nothing short of marvellous. It was wonderful how he got it, and the agents would be delighted if he did not I get it so good—(laughter)—so that they might have a ' chance of bringing more clairtts and having something</p>
        <p>to work on. At present they did a lot of work for littla resnits.</p>
        <p>The Revising Barrister, in acknowledging the com- pliment, said he had no right to take the vote of thanks to himself because he was simply doing a di ty for which he was paid and he was very well satisfl ed with the payment. He expected the payment was Ii sed at a time when the work was much harder than it was now, but certainly the work was not only made easy by the work of the overseers but by the work of the political agents. He was getting more and more satisfied that political agents were a distinct advantage in assisting him and, so long as they understood each other, lie thought they were assisting the ends of justice. When he said justice he referred to the rights of voters ,those who ought to be put on the list and those who ought not. With regard to the lodger claims and to all the claims, he was simply influenced by what he considered to be the law. Sentiment was altogether outside the question. He knew that some people allowed them- selves to be ruled by sentiment but he had come to the conclusion, although he was not put upon oath to administer law in connection with the revision, that he was certainly put to perform the duties because he was supposed to be a lawyer and to under- stand the law with regard to registration. It might be a very strong supposition but he endeavoured to carry it out. He was satisfied in his own mi nd that his view of the lodger franchise was correct. He might regret that a great many deserving people who seemed as if they ought to have a vote did not get one, but that was not his fault but the fault of the Act of Parlia- ment and those who were dissatisfied with that state of things should take immediate steps, when Parliament was sitting, to get their friends to alter the law. As the law stood ho was perfectly s ttisflA that h 3 had been correct. If he had done anything at all, he had rather drawn a Bee in favour of a voter than against him. He had been at very great trauble to ascertain what was the intention and he thought that if they would carefully consider the position they would find that it was thought necessary to give to a person in the position of a lodger who contributed his share of occupation of premises which paid rates, the same rights with regard to voting as if he occupied a ho use by himself. That was his notion of the meaning of the law and, of course, when one came to deal with small tenements where it was almost impossible to make a separate tenancy of the value of .£lO, it was the fault of the legislation. It might be defective or it might not,it , I might ba right or wrong—that was no part of his busi- ness. His opinion was that that was the difficulty—and ' the only way to remedy it was by another Act of Parlia- ment. Ho was told something of the sort was con- templated, and he was sure he did not object so long ' as it resulted in fair play to everybody. He was sure a they would do him the justice to think that in giving those decisions he had done so after carefully consider- ing what he believed to be the law on the subject. He might say with regard to the political agents, that every day he became convinced that they did a great deal more work than he once thought they did—(langhter)-- and their presence at the Court was of the greatest hell) to him and to the assistant overseer. If he were to talk from now till Daomsday he could not say too much in favour of the wonderful way in which Mr. Woodcoet prepared his list.</p>
        <p>C</p>
