        <p>The pretty Vicarage grounds at Padgate were on Thursday the scene of the annual garden fete in con- nection with Christ Church. The promoters were not fortunate in their choice of date, for the weather conditions were far from ideal for such a function. Daring most of the morning rain fell heavily but it cleared about noon and the fete was opened in pleasant sunshine. Later, however, there were more drenching showers' but notwithstanding the climatic discourage- meats, the fete was about the most successful ever , held. The stalls were attractively arranged in several •sj marquees and were presided over by the following ladies :- Fancy stall : Mesdames Buckton, Royston, Black- well, Vaughan, Elcoat, Walsh, Fair, Fryer, and Burrell,, Misses K. and L. Birtles. A Market stall : Mesdames Gorton, James Hotightoia and Dennett, and Miss Bowen. Refreshment stall : Miss Jackson assisted by' Mesdames Denham, Osborne, Pendlebury, W. Kerfoot. Norman Nield, Misses Short, Birchall, Birtles, Royston and Houghton. Sweet stall : Miss Dorothy Denham and Miss M. Kerfoot. Rummage stall : Mesdames Young, Knowles and Lowe, Misses Woods, Brimelow and Heath. Fish pond : Miss Elsie Horridge. Toy stall : Misses Horridgo, E. Malpas and Whitlow. 1 OPENING CEREMONY. The fete was opened in.'the presence of a large gather- ing by Mrs. Dewhurst, of Wyborne Gate, Birkdale. The Vicar (Rev. G. W. Dickinson) presided and the attendance included the Rev. W. Pollock-Hill (late 'Vicar of the parish and now of Ashton-in-Makerfleld), ' and the Rev. A. F. White (Vicar of Hollinfare). The Vicar read a letter of apology for non-attendance from the Rev. G. Vale Owen who had an appointment with the Bishop of Liverpool with reference to the consecration of the new church at Orford. Referring to the objects of the fete, he said there were various schemes in connection with the parish which required support. First and foremost perhaps was the Church House, which sadly needed funds. Then the Sunday school urgently needed help and there was also that old bugbear which some people did not like but which they would have to face and settle-the endowment question. The present arrangement could not be considered satis- factory and he would like to see the move that was started by the late Vicar carried to completion before very long, and then they at Padgato could look forward to even bigger things than they had done in the past.At present they were handicapped by those miserable '.l quarterly collections-miserable in every sense of the word in that he was sure the people were tired of them. the Vicar did not like them, and they never realised expectations. (Laughter.) He had other schemes in his head. One was the improvement of the aisle and the other the extension of the churchyard ; so that ' every penny they could lay their handa on would bei needed. Then the mother church was not at present doing anything for the daughter church at Orford. The," matter was before the Church Council and would be brought before the people later. The Vicar then announced that Lady Greenall had promised a donation of £lO, and in welcoming the presence of visitors from Southport exhorted the stallholders to "fleece them most merrily." It was good that in sunny Southport members of a wealthy congregation like that of Holy Trinity should take an interest in the outlying parishes of the diocese. (Applause.) Mrs. Dewhurst said the duty entrusted to her was quite a new one but she undertook it with the more readiness as it enabled her to mark the interest she felt in their new Vicar. She sincerely trusted that the cause which brought them there that day might receive generous support and that Mr. Dickinson's efforts for the benefit of that, his now sphere of labour, might be largely blessed. (Applause). Miss Dorothy Bennett then presented Mrs. Dewhurst with a handsome bouquet. A hearty vote of thanks was accorded to Mrs. Dewhurst on the proposition of Mr. Gorton (vicar's warden), seconded by Mr. Dennett (people's warden). SOME OF THE ATTRACTIONS. ef' There was no lack of attractions for the entertain. 1 ment of the visitors, children and adults being admirably catered for. A small horticultural show aroused lima-, interest, the prize winners were being :-Batton hole z. 1, May Whitlow; 2, John Plumb. Wild flowers (boys) 1, Harry Whitlow ' • 2, James Povey. Wild flower i (girls) : 1, Dorothy DI Court ; 2, Charlotte Gorton. ) Garden flowers (boys) : 1, William L. Powell ; 2f e Edmund Whitlow. Garden flowers (girls) : 1, Charlotte A Gorton • 2 Ethel Kerfoot. Cauliflower: 1, J Daintith ; 1 2, 1 Shultieworth. Celery: 1, A Clarke; 2, Mrs Sadlovr• A Onions : 1, J Daintith. Apples : 1, J Shuttleworthi 2, R Tunstall. Potatoes : 1, Henry Holcroft. A The promoters of the fête are nothing if not enter' .. prising and this year they had arranged a new featuSe in the shape of a baby competition. There were fone - classes-lst, up to six months old; 2nd, between si% and twelve months ; 3rd, from twelve to eighteen months ; 4th, from eighteen months to two years. Mrs. Buckton had given the prizes. The qualifications taken into account were (a), general condition of health (b), firmness of flesh (c), suitability and neatness ei clothing (regardless of quality of material). The judg: lug took place in the presence of a largo number el. interested spectators. The following were the prize winners :-E. B. White, J. D. Blatt, Mary Littlentorer Lily Carpenter, Gladys Lythgoe. There were several other interesting competition, the - winners being :-Ingenuity competition, ladies, 1, Mrs.. Promfrey ; 2, Mrs. Hankinson ; 3. Mrs. Denham 1 /-, gentlemen, I,Mr.Walter Critchley ;2,Mr.Walter Bibby children, 1, Miss Gorton ; 2, Miss A. Houghton ; extra prize, Mrs. Denham. Gents' hat trimming competition, 1, Mr. E. Gorton ; 2, Mr. F. Blackwell. Gents button ...:' hole, 1, Mr. Geoffrey Houghton; 2, Mr. J. J. Antrobus.. Ladies' washing competition, 1, Miss Critchley ; 2, AliV• Rowles. Gents washing competition, 1, Mr. 13. Dutton, 2,2, Mr. G. Ratcliffe. Ladies' shooting CO petition. 1, Miss Pendlebury ; 2, Miss J. Haddocs , Gents' shooting competition, 1, Mr. H. S. Padge ; 2P - Mr. F. Monks. . The proceeds of the fête amounted to .£l5O. ,;" . ,....,. - Death.- _ NOrtm.l.N.-Sept. 9th, at 63, Sankey-street, Warringtog,.. . Francis Edwin (Toddle), son of Mr. and Mrs. F • D._ IP, Norman, aged 8 years. Interment Warrington Cemetery, Saturday, September 12th, at 2,30. ig- g,,Harir m -Septeber 7th, at 4, Alexandra-street, vcarr' ton, Sarah, the beloved wife of Jacob Holt. Inte at Warrington Cemetery 10th inst. ~.......01 ------ ' Warrington.-Printed and published by .70FIN WivALoErg &amp; Co., at The Examines. General 'Printing Bewsey-street, and published by them at. the.a.orfa: place, and also at The Examiner Offices, Viotor_,.. L,aet , WIDNES -32, George-street, ST. HELENS: 3, D1,..1",!`„t. - street, EARLESTOWN; Church-street Ruscon.v, Pur . 12th, 1908, ....,</p>
